const html: string = "<h2>Podium Abstracts <span class=\"sp-count\">106</span></h2><p class=\"sp-note\">Submissions are grouped by presentation type. Each entry includes its abstract title, author names, institution, and abstract body.</p><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-1\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 1</p><h3>Association of lag between initiation of Detrusor contraction to initiation of flow of urine (PdetQlag) with bladder neck obstruction: a retrospective urodynamic analysis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Shankar P, Agarwal MM, Bhaskar V, Gupta S, Bandhu V, Agnihotri R, Rathore D</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Apollo Medics Super Speciality Hospitals</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo evaluate the association of PdetQlag, the interval between detrusor pressure development and initiation of urinary flow, with bladder neck obstruction and to assess its ability to discriminate bladder neck obstruction from other diagnostic groups.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA retrospective analysis of urodynamic studies on adult men and women was performed. PdetQlag was evaluated across six diagnostic groups: external urethral sphincter dysfunction (EUSD), benign enlargement of prostate (BPE), bladder neck obstruction (BNO), no obstruction (NOOBS; normal urodynamic study, stress incontinence, urge incontinence), detrusor underactivity (DUA), and urethral stricture disease (USD). In view of non-normal distribution of PdetQlag differences across groups were assessed using the Kruskal–Wallis test, followed by pairwise comparisons with Holm correction. BNO was compared with all other groups combined using the Mann–Whitney U test. Receiver operating characteristic analysis was performed to assess discriminatory performance.</p><p>Results<br/>\nOf 218 observations (178 males, 40 females; median age 53, IQR 42 – 63), 42 (19.3%) were classified as bladder neck obstruction. Median (IQR) PdetQlag was highest in BNO [16 (12.25–24.75) seconds], compared with BPE [9 (6.40–14.00) seconds], EUSD [8 (6.00–11.50) seconds], DUA [8 (7.00–9.50) seconds], USD [7 (5.00–8.00) seconds], and NOOBS [4 (2.50–8.00) seconds]. PdetQlag differed significantly across the six diagnostic groups (Kruskal–Wallis H=59.208, p&lt;0.001). PdetQlag was significantly higher in bladder neck obstruction than in all other diagnoses combined (Mann–Whitney U=5962, p&lt;0.001). ROC analysis demonstrated an area under the curve of 0.807. At a threshold of &gt;10 seconds, sensitivity was 81.0% and specificity was 72.7%.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nPdetQlag was significantly prolonged in BNO and demonstrated good discriminatory ability in this dataset. PdetQlag may serve as a useful additional urodynamic parameter in the assessment of bladder neck obstruction. Larger prospective studies using an independently established diagnostic reference standard are required for validation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-2\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 2</p><h3>Our Experience of Robotic Donor Nephrectomy Using the CMR Versius Surgical System</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr B S Aulakh , Dr Amit Tuli, Dr Shikha Tiwari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Aykai Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Our Experience of Robotic Donor Nephrectomy Using the CMR Versius Surgical System</p><p>Introduction:<br/>\nLaparoscopic donor nephrectomy is an established approach for living kidney donation, offering reduced postoperative morbidity and faster recovery compared with open surgery. Robotic donor nephrectomy may provide additional advantages through enhanced three-dimensional visualization, wristed instrumentation, improved ergonomics and greater precision during hilar dissection. The CMR Versius®️ robotic surgical system represents a relatively new robotic platform, and experience with its application in living donor nephrectomy remains limited. We present our initial experience with robotic donor nephrectomy using the CMR Versius system.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was performed of consecutive living kidney donors undergoing robotic donor nephrectomy using the CMR Versius robotic surgical system at our institution. Patient demographics, side of nephrectomy, renal vascular anatomy, operative time, docking and console time, warm ischemia time, estimated blood loss, intraoperative complications, conversion to open or conventional laparoscopy, postoperative complications, length of hospital stay and early graft function were evaluated.</p><p>Results:<br/>\n25 consecutive donor nephrectomies were performed using the CMR Versius system during the study period. The mean donor age was 50 years, with 48% being female. Left-sided donor nephrectomy was performed in 100% of cases. The mean operative time was 100 minutes and mean warm ischemia time was 1 minute 30 seconds .Estimated blood loss was minimal .No intraoperative mortality or major vascular injury was encountered. Postoperative recovery was uneventful in the majority of donors, with a median hospital stay of 2 days. No Clavien-Dindo grade ≥III complications were observed. All retrieved grafts demonstrated satisfactory early function, with 100% achieving immediate graft function.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nOur initial experience suggests that robotic donor nephrectomy using the CMR Versius robotic surgical system is technically feasible and can be performed safely with acceptable perioperative outcomes. The modular robotic platform offers enhanced visualization, articulated instrumentation and improved surgeon ergonomics, which may be particularly useful during precise hilar dissection and vascular control. Larger prospective series with longer follow-up and comparison with conventional laparoscopic donor nephrectomy are required to determine the potential advantages and cost-effectiveness of this approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-3\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 3</p><h3>Transitional Urology: From Congenital Anatomy to Functional Reconstruction in case based approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S.Pandey</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Kokilaben Dhirubhai Ambani Hospital , Mumbai</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>The seven phenotypes demonstrated markedly different anatomical challenges but a common requirement for longitudinal, multidisciplinary management. Patients with MRKH syndrome and vaginal agenesis required individualized strategies directed toward functional vaginal reconstruction and sexual health while considering associated urinary tract anomalies. Exstrophy–epispadias required sequential management addressing bladder development, continence and genital reconstruction. Aphallia and micropenis required individualized assessment of urinary, genital, sexual and psychosocial goals rather than an anatomy-only approach. Patients with severe failed hypospadias demonstrated the importance of abandoning repeated minor repairs and adopting staged salvage reconstruction using healthy tissue. Congenital penile curvature highlighted the importance of objective phenotyping and correction based on curvature, penile length, tissue characteristics and functional expectations. Across the spectrum, successful management depended on detailed counselling ,appropriate timing, preservation of future reconstructive options and continued follow-up during the transition from paediatric to adult state.<br/>\nD Conclusions<br/>\nComplex congenital urological conditions should be viewed as lifelong transitional disorders rather than isolated surgical problems. A phenotype-based approach allows reconstruction to be tailored to anatomy, function, growth, sexuality and patient expectations. The ultimate endpoint is not simply anatomical reconstruction, but preservation of renal and urinary function, restoration of genital and sexual function, psychological wellbeing and a successful transition into independent adult life. Transitional urology therefore requires a shift from “repairing an anomaly” to “reconstructing possibilities across a lifetime.”</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-4\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 4</p><h3>NERVE SPARING REDUCTION CLITOROPLASTY AS AESTHETIC &amp; FUNCTIONAL PROCEDURE</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">S.Pandey</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Kokilaben Dhirubhai Ambani Hospital , Mumbai</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>NERVE SPARING REDUCTION CLITOROPLASTY AS AESTHETIC &amp; FUNCTIONAL PROCEDURE<br/>\nIntroduction and objective:-<br/>\nClitoroplasty, especially in an adult, is a rare procedure. The goals of clitoroplasty are to achieve a normal genital appearance and to preserve sensation with a satisfactory sexual response.<br/>\nManagement of the enlarged clitoris, because of its importance for sexual function, has been and remains one of the most controversial topics in pediatric urology. Early controversy surrounding clitoroplasty resulted from many factors including an incomplete understanding of clitoral anatomy and incorrect assumptions of the role of the clitoris in sexual function. With a better understanding of anatomy and function, procedures have evolved to preserve clitoral tissue, especially with respect to the neurovascular bundles and reduction by corporectomy These changes have been made in an effort to preserve clitoral sensation and preserve orgasmic potential.<br/>\nMethods:-<br/>\nClitoromegaly of varied causes are an agony in prime of life . Women in age group of 16 to 27 presented with Clitoromegaly of 5-8 cm resulting from Idiopathic, Congenital adrenal hyperplasia &amp; Adenoma as pathology All women underwent a clitoral reduction procedure with preservation of the neurovascular bundle to the glans clitoris.<br/>\nThe technical details for the same have been demonstrated in the video as point of technique in clitoral disassembly followed by ablation and reconstruction<br/>\nResults:-<br/>\nAll 7 patients have been followed up for a period of over a year and had satisfactory cosmetic and sexual function.<br/>\nCONCLUSIONS :<br/>\nClitoroplasty is an uncommon procedure that is useful in correcting acquired clitoromegaly. The results of nerve sparing reduction clitoroplasty is cosmetically acceptable, and sexual function can be preserved thus addressing an unmet need in female sexual dysfunction as a reconstructive domain of a urologist</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-5\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 5</p><h3>Primary Sigmoid Vaginoplasty in Gender Affirmation surgery:Technique and outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr HK Nagaraj, Dr Arjun N</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sapthagiri medical college and research institute</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objective</p><p>Sigmoid vaginoplasty is an established option for vaginal reconstruction in transgender women, offering adequate neovaginal depth, mucosal lining, reduced contraction, and self-lubrication. The objective was to evaluate the surgical technique and outcomes of primary sigmoid vaginoplasty as a gender-affirming procedure at a tertiary care center.</p><p>Methods</p><p>This retrospective series included 30 consecutive transgender women who underwent primary sigmoid vaginoplasty between 2023 and 2025. The sigmoid colon was mobilized, a 15–17 cm segment was isolated, and bowel continuity was restored using stapled anastomosis. Bilateral orchidectomy, penile disassembly, neoclitoral creation from glans tissue, perineal dissection, and antegrade transfer of the sigmoid conduit into the neovaginal space were performed. Genital reconstruction included creation of the introitus, labia majora, urethral positioning, and clitoroplasty. WMSM2026-Template_Poster 22.pptx</p><p>Results</p><p>The mean age was 28 ± 5.4 years and mean BMI was 26.8 ± 5.6 kg/m². Overall, 90% of patients had no intraoperative or postoperative complications. Complications included one anastomotic leak requiring reoperation, one intraoperative anal canal injury requiring ileostomy, and one prolonged postoperative ileus. One patient developed neo-introitus stenosis due to defaulting dilatation, which improved with serial dilation. Follow-up ranged from 6 to 18 months. Mean neovaginal depth was 5.5 ± 0.8 inches. All patients reported ability to participate in vaginal intercourse and satisfaction with neovaginal depth and sensation.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>Primary sigmoid vaginoplasty is a dependable gender-affirming surgical option providing adequate depth, tactile satisfaction, and functional sexual outcomes. Optimal results require multidisciplinary collaboration involving urology, plastic surgery, and transgender healthcare teams.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-6\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 6</p><h3>TREATMENT DURABILITY, TOXICITY AND PATIENT-CENTRED EFFECTIVENESS OF GEMCITABINE–DOCETAXEL VERSUS BCG IN BCG-NAÏVE NMIBC</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR SHUBHAM JAIN, DR AVIRAL SHRIVASTAVA , PROF SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASHVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUMAR , DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS BHU VARANASI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Bacillus Calmette–Guérin (BCG) remains the standard intravesical therapy for intermediate- and high-risk non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC), but toxicity and treatment intolerance may compromise long-term delivery. Sequential intravesical gemcitabine–docetaxel (Gem/Doce) has emerged as a promising first-line alternative. We compared treatment durability, toxicity and patient-centred effectiveness of Gem/Doce versus BCG in BCG-naïve NMIBC.</p><p>Methods: This prospective comparative study was conducted from June 2023 to December 2025 and enrolled 128 BCG-naïve patients with intermediate- or high-risk NMIBC following complete TURBT. Of these, 108 patients completed the predefined 12-month follow-up and were included in the primary analysis (Gem/Doce, n=54; BCG, n=54); 20 patients remained under follow-up. Both groups received six-week induction followed by risk-adapted maintenance. Treatment durability was assessed using 12-month recurrence-free survival (RFS) and progression-free survival (PFS). Adverse events were graded using CTCAE v5.0. Patient-centred effectiveness was assessed using EORTC QLQ-C30, EORTC QLQ-NMIBC24 and quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs).</p><p>Results: At 12 months, RFS was 94.4% with Gem/Doce versus 75.9% with BCG, while PFS was 100% versus 92.6%, respectively. Treatment-related adverse events occurred in 13.0% of Gem/Doce patients compared with 33.3% receiving BCG, with Grade ≥3 events observed only in the BCG arm. Gem/Doce demonstrated better global health status and physical functioning, with lower urinary symptom burden, malaise and intravesical treatment-related problems. These patient-reported benefits persisted during maintenance. Mean 12-month QALYs favoured Gem/Doce (0.8807 vs 0.7198), representing a gain of 0.1609 QALYs. Gem/Doce also provided greater recurrence-free time, particularly among high-risk patients.</p><p>Conclusions: Among patients completing 12-month follow-up, intravesical Gem/Doce demonstrated superior treatment durability, lower toxicity and better patient-centred effectiveness than BCG. The favourable combination of durable disease control, tolerability and preservation of quality of life may be particularly relevant when sustained treatment adherence and patient experience are prioritised.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-7\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 7</p><h3>Large Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of Kidney: A Rare Tumor with Aggressive Histopathology</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR RAKESH RAI, DR MADAN GOPAL, PROF SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUMAR, DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS , BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS: To report a rare case of primary large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) of the kidney, highlighting its clinical presentation, radiological characteristics, histopathological diagnosis, management and short-term outcome, with a review of the limited literature on this uncommon renal malignancy.<br/>\nMETHODS: A 49-year-old male presented with dyspepsia and no specific urological symptoms. Ultrasonography incidentally detected a renal mass, further characterized by contrast-enhanced CT. The lesion measured 9.1 × 7.8 × 9.6 cm with a high RENAL nephrometry score of 12x. Metastatic evaluation was negative. Laparoscopic right radical nephrectomy was performed, followed by histopathological and immunohistochemical evaluation and clinical follow-up.<br/>\nRESULTS: The large renal mass involved the lower and middle poles and abutted two renal veins and one renal artery, without vascular thrombosis, nodal involvement or perinephric fat infiltration. Histology showed dyscohesive sheets of large polygonal cells with round nuclei, coarse chromatin, prominent nucleoli and &gt;10 mitoses per 10 high-power fields, with approximately 20% necrosis. Tumor cells were positive for synaptophysin and negative for WT1, CD10, CK7, GATA3, NKX2.2 and PAX8, supporting primary renal LCNEC. The final diagnosis was WHO grade 4 LCNEC, pathological stage pT3a, with negative surgical margins. The patient received four cycles of etoposide plus cisplatin-based chemotherapy and remained asymptomatic at one-year follow-up.<br/>\nCONCLUSION: Primary renal LCNEC is an exceptionally rare, high-grade and aggressive malignancy with limited evidence to guide treatment. Accurate histopathological and immunohistochemical diagnosis is essential to distinguish it from other renal malignancies and neuroendocrine neoplasms. Radical nephrectomy followed by platinum-based chemotherapy may be considered in high-risk disease, although treatment strategies are largely extrapolated from pulmonary neuroendocrine carcinoma. Larger multicentre studies and dedicated registries are needed to establish prognostic factors and optimal management.<br/>\nKeywords: large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma; kidney; renal tumor; radical nephrectomy; platinum-based chemotherapy</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-8\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 8</p><h3>Urinary microRNA Signatures as Non-invasive Diagnostic and Prognostic Biomarkers in Urinary Bladder Cancer</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr ABHINABA MOWAR, DR ANIL BALIYAN, PROF SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUMAR, DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS-BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Urinary bladder cancer (UBC) requires repeated invasive surveillance, while conventional urine cytology has limited sensitivity, particularly for low-grade disease. Urinary microRNAs (miRNAs), owing to their stability in body fluids, may provide non-invasive biomarkers for diagnosis and prognostication. We evaluated urinary miRNA expression in patients with UBC and healthy controls and explored their diagnostic and pathological associations.</p><p>Methods: This single-centre prospective observational study included 60 participants (30 histopathologically confirmed, treatment-naïve UBC patients and 30 matched healthy controls) recruited between March 2023 and April 2025. Preoperative urine samples were collected for RNA extraction. miRNA libraries were constructed and subjected to high-throughput sequencing, followed by differential expression analysis and validation using real-time quantitative RT-PCR. Diagnostic performance was assessed using receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analysis, with pathological grade and stage evaluated using relevant statistical tests.</p><p>Results: Sequencing identified 865 miRNAs, including 708 detected in UBC samples and 540 in controls, with 383 shared between groups. Urinary miR-6724-5p, miR-7704 and miR-1273h-5p demonstrated significant differential expression and diagnostic potential, with AUCs of 0.788, 0.700 and 0.729, respectively. Their sensitivities were 90.63%, 84.38% and 83.87%, respectively. miR-548ae, miR-7704 and miR-1273h-5p showed associations with histological grade, with AUCs of 0.680, 0.613 and 0.676, respectively. For T1 versus T2 disease, miR-7704 and miR-1273h-5p demonstrated AUCs of 0.611 and 0.633, respectively.</p><p>Conclusion: A urinary miRNA panel comprising miR-6724-5p, miR-1273h-5p, miR-7704 and miR-548ae-5p shows promise as a non-invasive biomarker strategy for UBC detection and pathological characterization. Larger multicentre studies with longer follow-up are required for external validation and assessment of recurrence and survival prediction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-9\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 9</p><h3>Effect of wireles devices on male infertility</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.rakesh Rai</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Abstract</p><p>Background:<br/>\nThe widespread use of wireless electronic devices, particularly mobile phones, has raised concerns regarding their potential effects on male reproductive health. Radiofrequency electromagnetic radiation and prolonged mobile phone exposure have been hypothesised to influence spermatogenesis through oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, altered testicular microenvironment, and sperm DNA damage. However, evidence regarding the clinical relevance of these effects remains inconsistent. The present study evaluated the association between mobile phone usage and male reproductive parameters, with particular emphasis on semen quality, oxidative stress, and sperm DNA fragmentation.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nMale participants undergoing evaluation for reproductive health were assessed for mobile phone usage using a structured exposure/usage scoring system. Semen parameters were evaluated according to standard laboratory procedures, including sperm concentration, total and progressive motility, and morphology. Markers of oxidative stress and sperm DNA</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe study demonstrated a trend towards poorer semen quality among individuals with higher mobile phone usage scores. However, the observed differences in semen parameters did not reach statistical significance. Similarly, no statistically significant association was identified between mobile phone usage and markers of oxidative stress or sperm DNA fragmentation. The absence of significant associations suggests that the degree of mobile phone exposure observed in this cohort may not be sufficient to produce measurable adverse effects on male reproductive function. The findings also indicat</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nIn this cohort, mobile phone usage was not significantly associated with deterioration in semen parameters, increased oxidative stress, or sperm DNA fragmentation. Although a trend towards poorer semen quality was observed with increasing exposure, the findings do not demonstrate a measurable adverse effect of mobile phone use on male reproductive health at the exposure levels encountered. Larger prospective studies with objective exposure assessment, longer follow-up, and ev</p><p>Keywords: Male infertility; Mobile phone; Wireless devices; Semen parameters; Oxidative stress; Sperm DNA fragmentation; Radiofrequency electromagnetic radiation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-10\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 10</p><h3>Trifecta Outcomes in Transplant: Group-wise and Contemporary Comparisons of Robot-Assisted Versus Open Kidney Transplantation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Guleria Karandeep, Khan Umar, Mitra Shaunak, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) seeks to reduce access-related morbidity while preserving the vascular precision and graft outcomes of open kidney transplantation (OKT). Pooled analyses may disadvantage RAKT by incorporating early learning-phase cases. We compared Trifecta outcomes across 435 sequential RAKTs and 1,800 OKTs, evaluated chronological robotic groups against the open benchmark, and assessed the last 100 RAKTs as a contemporary-mastery cohort.</p><p>Materials and Methods: This retrospective analysis included 435 living-donor RAKTs divided chronologically into Group A (cases 1–100), Group B (101–200), Group C (201–300), and Group D (301–435), with an additional last-100 cohort (336–435), versus 1,800 OKTs. Trifecta required no dialysis within seven days, no Clavien–Dindo grade ≥III complication, and rewarming time &lt;48 minutes. Pearson chi-square tests, odds ratios (ORs), risk ratios, absolute risk differences, and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated.</p><p>Results: Trifecta was achieved in 184/435 RAKTs (42.3%) versus 1,413/1,800 OKTs (78.5%; OR 0.20, 95% CI 0.16–0.25; p&lt;0.001). RAKT Trifecta increased progressively from 27.0% in Group A to 31.0% in Group B, 44.0% in Group C, and 60.7% in Group D; corresponding ORs versus OKT were 0.10, 0.12, 0.22, and 0.42, respectively (all p&lt;0.001). In the last 100 RAKTs, Trifecta reached 62.0%, reducing the absolute deficit versus OKT from 51.5 to 16.5 percentage points (OR 0.45, 95% CI 0.29–0.68; p&lt;0.001). No-DGF (97.0% vs 95.0%; p=0.367) and no-major-complication rates (97.0% vs 96.0%; p=0.617) were comparable. The remaining difference was driven by rewarming time &lt;48 minutes (66.0% vs 83.0%; p&lt;0.001).</p><p>Conclusions: RAKT showed progressive convergence toward the OKT Trifecta benchmark across sequential experience. Contemporary robotic outcomes retained comparable proposed safety and early graft function, while longer implantation-related rewarming remained the principal limitation. Reporting the complete program, chronological phases, and contemporary rolling cohort provides a more transparent assessment than any single pooled comparison.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-11\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 11</p><h3>Diagnostic Performance of Diffusion-Weighted MRI and Apparent Diffusion Coefficient Values in differentiating Benign from Malignant Renal Masses: A Prospective Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ashutosh Tiwari, Dr. Narayan Shubham, Dr. Madan Gopal Bhardwaj, Prof. Sameer Trivedi, Dr. Yashasvi Singh, Dr. Ujwal Kumar, Dr. Lalit Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS, BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS<br/>\nTo evaluate the diagnostic performance of diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging (DW-MRI) and apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) values in differentiating benign from malignant renal masses. A secondary objective was to explore whether ADC values could distinguish among renal cell carcinoma (RCC) histological subtypes.<br/>\nMETHODS<br/>\nThis single-centre prospective observational study was conducted at the Department of Urology, Institute of Medical Sciences, Banaras Hindu University, between January 2024 and June 2025. Patients aged 18–70 years with newly diagnosed renal masses and subsequent surgical histopathological confirmation were included. All patients underwent 3-T MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging using b-values of 50, 400 and 800 s/mm². ADC measurements were obtained from three solid tumour regions while avoiding necrosis, cystic areas and haemorrhage. ADC values were correlated with histopathological diagnosis. Statistical analysis included independent t-test, ANOVA and receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nOf 41 enrolled patients, 38 were analysed; 31 (81.6%) had malignant and 7 (18.4%) had benign renal masses. Benign lesions comprised 5 angiomyolipomas and 2 oncocytomas, while malignant lesions included 21 clear cell, 7 papillary and 3 chromophobe RCCs. Mean ADC was significantly higher in benign than malignant lesions (1314.9 ± 144.5 vs. 1037.7 ± 129 ×10⁻³ mm²/s; p&lt;0.001). Papillary RCC demonstrated the lowest mean ADC (865.4 ± 84 ×10⁻³ mm²/s). ROC analysis showed excellent discrimination between benign and malignant masses (AUC 0.945; 95% CI 0.854–1.000). An ADC cutoff of ≥1118 ×10⁻³ mm²/s identified benign lesions with 100% sensitivity and 67.7% specificity.<br/>\nCONCLUSIONS<br/>\nADC values demonstrated promising ability to differentiate benign from malignant renal masses, with significantly lower values in malignant lesions. However, RCC subtype findings remain exploratory because of small subgroup sizes. The proposed ADC cutoff should be considered preliminary owing to the small, imbalanced, surgically selected cohort and requires validation in larger multicentre prospective studies before routine clinical application.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-12\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 12</p><h3>TRIORCHIDISM: A RARE CASE REPORT</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR ABHINABA MOWAR, DR PANKAJ JAISWAL, PROF SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUMAR, DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS-BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nPolyorchidism (triorchidism) is an exceedingly rare congenital anomaly, with fewer than 200 cases reported worldwide. It is most often an incidental finding, discovered during evaluation for scrotal swelling, infertility, or, as in this case, routine medical fitness assessment.<br/>\nCase Report<br/>\nAn 18-year-old male presented to the urology OPD for pre-recruitment (Army) medical evaluation, with a history of three palpable testicles since childhood and no other complaints. On examination, the penis and external urethral meatus were normal. The right testis was normal on palpation (~20cc). On the left side, two separate firm testicular swellings (~10cc each) were palpable independently, though only a single spermatic cord could be identified — raising a differential of epididymal cyst. Testicular ultrasonography confirmed a normal right testis (35×24×30 mm) and a relatively smaller left testis (26×15×21 mm), with an additional well-defined oval structure (17×10×13 mm) in the left hemi-scrotum lateral to the left testis, showing echotexture and internal vascularity identical to testicular parenchyma — confirming a supernumerary testis. No focal lesion, calcification, or hydrocoele was noted. MRI pelvis was advised for further ductal and anatomical characterization.<br/>\nDiscussion<br/>\nFirst described in 1670, polyorchidism is classified per Leung's system based on the presence or absence of an independent excretory duct for the supernumerary testis. Left-sided predominance, as seen here, is well documented in literature. Diagnosis is largely incidental and ultrasound-based, with MRI aiding in delineating ductal anatomy and excluding associated anomalies such as cryptorchidism, hernia, or torsion. Management in asymptomatic, normally vascularized supernumerary testes is conservative, with long-term surveillance given a theoretically increased, though debated, malignancy risk.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nThis case underscores the importance of considering polyorchidism when evaluating scrotal swellings or incidental findings, particularly in occupational fitness settings, and highlights ultrasonography as the first-line diagnostic tool, complemented by MRI, in guiding safe conservative management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-13\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 13</p><h3>From Novice to Mastery in a High-Volume Robot-Assisted Kidney Transplant Program: Learning-Curve Dynamics, Surgical Nuances, and Lessons Learned from Over 400 Cases</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Kushwaha Swapnil Singh, Aggarwal Amit, Gaur Pankaj, Guleria Karandeep, Khan Umar, Mitra Shaunak, Kumar Anant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPER SPECIALITY HOSPITAL, VAISHALI, GHAZIABAD, INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) is technically demanding because vascular precision, graft hypothermia, rewarming time, ureteric reconstruction, and team coordination must be optimized simultaneously. We evaluated the maturation of a high-volume RAKT programme and identified technical refinements that transformed recurrent challenges into a standardized operative pathway.</p><p>Materials and Methods: Outcomes of 435 consecutive RAKTs were evaluated chronologically: Group A (cases 1–100), Group B (101–200), Group C (201–300), and Group D (301–435). Continuous and categorical variables were reported as mean ± standard deviation and number (percentage), respectively. Groups were compared using summary-data analysis of variance or chi-square tests. Learning was evaluated through phase trajectories, subgroup-mean Shewhart X-bar charts, and aggregate phase-boundary CUSUM plots for console, rewarming, venous anastomosis, and arterial anastomosis times. Technical modifications were reconstructed from structured operative learning records.</p><p>Results: Console time decreased from 158.42 ± 40.18 to 126.84 ± 29.47 minutes, rewarming time from 60.02 ± 21.94 to 43.92 ± 10.86 minutes, venous anastomosis time from 20.24 ± 3.18 to 15.76 ± 2.32 minutes, and arterial anastomosis time from 13.91 ± 3.12 to 11.08 ± 2.35 minutes (all p&lt;0.001). Total operative time remained stable despite increases in multiple-vessel grafts from 17% to 32.6% and vascular reconstruction from 17% to 34.1%. Hospital stay, pain, blood loss, transfusion, wound morbidity, and re-exploration improved. Day-7, day-30, and day-90 graft function remained preserved, with progressive improvement in MDRD eGFR. Shewhart and CUSUM analyses demonstrated sustained reductions in transplant-specific technical times, particularly after Group B.</p><p>Conclusions: RAKT mastery extends beyond operative speed. It requires standardized bench preparation, atraumatic graft handling, reliable orientation, efficient vascular reconstruction, controlled regional hypothermia, coordinated bedside assistance, and reproducible rescue strategies. Continued improvement beyond the conventional 20–35-case learning threshold suggests that individual proficiency precedes institutional mastery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-14\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 14</p><h3>Tissue-Derived Extracellular Vesicles as a Potential Biomarker in Prostate Cancer</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Sanjay Kumar, Amlesh Seth, Seema Kaushal, Prabhakar Singh, Subash Chandra Yadav, Javed Qadri</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nProstate cancer remains a major clinical challenge, highlighting the need for novel biomarkers for its detection and characterization. Extracellular vesicles (EVs) are membrane-bound particles released by cells that may reflect the biological characteristics of their cells of origin. This pilot study evaluated the presence of EVs in prostate biopsy tissue and explored their association with histopathologically confirmed prostate cancer.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nThis prospective observational pilot study included patients undergoing prostate biopsy for suspected prostate cancer. Biopsy tissue samples were processed and evaluated for EVs using electron microscopy. Histopathological examination was performed to confirm and grade prostate cancer.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nTwenty patients were included in the preliminary analysis. Tissue EVs were identified in 4 patients (20%), all of whom had histopathologically confirmed prostate adenocarcinoma. Serum PSA levels in these patients were 12, 55, 74 and 35 ng/mL, with corresponding Gleason Grade Groups of 4, 3, 4 and 3, respectively. Among the remaining 16 patients without demonstrable tissue EVs, 10 (62.5%) had biopsy-proven prostate cancer, while 6 (37.5%) had benign disease. Overall, EVs were detected in 4 of 14 patients (28.6%) with prostate cancer and in none of the 6 patients with benign histopathology.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nIn this preliminary cohort, extracellular vesicles were detected exclusively in prostate biopsy tissue from patients with histopathologically confirmed prostate cancer, although they were present in only a subset of malignant cases. These findings are hypothesis-generating and suggest that tissue-derived EVs may have potential as a biomarker in prostate cancer. Larger studies are required to establish their diagnostic and prognostic utility.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-15\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 15</p><h3>SURGICAL AND CLINICOPATHOLOGICAL CORRELATION OF LEVELS OF CIRCULATING MITOCHONDRIAL TUMOR DNA IN UROTHELIAL CARCINOMA</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR SHUBHAM JAIN , DR MADAN GOPAL BHARDWAJ , PROF. SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASHVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUMAR , DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS BHU , VARANASI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS</p><p>To evaluate plasma cell-free mitochondrial DNA (cf-mtDNA) markers MT-ND1, MT-ND4 and MT-ND5 in urothelial carcinoma and assess their association with clinicopathological features, treatment response, recurrence and survival.</p><p>METHODS</p><p>This prospective observational study was conducted from January 2025 to March 2026 at Banaras Hindu University. Patients with primary or previously diagnosed bladder carcinoma undergoing TURBT, radical cystectomy or other indicated procedures were included; patients with inadequate cfDNA yield, prior chemotherapy/radiotherapy, active urinary infection/inflammation, other malignancies or extensive prior bladder surgery were excluded. Plasma samples were collected at baseline, one month postoperatively, after chemotherapy and at recurrence. cfDNA was extracted by phenol-chloroform and 20 ng DNA underwent SYBR Green quantitative PCR for MT-ND1, MT-ND4 and MT-ND5. Clinicopathological variables and outcomes were correlated using appropriate non-parametric, parametric and survival analyses.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>Of 62 enrolled patients, 56 were analysed, alongside 17 healthy controls. Mean age was 58.89±12.27 years and the male ratio was 4.6:1. MT-ND1 and MT-ND5 levels differed significantly between patients and controls (p&lt;0.01). Following surgery, MT-ND5 decreased significantly (p=0.0046), whereas MT-ND4 remained stable. Marker levels were not associated with tumor volume. MT-ND4 showed significant variation across grades/histologies (p=0.0417), with reduced shedding in aggressive variant histology versus low-grade tumors (p=0.0339), and was significantly lower in muscle-invasive disease (p=0.0339). Chemotherapy analysis in three patients showed a non-significant downward trend in MT-ND1 and MT-ND5. During mean follow-up of 284 days, eight events occurred (five recurrences, three deaths); survival differences according to marker expression were not statistically significant.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>Plasma cf-mtDNA demonstrates potential as a minimally invasive biomarker panel in urothelial carcinoma. MT-ND1 and MT-ND5 may aid cancer detection, while reduced MT-ND4 shedding appears associated with aggressive histology and muscle invasion. Larger cohorts with longer follow-up are required to validate diagnostic and prognostic utility, establish clinically applicable thresholds and support future clinical risk stratification.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-16\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 16</p><h3>ASSESSMENT OF PENTAFACTA OUTCOME IN RADICAL CYSTECTOMY: TERTIARY CARE CENTRE EXPERIENCE IN NORTH INDIA</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ashutosh Tiwari, Dr. Madan Gopal Bhardwaj, Prof. Sameer Trivedi, Dr. Yashasvi Singh, Dr. Ujwal Kumar, Dr. Lalit Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS, BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS<br/>\nTo evaluate the impact of individual components of the pentafecta on oncological and survival outcomes after radical cystectomy with bilateral pelvic lymph node dissection (RC-PLND). We also assessed which pentafecta components most strongly influenced early oncological outcomes and survival.<br/>\nMETHODS<br/>\nWe conducted a single-centre retrospective study of 158 patients undergoing RC-PLND at a tertiary referral centre in Northern India between January 2016 and January 2025. Pentafecta comprised negative surgical margins, adequate lymph node dissection (≥16 nodes), absence of Clavien-Dindo grade ≥3 perioperative complications, absence of urinary diversion-related complications, and no recurrence within 12 months. OS and RFS were assessed using Kaplan-Meier and log-rank methods, while Cox proportional hazards regression identified predictors of survival.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nThe cohort comprised 124 men and 34 women, with a mean age of 57.22±12.44 years. Complete pentafecta was achieved in 78 (49.37%) patients. Negative margins, adequate lymph node yield, absence of major complications, absence of diversion-related complications, and absence of 1-year recurrence were achieved in 93.67%, 72.15%, 87.34%, 87.34%, and 89.87%, respectively. Patients achieving pentafecta had significantly lower 1-year recurrence (1.28% vs. 18.75%, p=0.00027) and superior 1-year RFS (98.72% vs. 81.25%, p&lt;0.001). However, mean OS did not differ significantly between pentafecta and non-pentafecta groups (62.14 vs. 63.08 months; p=0.864), with identical long-term mortality events. On multivariate analysis, increasing age independently predicted poorer OS (HR 1.06, 95% CI 1.02–1.11; p=0.005), while younger age independently predicted early recurrence (HR 0.93, 95% CI 0.89–0.96; p&lt;0.001).<br/>\nCONCLUSIONS<br/>\nComplete pentafecta achievement is a strong indicator of short-term oncological quality and significantly predicts 1-year recurrence-free survival after RC-PLND. Its inability to significantly improve long-term OS suggests that late mortality is influenced more by patient age, tumour biology and pathological stage than perioperative surgical quality. Pentafecta may serve as a practical quality-assurance benchmark for radical cystectomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-17\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 17</p><h3>SURGICAL AND CLINICOPATHOLOGICAL CORRELATION OF LEVELS OF CIRCULATING MITOCHONDRIAL TUMOR DNA IN UROTHELIAL CARCINOMA</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR SHUBHAM JAIN, DR MADAN GOPAL BHARADWAJ, PROF SAMEER TRIVEDI, DR YASHASHVI SINGH, DR UJWAL KUAMR , DR LALIT KUMAR</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS</p><p>To evaluate circulating cell-free mitochondrial DNA (cf-mtDNA) markers ND1, ND4, and ND5 in urothelial carcinoma and correlate their levels with tumor burden, histopathological characteristics, postoperative changes, response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy, recurrence, and survival.</p><p>METHODS</p><p>This prospective observational study was conducted from January 2025 to March 2026 at the Departments of Urology and Molecular and Human Genetics, Banaras Hindu University, following Institutional Ethics Committee approval. Patients with primary or previously diagnosed urinary bladder carcinoma undergoing TURBT or radical cystoprostatectomy with bilateral pelvic lymph node dissection were included. Patients with inadequate cfDNA yield, prior chemotherapy/radiotherapy, active urinary infection or inflammation, other active malignancies, or previous extensive bladder surgery were excluded.</p><p>Blood samples were collected at surgery/initiation of neoadjuvant chemotherapy, one month postoperatively, after chemotherapy completion, and at recurrence. Plasma was isolated, stored at −80°C, and cfDNA extracted using the phenol-chloroform method. DNA was quantified using NanoDrop, and 20 ng DNA underwent SYBR Green-based quantitative PCR for ND1, ND4, and ND5. Clinical, radiological, operative, pathological, and follow-up data were recorded. Statistical analysis included Mann-Whitney U, Welch's t-test, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis, Spearman correlation, Kaplan-Meier survival analysis, log-rank testing, and diagnostic performance measures using SPSS v26.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>Baseline and longitudinal ND1, ND4, and ND5 levels were compared according to tumor burden, pathological stage, grade, depth of invasion, histological subtype, urine cytology, treatment response, recurrence, disease-free survival, and overall survival. Pre- and postoperative changes were assessed within individual patients, while expression levels were evaluated in relation to neoadjuvant chemotherapy response.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>Circulating cf-mtDNA markers ND1, ND4, and ND5 may represent promising minimally invasive biomarkers in urothelial carcinoma. Their association with tumor burden, pathological aggressiveness, treatment response, recurrence, and survival may support development of diagnostic and prognostic strategies. Larger prospective studies are required to validate these findings and establish clinically applicable thresholds.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-18\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 18</p><h3>Integration of MRI and PSMA PET /CT for predicting positive surgical margin in Robot assisted radical prostatectomy :- A machine learning approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Rahul kumar, Harshit Rewari , Gagan Tawar , Puneet Ahluwalia, Gagan Gautam</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta The Medicity , Gurgaon</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction : Positive surgical margin is associated with BCR free survival , cancer specific survival , overall survival , cancer specific mortality, and overall mortality in prostate cancer patients. Therefore PSM could serve as poor prognostic factor in patients with prostate cancer. Our objective was to develop and internally validate a machine learning model that integrates clinical, MPMRI and PSMA PET/CT to predict positive surgical margin before RARP.<br/>\nMATERAIL AND METHODS : A retrospective analysis of a prospectively collected database of 757 patients was performed. All patients who underwent mpMRI and PSMA PET/CT were included in the cohort. Three predictive model : logistic regression , Random forest and XG Boost were trained and evaluated using 5 fold cross validation and tested on holdout set. Model performance was assessed with Area under the curve(AUC) , calibration plots and Decision curve analysis(DCA). SHAP (Shapely additive explanation) was used to identify the predictor variables.<br/>\nRESULT: The overall PSM was observed in 154(20.4%) patients, with unifocal PSM in 108 (14.9%) and multifocal PSM in 46(6.3%) patients. The median IQR length of PSM was 3(2-3.5 mm). The random forest model achieved the highest AUC(0.72; 0.67-0.77), outperforming XG Boost and logistic regression. At the optimal threshold. the RF achieved a negative predictive value of 89%. Decision curve analysis shoed that the model provided net clinical benefit at clinically relevant threshold of 12-26%. The calibration plot shoed excellent agreement. SHAP analysis identified preop PSA , % of positive biopsy cores, MRI clinical stage and PSA SUV max as the principal drivers of PSM.<br/>\nCONCLUSION: We developed a machine learning framework with an accessible model to predict PSM post RARP. This tool can assist surgeons in selecting cases for a nerve sparing approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-19\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 19</p><h3>Assessment of Metabolic Syndrome with Survival and Adverse Pathology in Urological Cancers: An Ambispective Study INTERIM ANALYSIS</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Rahul Tayal , DR. Rudra Prasad Ghorai , Dr. Sreerag K.S. , Dr, Sidhartha Kalra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JIPMER</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To assess metabolic syndrome (MetS) status among patients with urological cancers and its association with adverse pathology and survival.<br/>\nMethods: Ambispective observational study,tertiary-care urology department. Patients &gt;18 years with histologically confirmed urological malignancy (January 2012–June 2026) and minimum 6 months follow-up were included; prior or synchronous non-urological malignancy was excluded. MetS was defined by modified NCEP-ATP III criteria for Indians any three of: waist ≥90/≥80 cm (men/women) or BMI &gt;30; triglycerides ≥150 mg/dL; HDL &lt;40/&lt;50 mg/dL (men/women); blood pressure ≥130/85 mmHg or treated; fasting glucose ≥100 mg/dL or diabetes. Adverse pathology was high grade and/or ≥pT3, node-positive or metastatic disease. Target sample 360; this interim analysis reports the first 140 patients.<br/>\nResults: Mean age was 59.8 ± 12.0 years; 119 men (85.0%), 21 women (15.0%); mean BMI 24.6 ± 4.6 kg/m². Smoking was reported by 61.2%. Malignancies: bladder 66 (47.1%), renal cell carcinoma 47 (33.6%), prostate 15 (10.7%), penile 6 (4.3%), upper tract urothelial 5 (3.6%), testis 1 (0.7%). MetS was present in 74/140 (52.9%; 95% CI 44.6–61.1), with 49 (35.0%) having ≥4 components. Raised blood pressure was commonest (71.4%), then raised fasting glucose (53.6%), hypertriglyceridaemia (48.6%), low HDL (46.4%) and central obesity (45.7%). MetS was significantly commoner in renal cell carcinoma than other sites (70.2% vs 44.1%), and MetS-positive patients had significantly higher BMI (26.2 vs 22.8 kg/m²), though age was comparable (60.1 vs 59.4 years). Advanced disease (≥pT3/N+/M1) was more frequent with MetS (43.5% vs 30.2%) but not significantly; high-grade tumours were comparable (76.8% vs 75.0%).<br/>\nConclusion: MetS is highly prevalent (52.9%) in urological malignancy, exceeding the ~30% reported in mixed cancer cohorts, and is significantly associated with renal cell carcinoma. The trend towards more advanced stage requires confirmation in the full cohort; survival analysis awaits complete recruitment and follow-up.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-20\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 20</p><h3>One Incision, Two Pathologies: Simultaneous Open Partial Nephrectomy and Pyelolithotomy for a Synchronous Renal Mass and Ipsilateral Renal Calculi.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Manoj Kumar, Amlesh Seth</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: The coexistence of a renal mass suspicious for malignancy with ipsilateral renal stone disease is rare and presents a complex surgical challenge. These conditions are commonly managed with staged procedures, increasing anaesthetic exposure, healthcare costs, and potential morbidity. We describe the feasibility and outcomes of a single-stage open partial nephrectomy combined with pyelolithotomy as a nephron-sparing approach.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: A 24-year-old male presented with a four-month history of left flank pain and lower urinary tract symptoms. Imaging revealed a 5.4 × 4.0 cm predominantly exophytic lower pole left renal mass suspicious for malignancy, a 2 cm obstructing pelviureteric junction calculus with moderate hydronephrosis, and an additional 5 mm lower pole renal calculus. The patient had a history of endoscopic management for a prostatic urethral calculus. Through a left flank incision, a Double-J stent was placed, followed by pyelolithotomy with complete stone extraction. Off-clamp partial nephrectomy was then performed with excision of the renal mass, pelvicalyceal repair, and renorrhaphy.<br/>\nResults: The procedure was completed successfully without intraoperative complications. Complete clearance of both calculi and successful excision of the renal mass were achieved while preserving renal parenchyma. The postoperative course was uneventful, renal function remained preserved, and the patient was discharged in stable condition.<br/>\nConclusion: Simultaneous open partial nephrectomy and pyelolithotomy is a safe and effective single-stage nephron-sparing option in carefully selected patients with concurrent renal mass and ipsilateral stone disease. This approach achieves definitive management of both pathologies while avoiding the morbidity, cost, and inconvenience associated with staged procedures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-21\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 21</p><h3>Tissue-Derived Extracellular Vesicles as a Potential Biomarker in Prostate Cancer</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Sanjay Kumar, Amlesh Seth, Seema Kaushal, Prabhakar Singh, Subash Chandra Yadav, Javed Qadri</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nProstate cancer remains a major clinical challenge, highlighting the need for novel biomarkers for its detection and characterization. Extracellular vesicles (EVs) are membrane-bound particles released by cells that may reflect the biological characteristics of their cells of origin. This pilot study evaluated the presence of EVs in prostate biopsy tissue and explored their association with histopathologically confirmed prostate cancer.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nThis prospective observational pilot study included patients undergoing prostate biopsy for suspected prostate cancer. Biopsy tissue samples were processed and evaluated for EVs using electron microscopy. Histopathological examination was performed to confirm and grade prostate cancer.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nTwenty patients were included in the preliminary analysis. Tissue EVs were identified in 4 patients (20%), all of whom had histopathologically confirmed prostate adenocarcinoma. Serum PSA levels in these patients were 12, 55, 74 and 35 ng/mL, with corresponding Gleason Grade Groups of 4, 3, 4 and 3, respectively. Among the remaining 16 patients without demonstrable tissue EVs, 10 (62.5%) had biopsy-proven prostate cancer, while 6 (37.5%) had benign disease. Overall, EVs were detected in 4 of 14 patients (28.6%) with prostate cancer and in none of the 6 patients with benign histopathology.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nIn this preliminary cohort, extracellular vesicles were detected exclusively in prostate biopsy tissue from patients with histopathologically confirmed prostate cancer, although they were present in only a subset of malignant cases. These findings are hypothesis-generating and suggest that tissue-derived EVs may have potential as a biomarker in prostate cancer. Larger studies are required to establish their diagnostic and prognostic utility.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-22\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 22</p><h3>CT Volumetric Analysis for Renal Function Assessment and the Impact of Warm Ischemia Time Following Partial Nephrectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR FAIZ KHAN YUSUFI, DR NITIKA, DR PRAKHAR, DR SANCHIT, DR UDAY PRATAP SINGH</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGI LUCKNOW</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Partial nephrectomy (PN) is the preferred treatment for small renal masses, providing effective oncological control while preserving renal parenchyma. Conventional serum-based GFR equations may not accurately reflect individual renal unit function. This study evaluates the correlation between CT volumetric analysis-based GFR estimation and nuclear medicine renal scans and assesses the effect of warm ischemia time (WIT) on postoperative renal function.</p><p>Materials and Methods: Fifty-five patients undergoing PN at SGPGIMS, Lucknow, between July 2024 and June 2025 were prospectively analyzed. Patients undergoing open, laparoscopic, or robotic PN were included. Patients with solitary or bilateral tumors, off-clamp procedures, cold ischemia, re-clamping, or conversion to open surgery were excluded. Preoperative DTPA renal scans and contrast-enhanced CT/MRI were performed. CT volumetric software was used to estimate renal parenchymal volume and GFR preoperatively and at 3-month follow-up. Correlations between CT-derived parameters and DTPA GFR were analyzed, along with the association between WIT and postoperative GFR.</p><p>Results: The mean age was 54.1 ± 11.6 years, with most tumors being T1a; mean tumor size was 5.6 ± 1.2 cm. Preoperative CT parenchymal volume correlated strongly with DTPA GFR (r=0.71, p&lt;0.001), with similar postoperative correlation (r=0.68, p&lt;0.001). CT-estimated GFR demonstrated strong correlation with DTPA GFR preoperatively (r=0.74) and postoperatively (r=0.72). WIT showed a significant inverse correlation with postoperative GFR (r=-0.61, p&lt;0.001). Ischemia exceeding 40 minutes was associated with greater functional decline. Multivariate analysis identified WIT as an independent predictor of GFR loss (β=-0.27, p=0.003).</p><p>Conclusion: CT volumetric analysis provides a reliable surrogate for renal functional assessment and correlates well with nuclear renal scans. Minimizing WIT, particularly below 40 minutes, may help preserve postoperative renal function following PN.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-23\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 23</p><h3>Laparoscopic Right Donor Nephrectomy in the Presence of Multiple Renal Veins: An Eight-Case Series Evaluating Feasibility, Technique, and Outcome.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S Mitra, Dr A Kumar, Dr MS Jha, Dr R Goel, Dr KD Guleria</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max superspecialty hospital, Saket, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Right laparoscopic donor nephrectomy (LDN) is less commonly performed compared to left LDN due to the shorter renal vein and the perceived higher risk of venous complications. The challenge becomes more complex when multiple renal veins are present. Procurement of an inferior vena cava (IVC) cuff using a laparoscopic Satinsky clamp may mitigate these limitations alongwith the use of TA 30 stapler. This study evaluates the feasibility, safety, and outcomes of right LDN using these techniques in donors with complex venous anatomy.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: Eight living kidney donors with multiple right renal veins underwent right LDN using a standardized transperitoneal technique. A laparoscopic Satinsky clamp or TA 30 stapler was applied parallel to the IVC to obtain a venous cuff incorporating all renal veins. Operative metrics, donor recovery, and recipient graft outcomes were recorded. Donor characteristics and perioperative outcomes are summarized in Table 1 and Table 2.<br/>\nResults: All procedures were completed laparoscopically without conversion or intraoperative complications. The IVC cuff technique provided adequate renal vein length in all cases. Donor recovery was uneventful, with minimal drain output and drain removal on postoperative day 2. All recipients demonstrated immediate graft function, with average serum creatinine 1.4 mg/dL at discharge.<br/>\nConclusion: Right LDN in the presence of multiple renal veins is feasible and safe when utilizing a laparoscopic Satinsky clamp to obtain an IVC cuff. The technique ensures optimal venous length and excellent graft outcomes. Larger studies are warranted to validate these early results.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-24\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 24</p><h3>Outcomes of Robotic Kidney Transplantation Versus Open Kidney Transplantation in Grafts with Multiple Renal Vessels: A Comparative Retrospective Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S Mitra, Dr A Kumar, Dr MS Jha, Dr R Goel, Dr KD Guleria</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max superspecialty hospital, Saket, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nKidney grafts with multiple renal arteries are common anatomical variations, historically considered surgical challenges. With advances in minimally invasive surgery, robotic-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) has emerged as a feasible option even for complex vascular anatomies. However, comparative data between RAKT and open kidney transplantation (OKT) in grafts with multiple vessels (GMVs) remain limited.<br/>\nObjective:<br/>\nTo compare intraoperative and postoperative outcomes of robotic kidney transplantation versus open kidney transplantation in recipients receiving grafts with multiple renal arteries.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nWe conducted a retrospective analysis at a high-volume transplant center including patients who underwent live-donor kidney transplants between January 2015 and May 2023. Patients were grouped based on surgical approach: RAKT vs. OKT. Only cases with two or more graft vessels were included. Parameters assessed included operative duration, warm ischemia time (WIT), re-warm ischemia time (RIT), intraoperative blood loss, transfusion requirements, delayed graft function (DGF), graft rejection, and estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) postoperatively. Statistical comparison was performed using t-tests and chi-square tests.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nDespite the increased technical complexity, RAKT in GMV demonstrated comparable operative time and WIT to OKT. Postoperative eGFR at 1 week was similar between groups. Robotic cases showed a trend towards reduced blood loss, lower analgesic requirements, shorter incision length, and fewer wound complications. The incidence of DGF and acute rejection was not significantly different.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nRAKT is a safe and effective alternative to OKT in grafts with multiple vessels, offering minimally invasive advantages without compromising graft outcomes. With increasing surgical experience, multiple renal arteries should not be considered a contraindication for robotic kidney transplantation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-25\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 25</p><h3>Beyond the Leak: A Cross sectional study of Knowledge, Attitudes and Practices Regarding Stress and Urge Urinary Incontinence Among Postmenopausal Women</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Qazi Imran, Dr Kunj Patel, Dr Amir Hussain, Dr Abhishek Pathak</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ERA MEDICAL COLLEGE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction : Urinary incontinence (UI) is common among postmenopausal women but is frequently under-recognized and undertreated, particularly in populations with low health literacy. This study assessed the knowledge, attitude and practice (KAP) regarding stress urinary incontinence (SUI) and urgency urinary incontinence (UUI) among postmenopausal women attending a tertiary care centre.</p><p>Methods: A cross-sectional study was conducted among 102 postmenopausal women predominantly belonging to low- and middle-socioeconomic strata. A structured interviewer-administered questionnaire assessed knowledge regarding the causes and types of UI, attitudes towards the condition and healthcare-seeking behaviour, treatment practices, perceived complications and strategies used to cope with urinary leakage.</p><p>Results: The mean age was 59.4 ± 7.1 years. SUI, UUI and mixed UI were reported by 43%, 32% and 25% participants, respectively. Only 38% recognized UI as a medical condition, while 62% considered urinary leakage a normal consequence of ageing. Only 27% could differentiate SUI from UUI and 31% were aware of pelvic-floor exercises. Although 42% believed that UI was treatable, 67% felt embarrassed discussing the problem. Only 28% had previously consulted a doctor and 14% had consulted a urologist; 54% had delayed consultation for more than one year. Pad/cloth use (61%), fluid restriction (47%) and social activity restriction (39%) were common coping practices. Anxiety/embarrassment (46%), recurrent urinary symptoms (31%) and sleep disturbance (28%) were commonly reported complications.</p><p>Conclusion: Significant gaps in knowledge and healthcare-seeking behaviour were identified. Low literacy, misconceptions about ageing and embarrassment appeared to contribute to delayed consultation. Simple counselling and community-based awareness programs may facilitate earlier recognition, appropriate urological referral and effective management and better quality of life.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-26\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 26</p><h3>- Largest Single-Centre Experience of Robot-Assisted Kidney Transplantation: Outcome Analysis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr MUK Sherwani, Dr A Kumar, Dr MS Jha, Dr R Doel, Dr KD Guleria</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max superspecialty hospital, Saket, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>**Background:** Robot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) is a minimally invasive alternative to open kidney transplantation (OKT), but large single-centre series over prolonged periods are limited. We report perioperative and early functional outcomes from a large single-centre RAKT series.</p><p>**Objective:** To evaluate ischemia parameters, anastomosis efficiency, learning-curve trends, and early graft recovery following RAKT.</p><p>**Methods:** Data from 468 consecutive patients undergoing RAKT over 10 years at a tertiary transplant centre were retrospectively analysed. All procedures were performed by the same robotic transplant team. Operative variables included warm ischemia time (WIT), cold ischemia time (CIT), rewarming ischemia time (RWIT), arterial and venous anastomosis times, estimated blood loss, and transfusion requirement. Temporal trends in ischemia and anastomosis times were evaluated. Early graft function was assessed by serum creatinine. Delayed graft function (DGF), postoperative dialysis, and perioperative complications were recorded.</p><p>**Results:** Median recipient age was 40 years and mean BMI was 24.8 kg/m². Median WIT, CIT, and RWIT were 5, 62, and 54 minutes, respectively. Arterial and venous anastomosis times improved progressively with increasing case volume. Estimated blood loss was 200 mL. Multiple renal vessels were managed with tailored reconstruction. Median serum creatinine was 1.4 mg/dL on postoperative day 7, 1.31 mg/dL at day 30, and 1.3 mg/dL at day 90. DGF occurred in 9.8%; postoperative dialysis and major complications occurred in 3.1%. Median hospital stay was 7 days in uncomplicated cases. Two patients required re-exploration for subcapsular hematoma and anastomotic bleeding, while one underwent graft nephrectomy for accelerated rejection.</p><p>**Conclusions:** This large single-centre experience demonstrates favourable perioperative outcomes and early graft functional recovery following RAKT. Progressive improvement in anastomosis times suggests a learning-curve effect. Low major morbidity and favourable graft outcomes support overall feasibility, safety, and reproducibility of RAKT in experienced, high-volume transplant programs in practice.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-27\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 27</p><h3>Impact of Pneumoperitoneum on Graft Histology: A Comparative Zero-Time and Follow-Up Biopsy Analysis in Robotic Versus Open Kidney Transplantation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr MUK Sherwani, Dr A Kumar, Dr MS Jha, Dr Karan, Dr Ritesh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPERSPECIALIY HOSPITAL, SAKET</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>**Background:** Robotic-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) offers minimally invasive advantages; however, concerns remain regarding the effects of pneumoperitoneum on graft perfusion and microstructural integrity. Although pneumoperitoneum may cause transient hypoperfusion, its histological consequences in renal allografts remain inadequately studied.</p><p>**Objective:** To evaluate whether pneumoperitoneum during RAKT causes histological alterations detectable on zero-time biopsy and to correlate these findings with early graft function and subsequent biopsy findings in patients developing graft dysfunction.</p><p>**Methods:** This prospective comparative study included 107 renal transplant recipients: 57 underwent RAKT and 50 open kidney transplantation (OKT). Zero-time biopsies were obtained after graft reperfusion and completion of ureteroneocystostomy, immediately before abdominal closure. Histopathological parameters included interstitial inflammation, tubular injury, vascular changes, C4d deposition, and interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy. Serum creatinine was assessed on postoperative days 1, 3, 5, and 7 and at 30 days. Patients developing graft dysfunction underwent re-biopsy.</p><p>**Results:** RAKT recipients were older, had higher BMI, and included more females than OKT recipients. Rewarming ischemia time and total ischemia time were significantly longer in RAKT. Despite this, zero-time biopsies showed no significant differences in interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy, tubular injury, interstitial inflammation, vascular changes, or C4d deposition. Serum creatinine was comparable at all postoperative time points, including POD1 (3.67 vs. 3.62 mg/dL; *p*=0.99) and POD30 (1.23 vs. 1.19 mg/dL; *p*=0.31). Six patients (5.6%) required re-biopsy: two RAKT and four OKT. Diagnoses included acute tubular injury, acute cellular rejection IIA, calcineurin inhibitor toxicity, borderline T-cell-mediated rejection with acute tubular injury, and acute pyelonephritis. Zero-time biopsy findings did not predict subsequent pathology.</p><p>**Conclusion:** Despite pneumoperitoneum and longer ischemia times, RAKT showed no detectable adverse histological changes on zero-time biopsy and comparable early graft function to OKT. Subsequent graft dysfunction had heterogeneous etiologies unrelated to initial histology, supporting the histological safety and early functional efficacy of RAKT.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-28\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 28</p><h3>Association of urinary stone microbiota with preoperative radiological density and stone composition: a prospective observational study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Shikhar Garg, Dr.Vikas Kumar Panwar, Dr.Ankur Mittal, Dr.Dileep Kumar Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Rishikesh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the association between urinary stone microbiota, preoperative radiological (Hounsfield unit) density, and stone composition in patients with renal and ureteric calculi, and to correlate preoperative urine culture with intraoperative stone culture results.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis ongoing prospective observational study is being conducted in the Department of Urology, AIIMS Rishikesh, over 18 months. Patients aged 18 years and above with radiologically confirmed renal or ureteric stones planned for surgery are enrolled after informed consent; pregnant patients, those with active urinary tract infection, and those on anticoagulants are excluded. Preoperative CT is used to record Hounsfield unit (HU) density at the stone centre. Intraoperatively retrieved stone fragments undergo Fourier Transform Infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy for compositional analysis, and culture by direct inoculation (blood agar, MacConkey, chocolate agar) and enrichment in Brain Heart Infusion broth, with organism identification and antibiotic sensitivity testing performed on VITEK 2 Compact. Preoperative midstream urine culture is compared with the corresponding stone culture. Data will be analysed on SPSS v26 using Kolmogorov-Smirnov, chi-square, and multivariable regression tests, with p&lt;0.05 taken as significant. Calculated sample size is 160.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nRecruitment is ongoing; approximately 150 of the planned 160 patients have been enrolled to date. Preoperative HU values, FT-IR-based stone composition, and paired urine and stone culture data are being compiled for all enrolled patients. Statistical analysis correlating stone microbiota with radiological density and composition is currently underway, and final results will be available on completion of enrolment and laboratory analysis.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nBy correlating a routinely available preoperative imaging parameter (HU density) with stone composition and microbiota, this study aims to help predict infection risk and guide targeted perioperative antibiotic and treatment strategies in urolithiasis before surgery. Complete findings will be presented once enrolment and analysis conclude.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-29\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 29</p><h3>Biparametric Versus Multiparametric MRI for Preoperative Staging of Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer Using the VI-RADS Scoring System: A Prospective Non-Inferiority Study from a North Indian Tertiary Centre</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Shubham Singh, Anupam Shukla, Aniket Anand, Hiralal, Uday Pratap Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGI Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Preoperative distinction between NMIBC and MIBC guides bladder cancer treatment. mpMRI with VI-RADS is standard, but its DCE component requires gadolinium—contraindicated in renal impairment, carrying deposition risk, and increasing scan time/cost by ~50%. Whether bpMRI (T2WI+DWI alone) matches mpMRI accuracy in treatment-naïve Indian patients is unestablished.</p><p>Objective: To prospectively compare bpMRI vs. mpMRI VI-RADS for predicting muscle invasion, test non-inferiority, quantify DCE's added value, and assess cost-effectiveness at a North Indian tertiary centre.</p><p>Methods: 71 treatment-naïve patients with histologically/cystoscopically suspected bladder cancer underwent 3.0T MRI at SGPGI Lucknow (Jan 2022–Mar 2026) after institutional ethical clearance. Two blinded radiologists independently scored bpMRI and mpMRI using VI-RADS, with histopathology as reference standard. Sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, accuracy, and AUC were compared (McNemar's test, DeLong's method); non-inferiority margin pre-specified at δ≤10%; interobserver agreement via Cohen's kappa.</p><p>Results: 32 patients (45.1%) had MIBC. mpMRI showed sensitivity 93.75%, specificity 92.31%, accuracy 92.96%, AUC 0.970; bpMRI showed sensitivity 90.62%, specificity 92.31%, accuracy 91.55%, AUC 0.912. Sensitivity difference was 3.12% (95% CI −5.4 to +11.6%; p=0.317), confirming non-inferiority. AUC difference was non-significant (0.058; p=0.21). Interobserver kappa was comparable (mpMRI 0.82, bpMRI 0.79; p=0.68). DCE changed VI-RADS scores in only 7/71 patients (9.9%), all equivocal VI-RADS 3 cases. bpMRI reduced scan time by 47% (20.4 min; p&lt;0.001) and cost by ~46% (₹3,100/patient), while entirely avoiding gadolinium-related risk.</p><p>Conclusions: bpMRI-based VI-RADS is non-inferior to mpMRI for staging muscle invasion in treatment-naïve bladder cancer, with DCE altering management-relevant scores in &lt;10% of cases. Given substantial reductions in scan time, cost, and gadolinium exposure, bpMRI merits adoption as the first-line VI-RADS protocol in resource-constrained settings.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-30\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 30</p><h3>Adenocarcinoma over lateral wall of urinary bladder managed by robotic assisted partial cystectomy in a CKD patient</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vikas Kumar Bansal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">YASHODA HOSPITAL &amp; RESEARCH CENTRE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>The objective of this presentation was to demonstrate a case of adenocarcinoma over lateral wall of urinary bladder managed by robotic assisted partial cystectomy.<br/>\nA 60 year old diabetic hypertensive and chronic kidney disease gentleman presented to our hospital with complaints of intermittent painless hematuria. On routine evaluation he was found to have a large 3 cm growth over left lateral wall of urinary bladder with a raised serum creatinine of 6.5 mg/dl. The patient underwent TURBT which revealed a solitary large growth over left lateral wall of urinary bladder, the biopsy of which was adenocarcinoma (intestinal type) with deep muscle postive for tumor with FDG PET showing a localized disease. In view of the raised creatinine, patient was managed by robotic partial cystectomy. DJ stent was placed prior to proceeding with robotic to safeguard the left ureter.<br/>\nPatient was discharged on POD 4 with foley catheter<br/>\nHis biopsy revealed :Adenocarcinoma enteric variant,Grade 2 moderately differentiated,Invades muscularis propria<br/>\nAll margins negative with distance from invasive tumor to closest margin 5mm,Left side pelvic lymph nodes positive for tumor.<br/>\nFoley catheter removed after 3 weeks, DJ stent removed after 6 weeks and Referrred to medical oncologist and started on immunotherapy.<br/>\nPartial cystectomy is a feasible option in cases of primary adenocarcinoma of urinary bladder where patient is not fit for a radical cystectomy and ileal conduit<br/>\nWhile doing partial cystectomy, safeguarding the ureter while obtaining a negative margins are of utmost priority<br/>\nPreop U cath or DJ stent placement helps in identifying and safeguarding the ureter<br/>\nSome patient may require adjuvant therapies in cases of lymph node positive disease</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-31\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 31</p><h3>Prospective head-to-head evaluation of MAP and RENAL nephrometry scores for prediction of surgical complexity in robotic partial nephrectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sarthak Sharma, Dr Prashant Gupta, Dr Neeraj Agarwal, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Nachiket Vyas, Dr Keshav Paliwal, Dr Rajat Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS, Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: MAP and RENAL scores anticipate difficulty in robotic partial nephrectomy (RAPN) but describe different anatomy — perinephric fat versus tumour. We asked whether they predict same difficulty or separate domains, and whether combining them adds value.<br/>\nMethods: Thirty consecutive patients undergoing RAPN were prospectively enrolled. Both scores calculated on preoperative CT angiography; adherent perinephric fat (APF) was recorded intraoperatively. Each score was tested head-to-head against every prespecified endpoint — operative time &gt;120 minutes (primary), APF, warm ischaemia time (WIT) &gt;25 minutes, blood loss, complications and Trifecta — using ROC with DeLong comparison, logistic regression, Spearman correlation and bootstrap/leave-one-out validation.<br/>\nResults: The scores changed places according to endpoint. MAP discriminated APF excellently (AUC 0.917) while RENAL performed no better than chance (0.627; DeLong p=0.003). For prolonged WIT the ranking inverted (RENAL 0.947 vs MAP 0.733). For total operative time, they were equivalent (0.889 vs 0.838). APF rose steeply across MAP groups (9.1%, 63.6%, 100%; ρ=0.751, p&lt;0.001); MAP ≥3 was optimal (sensitivity 93.8%, specificity 78.6%, accuracy 86.7%) and predicted longer operative (134 vs 114 minutes, p=0.008) and dissection times (p=0.014). RENAL ≥7 predicted WIT &gt;25 minutes (7/13 vs 0/17, p&lt;0.001), greater blood loss (p=0.024), longer stay (p=0.016) and Trifecta failure (46.2% vs 100%, p&lt;0.001). Combining the scores raised AUCs numerically but not significantly (p=0.234; p=0.583). No conversions or positive margins occurred; major complications 3.3%; eGFR preservation 99.76%.<br/>\nConclusions: MAP and RENAL are neither interchangeable nor additive — they are complementary. MAP measures the dissection challenge before clamping; RENAL the resection challenge after it; only total operative time depends on both. Scoring both preoperatively tells the surgeon not merely how long a case will take, but which part will be difficult — informing scheduling and consent. MAP ≥3 is validated as the working threshold in Indian robotic practice.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-32\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 32</p><h3>Renal Elastography Predicts Drainage Patterns in Pediatric Hydronephrosis: A Prospective Comparative Study with Nuclear Renography</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Devanshu Sharma, Dr Aniket Anand, Dr Anupam Shukla, Prof M.S. Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Pelviureteric junction obstruction (PUJO) is the leading cause of obstructive uropathy in children. While diuretic renography remains the standard for determining obstructive drainage, it involves ionizing radiation and limited availability. Shear wave elastography (SWE) is a novel, non-invasive method that quantifies renal cortical stiffness and may serve as a less invasive alternative for assessing obstruction.</p><p>Objective:<br/>\nTo compare performance of SWE with diuretic renography in prediction of obstructed drainage in children with unilateral hydronephrosis and suspected PUJO.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nIn this prospective observational study, children with unilateral hydronephrosis with suspected PUJO underwent SWE and 99mTc-ethylenedicysteine (EC) renography. SWE yielded Young’s modulus (YM) and shear wave velocity (SWV) values from both kidneys. EC provided differential/split function (SF) and drainage half life in minutes (T1/2) and correlation was analysed. Conditional logistic regression evaluated associations of SWE with obstructed drainage (T1/2&gt;20 min) and impaired function (&lt;40%), while ROC analysis determined diagnostic accuracy and optimal cutoffs.</p><p>Results:<br/>\n82 children (mean age 5.7 ± 4.8 years) were included out of which 35 had T1/2&lt;10, 10 had T1/2 between 10-20 and 37 had T1/2&gt;20. SWE values were significantly higher in affected kidneys (YM: 9.9 ± 5.4 vs 4.5 ± 3.5 kPa; SWV: 1.81 ± 1.17 vs 1.07 ± 0.38 m/s; p &lt; 0.001). SWE correlated positively with drainage T½ (r = 0.51 for YM, 0.43 for SWV) and inversely with SF (r = –0.37 and –0.35, respectively; p &lt; 0.01). SWV independently predicted delayed drainage ( OR 1.79, p = 0.007) and showed borderline association with impaired function. ROC analysis identified YM &gt;7.2 kPa and SWV &gt;1.48 m/s as optimal thresholds for delayed drainage (sensitivity 86.5%, 83.8%).</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nSWE correlates significantly with EC in pediatric PUJO. SWV independently predicts delayed drainage, supporting SWE as a promising non-invasive adjunct to renography.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-33\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 33</p><h3>Clinical Utility of Microstructure Mapping using Hybrid Multidimensional MRI in the Pre-Biopsy Detection of Clinically Significant Prostate Cancer</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shubham Singh, Dr Aaryan Anand, Dr Arpan Yadav , Dr Sanjoy Sureka</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Multiparametric MRI (mpMRI) has become central to prostate cancer (PCa) detection, but its negative predictive value for clinically significant PCa (csPCa; Gleason Grade Group ≥2) remains limited, with interobserver variability. Hybrid multidimensional MRI (HM-MRI), integrating T2-weighted and diffusion information, enables voxel-wise estimation of tissue microstructure and composition. We evaluated whether HM-MRI-derived tissue volume fractions can distinguish csPCa from benign prostate tissue.</p><p>Methods: Following Institutional Ethics Committee approval, 14 men aged 45–78 years with PSA &gt;1 ng/mL and suspected or symptomatic PCa underwent 3.0-T prostate MRI. HM-MRI used three echo times (47, 75, 100 ms) and three diffusion b-values (0, 750, 1500 s/mm²), generating a 3×3 multidimensional signal dataset. Lesions were segmented using PI-RADS v2.1. Custom MATLAB-based processing generated voxel-wise epithelium, lumen, and stroma volume-fraction maps. Biopsy histopathology served as the reference standard. Quantitative HM-MRI parameters were compared between malignant and benign regions using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.</p><p>Results: Cancerous regions demonstrated a markedly higher epithelial volume fraction than benign tissue (35.49±11.75% vs 11.86±8.53%; p&lt;0.001). Epithelial ADC was also lower in cancer than benign tissue (0.35±0.028 vs 0.42±0.093 μm²/ms; p=0.0494). Stromal volume fraction (62.54±10.79% vs 57.95±19.08%; p=0.217) and stromal ADC (1.45±0.21 vs 1.52±0.19 μm²/ms; p=0.391) did not differ significantly.</p><p>Conclusion: HM-MRI provides quantitative, voxel-wise tissue-composition maps and demonstrates a significantly increased epithelial volume fraction in csPCa. These findings support HM-MRI as a promising non-invasive biomarker for prebiopsy prostate cancer characterization, potentially improving lesion discrimination and reducing unnecessary biopsies and enabling more individualized biopsy selection in clinical practice.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-34\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 34</p><h3>Balanitis Xerotica Obliterans with stricture urethra: a series of 72 cases</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ajay Gandhi, Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. J.P. Swami</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Balanitis Xerotica obliterans (BXO) is a common condition affecting prepuce, glans and meatus. It has been postulated that obstruction at meatus leads to extravasation of urine into glands of Littre, with subsequent inflammation and spongiofibrosis, resulting in progression of disease proximally. We cannot use genital skin for reconstruction. Managing such urethral strictures can be challenging with a high recurrence rate.</p><p>Material and method: This is a retrospective study of 72 cases (January 2017 to January 2024) at a tertiary center. It aims to assess the outcome of BXO with meatal/ urethral involvement managed by different techniques.</p><p>Result: These patients were managed by meatoplasty, Buccal mucosal graft (BMG) / lingual graft augmentation urethroplasty or perineal urethrostomy. Two patients had squamous cell carcinoma for which partial penectomy was done.<br/>\nPatients were followed up with symptomatology and uroflowmetry.</p><p>Conclusion:</p><p>This case series highlights the importance of tailored management strategies for BXO-related urethral strictures. Treatment should provide relief of obstruction to prevent progression of disease. BXO with panurethral stricture can be augmented with BMG by transperineal route (Kulkarni technique). Perineal urethrostomy serves as a single stage valuable option for complex panurethral cases particularly in old age. Long term follow up is advocated for recurrence and malignancy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-35\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 35</p><h3>Impact of selecting resectoscope size according to external urethral meatal caliber on post-operative outcomes following Transurethral Resection of the Prostate</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Vibhushit Kaul, Dr. Sanjay Pandey</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sir Ganga Ram Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objective:<br/>\nTransurethral resection of prostate (TURP) remains the standard surgical treatment for benign prostatic enlargement causing bladder outlet obstruction (BOO). Urethral stricture is a recognized post-operative complication, and mismatch between resectoscope size and<br/>\nexternal urethral meatal (EUM) caliber may contribute to its development. Evidence supporting EUM-guided resectoscope selection remains limited, particularly in the Indian population. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of selecting resectoscope size according to EUM caliber on postoperative outcomes, with special emphasis on urethral stricture formation.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA prospective observational study was conducted at a tertiary care center in Western India over 12 months, enrolling 92 men undergoing TURP for bladder outlet obstruction. Under anesthesia, EUM caliber was measured using a meatal calibrator to select a 22 Fr, 24 Fr, or 26 Fr continuous-flow resectoscope. Patients were evaluated at 1 month, 3 months, and 1 year post-operatively for peri-operative parameters, functional outcomes, and complications.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe 22, 24 and 26 Fr resectoscopes were used in 33 (35.9%), 55 (59.8%), and 4 (4.3%) patients, respectively. Mean prostate volume (50.6 ± 26.9 cm³) and mean EUM caliber (25.04 ± 2.08 Fr) were significantly larger in the 24 Fr and 26 Fr groups (p&lt;0.05). Caliber-based selection minimized ancillary meatal dilations or urethrotomies. All groups achieved significant improvements in maximum flow rate, post-void residual volume, IPSS, and quality-of-life scores (p&lt;0.05). Resection time was longer in the 24 Fr cohort (p&lt;0.05). At 1 year, urethral stricture and bladder neck stenosis occurred in only 4 (4.3%) and 3 (3.3%) patients respectively, demonstrating no statistically significant differences between the groups.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nRoutine assessment of EUM caliber before TURP facilitates appropriate<br/>\nresectoscope selection. A caliber-based approach may help avoid additional urethral procedures while maintaining favourable functional outcomes and a low rate of urethral complications.</p><p>Source of Funding: Nil<br/>\nConflict of Interest: Nil</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-36\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 36</p><h3>A Prospective Study to Evaluate the Efficacy of Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery in the Management of Upper Tract Calculi</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR.Shubham Patel,Dr.Devendrakumar sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">C.K.Birla hospital (R.B.H), jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To evaluate the efficacy of RIRS for renal and upper ureteric calculi, and identify factors predicting stone clearance.<br/>\nMethods: In this prospective observational study, 150 patients with upper tract calculi underwent single-surgeon RIRS at a tertiary endourology centre (April 2024–September 2025). SFR was assessed endoscopically at second-look RIRS and by NCCT KUB at 3 months, and analysed against stone burden, location, density (Hounsfield units), and number.<br/>\nResults: Overall SFR was 73% after first RIRS, rising to 94% after second-look RIRS (p&lt;0.001). SFR was significantly lower for stones &gt;2 cm (57% vs 77% for ≤2 cm, p=0.018), lower pole location (61% vs 80%, p=0.012), and density &gt;1000 HU (53% vs 91%, p&lt;0.001). Single versus multiple stones showed no significant difference (80% vs 67%, p=0.10). After second RIRS, SFR improved to 82% for stones &gt;2 cm and 87% for lower pole stones. Complications were low-grade (Clavien I–II): fever/sepsis 8%, bleeding 5%, no transfusions. Plain X-ray KUB detected only 47% of clinically significant residual fragments, underscoring the limitations of imaging follow-up.<br/>\nConclusion: RIRS is a safe, effective, low-morbidity modality for upper tract calculi. Stone burden, density, and lower pole location are key predictors of residual fragments, but staged (second-look) RIRS significantly improves final stone-free rates, making it a valuable, patient-friendly alternative even for larger and anatomically challenging stones.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-37\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 37</p><h3>Decoding Metabolic Rewiring in Renal Cell Carcinoma tissue through High-Resolution ¹H NMR Metabolomics</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR Alok kumar,DR Apoorva saxena ,DR Sanjoy Sureka,DR Uday Pratap Singh,Dr MS AnsarDr</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SANJAY GANDHI POSTGRADUATE INSTITUTE OF MEDICAL SCIENCES</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is the most common kidney cancer in adults, accounting for approximately 90% of cases, with clear cell RCC as the predominant histological subtype and rising global incidence. RCC is marked by extensive molecular heterogeneity and metabolic reprogramming, involving angiogenesis, glucose utilization, and lipid metabolism that drive tumor growth and progression. Early diagnosis remains challenging due to the absence of specific symptoms and reliable biomarkers, and the disease shows relative resistance to chemotherapy and radiotherapy, resulting in poor outcomes. Current systemic treatments, including VEGF and immune checkpoint inhibitors, extend survival but fail to address underlying metabolic vulnerabilities that could serve as novel therapeutic targets. This study aimed to apply untargeted ¹H NMR spectroscopy to profile metabolic alterations in tumor and adjacent normal kidney tissues from RCC patients, to identify tumor-specific biomarkers and assess metabolic differences across diabetic status, histological subtypes, and risk stratifications.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: Tumor and adjacent normal kidney tissues from RCC patients were analyzed using untargeted ¹H NMR spectroscopy-based metabolic profiling. Multivariate and univariate statistical analyses were performed to identify differentially expressed metabolites. Diagnostic performance was assessed using area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) analysis, and pathway enrichment analysis was conducted to determine biological pathways associated with the identified alterations.<br/>\nResults: Ten metabolites showed high diagnostic accuracy (AUROC &gt; 0.7) in distinguishing tumor from adjacent normal tissue, including glucose, phosphorylcholine, serine, myo-inositol, phosphoenolpyruvic acid, maltose, histidine betaine, uridine, and taurine. Pathway enrichment analysis implicated significant alterations in glycolysis, amino acid metabolism, and one-carbon metabolism, pathways critical for biosynthesis and redox homeostasis in tumor cells.<br/>\nConclusions: These findings provide novel insights into the metabolic reprogramming underlying RCC and support the utility of ¹H NMR–based metabolomics for identifying tumor-specific biomarkers and uncovering potential therapeutic targets in RCC.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-38\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 38</p><h3>A randomized controlled trial comparing laparoscopic partial nephrectomy to robotic assisted partial nephrectomy — short-term outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Shubham Patel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">C.k.birla hospital (R.B.H) Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo compare warm ischemia time (WIT) between laparoscopic partial nephrectomy (LPN) and robot-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN), and to evaluate intraoperative, postoperative, pathological and renal functional outcomes between the two approaches.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis prospective, randomized controlled trial enrolled 64 patients with localized renal masses at a tertiary-care center, randomized 1:1 (32 LPN, 32 RAPN) using computer-generated sequences stratified by R.E.N.A.L. nephrometry score. Sample size was powered for the primary outcome (WIT). Intraoperative, postoperative, pathological and 3-month renal functional parameters were compared using independent t-test/Mann-Whitney U and chi-square/Fisher's exact tests, with p&lt;0.05 considered significant.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nBaseline demographics, comorbidities and tumor complexity were comparable. Mean WIT was 24.65±10.23 min (LPN) versus 21.78±6.82 min (RAPN, p=0.24). Operative time, blood loss, transfusion rates and hospital stay were similar between groups. Conversion to open surgery occurred in 12.5% of LPN cases versus 0% in RAPN (p=0.11). Paralytic ileus was more frequent with LPN (15.6% vs 0%, p=0.052). Negative surgical margins were achieved in 100% of cases in both arms. At 3 months, RAPN showed significantly smaller rises in serum creatinine (0.06±0.11 vs 0.12±0.12 mg/dL, p=0.04) and a trend toward better eGFR preservation (p=0.05) compared to LPN.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nRAPN achieves shorter warm ischemia time, fewer conversions, lower paralytic ileus rates, and superior short-term renal functional preservation compared to LPN, with equivalent oncological adequacy. LPN remains a safe and effective alternative in experienced hands. Approach selection should be individualized based on tumor complexity, surgeon expertise and institutional resources.<br/>\nConflict of Interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-39\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 39</p><h3>The chameleon of the bladder: Navigating clinical traps and management dilemmas in urinary bladder paragangliomas</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Vibhushit Kaul, Dr. Milind More, Dr. Mahesh Gupta, Dr. Sudhir Chadha</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sir Ganga Ram Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction/Aims:<br/>\nUrinary bladder paragangliomas (UBPG) are rare extra-adrenal neuroendocrine tumors arising from chromaffin cells. Due to their submucosal location, catecholamine secretion, and metastatic potential, they pose unique diagnostic and therapeutic challenges. This case series aims to highlight the clinical pitfalls, severe hemodynamic risks, and management dilemmas associated with handling unsuspected UBPGs during endoscopic surgery.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe retrospectively analyzed three patients diagnosed with UBPG following transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT). Data collection included clinical presentation, intraoperative events, histopathology, and postoperative management. Postoperative functional imaging was utilized to guide subsequent surgical and surveillance strategies. Clinical and radiological follow-up ranged from 3 to 12 months.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nPresentations included lower urinary tract symptoms, gross hematuria, and incidental detection. All tumors demonstrated classic neuroendocrine morphology without malignant features; one case showed muscularis propria invasion. One patient experienced a severe intraoperative hypertensive crisis during TURBT due to a lack of preoperative alpha-blockade. Postoperative functional imaging guided subsequent management decisions. Based on these findings, one patient underwent robot-assisted completion partial cystectomy for suspected residual disease (final pathology confirmed no residual tumor). The second patient, who had muscularis propria invasion, declined further surgical intervention. The third patient remained on active surveillance after negative functional imaging. At short-term follow-up (3–12 months), all three patients remain alive and completely recurrence-free.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nUBPGs require a high index of clinical suspicion for preoperative detection. Operating on unsuspected UBPGs without alpha-blockade introduces profound hemodynamic risks. While TURBT remains a viable diagnostic and therapeutic approach for small tumors, it carries inherent risks of incomplete excision and crisis. Successful outcomes depend on meticulous preoperative optimization, functional imaging, and multi-disciplinary care. Long-term surveillance is mandatory given the risk of late metastasis.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-40\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 40</p><h3>Thulium Fibre Laser versus Holmium:YAG Laser for Renal Stone Lithotripsy during RIRS: A Prospective Randomized Indian Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Rajnish Kumar, Dr. Bhagwati chandra verma, Dr. Sahil Data, Dr. P wadhwa, Dr. Manav Suryavanshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Amrita School of Medicine and Hospital, Faridabad, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo compare the lithotripsy efficacy, operative outcomes and safety of Thulium Fibre Laser (TFL) versus Holmium:YAG (Ho:YAG) laser during retrograde intrarenal surgery (RIRS) for renal calculi.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis was a single-centre prospective randomized clinical study conducted at a tertiary-care corporate hospital in India from June 2024 to May 2025. To our knowledge, this represents the first Indian prospective randomized clinical comparison of TFL and Ho:YAG during RIRS. Eighty patients were randomized equally to TFL (n=40) and Ho:YAG (n=40). Primary outcomes were laser-on time (LOT), stone ablation speed and lithotripsy efficacy (J/mm³). Secondary outcomes included total operative time, stone-clearance time, energy consumption, intraoperative visibility, stone-free rate (SFR) and perioperative complications. Stone-free status was assessed at approximately 2 weeks, with residual fragments &lt;3 mm considered clinically insignificant.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nBaseline stone burden was comparable between groups. Mean stone size was 13.22±3.79 mm with Ho:YAG and 13.95±4.74 mm with TFL (p=0.446). Total operative time was significantly shorter with Ho:YAG (47.85±20.60 vs 64.82±22.57 min; p=0.001), as was stone-clearance time (38.65±18.93 vs 53.98±19.62 min; p=0.001). LOT was significantly lower with Ho:YAG (5.48±5.39 vs 16.52±13.73 min; p&lt;0.001), with lower total energy consumption (6557.75±6855.74 vs 12800±12456.26 J; p=0.007). Lithotripsy efficacy was comparable (13.20±13.70 vs 17.40±11.70 J/mm³; p=0.141). TFL provided significantly better intraoperative visibility (3.72±0.55 vs 3.40±0.67; p=0.021). SFR was 80% with Ho:YAG versus 62.5% with TFL (p=0.084). Perioperative complications were comparable, with no postoperative urosepsis.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nIn this first Indian prospective randomized clinical comparison, Ho:YAG demonstrated significantly shorter LOT and operative duration, whereas TFL provided superior intraoperative visibility. Both lasers demonstrated comparable lithotripsy efficacy and safety. Larger multicentric Indian studies with standardized laser settings and matched stone characteristics are warranted.<br/>\nConflict of Interest<br/>\nNone declared.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-41\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 41</p><h3>Effectiveness of transcutaneous posterior tibial nerve stimulation in children with underactive bladder refractory to conservative management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Mohd Azfar, Dr Shubham Singh, Dr Anupam Shukla, Dr Mohammed Suleh Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nUnderactive bladder (UAB) in children is characterized by impaired detrusor contractility, incomplete bladder emptying, elevated post-void residual (PVR) urine, and recurrent urinary tract infections. Transcutaneous posterior tibial nerve stimulation (TTNS) is non-invasive neuromodulation technique that may improve lower urinary tract function. However, evidence regarding its efficacy in children with neurogenic and non-neurogenic UAB remains limited.</p><p>Aim<br/>\nTo evaluate the effectiveness of TTNS in children with UAB and compare treatment outcomes between neurogenic and non-neurogenic UAB.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nThis retrospective observational study included 52 children with UAB who underwent TTNS between 2013 and 2022. Patients received 12 weekly TTNS sessions. Clinical outcomes were assessed using bladder-diaries, clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) frequency, PVR measurement, and urodynamic parameters before and after treatment. Patients with recent bladder surgery, pelvic floor interventions, or active urinary tract infection were excluded. Outcomes were compared between neurogenic and non-neurogenic groups.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe non-neurogenic group demonstrated significant improvement in bladder emptying following TTNS. Mean PVR decreased from 248 mL to 84 mL (p&lt;0.0001), while the frequency of CIC decreased from 6 to 2.4 times per day (p&lt;0.001). Night-time urinary incontinence episodes also decreased significantly. Although several urodynamic parameters showed improvement, changes in bladder capacity, voided volume, detrusor pressure at maximum flow (Pdet@Qmax), and maximum urinary flow rate (Qmax) were not statistically significant. Patients with neurogenic UAB demonstrated only minimal improvement, with no significant changes in spontaneous voiding or voided volume.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nTTNS appears to be a promising non-invasive treatment for pediatric UAB, particularly in children with non-neurogenic dysfunction. Significant reductions in PVR, CIC frequency, and nocturnal incontinence suggest improved bladder emptying and functional outcomes. However, its efficacy appears limited in neurogenic UAB, potentially reflecting impaired neural pathways. Larger prospective controlled studies with longer follow-up are required to confirm these findings and identify children most likely to benefit from TTNS</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-42\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 42</p><h3>Role of restaging transurethral resection of bladder tumour (Re-TURBT) in high-grade and lamina-invasive non-muscle-invasive urothelial carcinoma: is it overtreatment?</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Apoorva Saxena,Dr Ashish Ranjan,Dr Sanchit Rustagi,Dr Uday Pratap Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Restaging TURBT (Re-TURBT) is guideline-recommended for high-grade and T1 non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer but carries surgical, financial and psychological burden and may be overtreatment when the primary resection is complete. Much of its reported benefit derives from cohorts including externally performed, variable-quality resections. We assessed the rate and predictors of residual disease and upstaging in a cohort in which both the primary TURBT and Re-TURBT were performed in-house, holding resection quality constant.</p><p>Methods: Single-centre prospective observational study (August 2024–July 2026), Department of Urology, SGPGIMS. Adults with high-grade and/or T1 urothelial carcinoma, or absent detrusor muscle in the primary specimen, in whom both resections were performed in-house, were included; externally resected patients were excluded. Re-TURBT at 4–6 weeks included complete resection and a dedicated deep tumour-bed biopsy. Residual disease (any urothelial carcinoma in the Re-TURBT specimen) and upstaging (≥T2) were determined; associations were tested with Fisher's exact test.</p><p>Results: 148 in-house patients were enrolled (mean age 62.4 ± 14.8 years; 91.9% male); 50 reached the pre-specified evaluable target and all were assessable. Residual disease occurred in 16/50 (32.0%) and upstaging to muscle-invasive disease in 0/50 (0%; 0% of T1). Tumour multiplicity was the dominant predictor: 48.5% residual disease with multiple tumours versus 0% with solitary tumours (p&lt;0.001) — no solitary, completely resected tumour harboured residual disease. Residual disease was numerically higher with absent detrusor muscle (54.5% vs 27.8%) and high grade (44.0% vs 22.7%); stage was not predictive.</p><p>Conclusions: After a high-quality in-house primary resection, residual disease (32%) and especially upstaging (0%) lay at the lower end of published ranges, with residual disease confined to multifocal, detrusor-absent or high-grade tumours. These data support selective de-escalation of Re-TURBT in carefully chosen patients with a solitary, completely resected tumour, pending multivariable analysis and survival follow-up.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-43\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 43</p><h3>Clinical Characteristics, Prognostic Factors and Survival Outcomes in Carcinoma Penis: A Retrospective Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Rahil Kumar, Rohit Gupta, Saurabh Meshram, Prashant Singh, Amlesh Seth, Brusabhanu Nayak</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objectives: Penile carcinoma is common in low- and middle-income countries, including India, where patients frequently present with more advanced disease compared to Western populations. Data on tumour and nodal determinants of survival in surgically managed Indian cohorts remain limited. We aimed to describe the clinical, tumour and nodal profile and to identify predictors of overall survival (OS) in patients undergoing surgery for carcinoma penis at our tertiary centre.<br/>\nMethods: A retrospective observational study was conducted on 144 consecutive patients who underwent primary surgical management for histologically confirmed penile carcinoma at our tertiary care center. All relevant epidemiological, clinical, pathological data including various treatment modalities, nodal staging were collected. OS was estimated for the cohort of 135 patients using the Kaplan–Meier survival analysis and univariable and multivariable Cox proportional-hazards regression model to identify clinico-pathological predictors of survival were generated.<br/>\nResults: The median patient age was 56 years and majority of them belong to hindu community, Squamous cell carcinoma was the predominant subtype (94.4%). Clinically node-positive disease was present in 41.7% of the cohort. Of the patients who underwent inguinal or pelvic nodal dissection, 46.6% were pathologically node-positive (pN+). Over a median follow-up of 48 months, the median OS was 112 months, and the 5-year OS was 72%. Pathological nodal positivity, lymphovascular invasion (LVI), and perineural invasion (PNI) were significantly associated with inferior survival outcomes. In multivariable analysis, PNI remained independently associated with an increased risk of death (adjusted HR 2.89; p=0.036)<br/>\nConclusions: In this surgically managed cohort, adverse local-pathology features (PNI and LVI) and pathological nodal involvement were the principal determinants of overall survival. These findings reinforce the critical prognostic value of accurate histopathological assessment and support guideline-concordant, risk-adapted surgical management of the inguinal lymph nodes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-44\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 44</p><h3>Spectrum of Nephrectomy Indications and Surgical Outcomes: A Three-Year retrospective Study from a Tertiary Care Centre</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Uma Kant Dutt, Dr Kajari, Dr Gunjan, Dr Nishikant</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS BILASPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Nephrectomy remains an integral procedure in the management of benign and malignant renal diseases. This study evaluated the clinicodemographic profile, indications, laterality, surgical approach and perioperative outcomes of nephrectomy at a tertiary care urology centre.<br/>\nMethods: A retrospective review of all nephrectomy-related procedures performed between May 2023 and May 2026 was conducted. Demographic characteristics, indications, laterality, operative approach, procedure type, operative duration, hospital stay, blood transfusion, comorbidities and perioperative complications were analysed.<br/>\nResults: 45 Nephrectomy procedures were performed; mean age was 51.2 years (18–79), with 24 (53.3%) males. Benign indications accounted for 27 (60.0%) cases, with stone-related non-functioning kidney being the commonest indication (19, 42.2%), followed by PUJ obstruction (4, 8.9%), EPN (2, 4.4%) and XGPN (1, 2.2%). Traumatic renal injury accounted for 1 (2.2%) case. Malignant diseases accounted for 18 (40.0%) cases, comprising RCC (13, 28.9%), upper tract urothelial carcinoma (4, 8.9%) and urinary bladder SCC with Right high grade TCC along with PUNLMP and small Poor functioning kidney (1, 2.2%). Among stone-related cases, calculi were located in the PUJ (10), upper ureter (6), Lower ureter (3). Right-sided procedures 9predominated (25, 55.6%) and 6 patients on Percutaneous drainage tube due to Pyonephrosis. Laparoscopic approach was used in 42 (93.3%) cases, including 3 (6.7%) conversions to open surgery and one combined laparoscopic nephroureterectomy with open cystectomy; 3 (6.7%) procedures were primarily open. Median operative time was approximately 3.2 hours and median hospital stay was 5 days. Perioperative complications included pleural injury (1), bowel injury (1), wound infection (2). Blood transfusion was required in selected cases, with no perioperative mortality.<br/>\nConclusion: Benign stone disease remains the predominant indication for nephrectomy in our region. Laparoscopic nephrectomy is a safe and effective approach across a broad spectrum of renal pathologies, with low conversion rates and acceptable perioperative morbidity</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-45\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 45</p><h3>Predicting outcomes of patients undergoing TURBT using 5 item Frailty index: An Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Raghav Khurana, dr Prashant Gupta, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Nachiket Vyas, Dr Neeraj Aggarwal, Dr, Dharmender Jangid, Dr Somender Bansal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Medical College and Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the five-item modified frailty index (mFI-5) as a predictor of adverse postoperative outcomes after transurethral resection of bladder tumour (TURBT), estimate frailty prevalence, and compare outcomes across frailty strata.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis prospective observational study included 168 patients undergoing TURBT at a tertiary-care centre in Jaipur. Patients were stratified as Group A (mFI-5 = 0), Group B (mFI-5 = 1–2), and Group C (mFI-5 &gt;=3). Postoperative complications, transfusion, ICU requirement, length of stay, 30-day readmission, and mortality were recorded. Multivariable Firth logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, tumour size, and completeness of resection.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nSevere frailty was present in 12 patients (7.1%). Overall, 57 patients (33.9%) developed postoperative complications. Complication rates increased from 26.6% in Group A and 31.5% in Group B to 91.7% in Group C (P &lt; 0.001). Compared with Group A, Group C independently predicted any complication (adjusted OR 12.22), Clavien–Dindo grade II or higher morbidity (OR 10.63), transfusion (OR 7.15), ICU care (OR 135.87), hospital stay of at least four days (OR 185.98), and increased healthcare-resource utilisation (OR 12.15). Each additional mFI-5 point independently increased the odds of any complication by 76%, significant morbidity two-fold, transfusion by 89%, ICU requirement 4.54-fold, and prolonged stay 3.48-fold. The index showed excellent discrimination for prolonged hospitalisation (AUC 0.861) and ICU requirement (AUC 0.840).<br/>\nDiscussion<br/>\nThe observed graded relationship between frailty and adverse outcomes remained independent of tumour size and operative factors. An mFI-5 &gt;=3 was highly specific for complications and ICU need, supporting its role as a preoperative rule-in red flag rather than a screening tool.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nThe mFI-5 is a rapid, inexpensive, and clinically useful predictor of morbidity and healthcare utilisation after TURBT. Routine preoperative assessment can identify high-risk patients for comorbidity optimisation, informed counselling, enhanced monitoring, and anticipatory ICU planning.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-46\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 46</p><h3>Impact of a structured intraoperative surgical checklist on resection quality and early oncological outcomes in transurethral resection of bladder tumour: a prospective comparative study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Digvijay Singh Tanwar, Dr A. S. Sandhu, Dr Jaideep Singh Soni</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo determine whether a structured eleven-item intraoperative checklist improves resection quality and early oncological outcomes after primary TURBT using a design that avoids checklist \"carry-over\" contamination between arms.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nIn this prospective comparative cohort study (October 2024–June 2026) at a tertiary institute, 66 adults undergoing primary TURBT were allocated — to an eleven-item checklist (n=33; covering tumour characteristics, carcinoma in situ, bimanual examination under anaesthesia, complete resection, detrusor visualisation, perforation, tumour mapping and narrow-band imaging [NBI]) or standard practice (n=33). Primary outcome was resection quality (complete resection, detrusor muscle in specimen, complications); secondary outcome was recurrence at three-month surveillance cystoscopy.</p><p>Results<br/>\nBaseline characteristics were mostly comparable . Adherence to every process indicator rose sharply (all p&lt;0.001): bimanual examination before (97.0% vs 0%) and after (87.9% vs 0%) resection, tumour-location documentation (100% vs 36.4%), and NBI before (100% vs 0%) and after (60.6% vs 0%) resection; NBI detected additional lesions in 24.2% of patients. Complete resection (97.0% vs 93.9%) and detrusor muscle sampling (88.5% vs 81.3%; risk difference +7.2%, 95% CI −12.8 to +25.4; p=0.50) did not differ, and the sole perforation occurred in the control arm. Recurrence at three months was 10.5% vs 16.7% (p=0.66). Muscle-invasive disease was less frequent in the checklist unit (9.1% vs 30.3%), attributable to case-mix differences between units rather than treatment effect.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nAn intraoperative checklist transformed process quality and documentation completeness in TURBT without added morbidity, in a design that excluded cross-contamination between arms, but did not detectably alter resection quality or early recurrence — the study was underpowered for these endpoints. It remains a simple, low-cost tool to standardise TURBT and reduce reliance on surgeon experience; larger RCTs are needed to confirm oncological benefit.</p><p>Conflict of interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-47\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 47</p><h3>Predictors of Histopathology at Post-Chemotherapy RPLND in Non-Seminomatous Germ Cell Tumors: A Single-Center Retrospective Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Shikhar Garg, Dr.Vikas Kumar Panwar, Dr.Ankur Mittal, Dr.Shivam Upadhayaye</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Rishikesh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: The post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal lymph node dissection (PC-RPLND)<br/>\nrepresents the standard therapy modality for patients with residual retroperitoneal masses after<br/>\nchemotherapy for non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT). These masses may consist of<br/>\nnecrosis/fibrosis, mature teratoma, or remaining germ cell tumor. The mature teratoma does<br/>\nnot respond to chemotherapy and requires complete surgical excision, therefore, its<br/>\npreoperative prediction is of clinical importance. However, due to the limitations in differential<br/>\ndiagnosis by conventional imaging technique this study is aimed to investigate the connection<br/>\nbetween radiologic response to chemotherapy and the presence of residual teratoma on PC-<br/>\nRPLND.<br/>\nObjective: To determine the presence of residual teratoma in patients undergoing PC-RPLND<br/>\nfor NSGCT by radiological response following first-line chemotherapy<br/>\nMethodology: A retrospective observational study was carried out at a tertiary referral centre<br/>\namong the consecutive cases of NSGCT patients who received chemotherapy along with PC-<br/>\nRPLND procedure. Data was collected which comprises the demographic, clinical, pathologic,<br/>\nradiologic, and treatment characteristics of the patients. The patients were classified according<br/>\nto the presence or absence of residual teratoma at the histopathology. The relationship between<br/>\nradiological response and residual teratoma was studied using appropriate statistical tests.<br/>\nResults: A total of 13 NSGCT patients who underwent PC-RPLND were studied (mean age<br/>\n29.1 + 9.6 years). Histopathology was found to reveal necrosis/fibrosis in 46.2% (n=6), mature<br/>\nteratoma in 38.5% (n=5), and viable GCT in 15.4% (n=2). Partial radiological response<br/>\ncorrelated with fibrosis, whereas stable masses after marker normalization predicted mature<br/>\nteratoma.<br/>\nConclusion: Establishing the predictive value of radiological response may improve<br/>\npreoperative risk stratification, optimize surgical decision-making, and facilitate personalized<br/>\nmanagement of patients with NSGCT undergoing PC-RPLND.<br/>\nKeywords: Non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT), Post-chemotherapy retroperitoneal<br/>\nlymph node dissection (PC-RPLND), Predictive factors, Radiological response, Residual<br/>\nteratoma</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-48\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 48</p><h3>Beyond the Stone: Unmasking Hereditary Xanthinuria in Early Childhood</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aisha Fatma, Dr Manoj Talwar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">batra hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nHereditary xanthinuria is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder of purine metabolism caused by impaired conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Type I results from xanthine dehydrogenase (XDH) deficiency. Accumulation of poorly soluble xanthine may cause urolithiasis, although presentation in early childhood is uncommon.<br/>\nCase Report<br/>\nA 1-year-10-month-old male child presented with fever, recurrent vomiting, abdominal pain, poor oral intake and decreased urine output. Evaluation revealed neutrophilic leukocytosis, positive C-reactive protein and 25–30 pus cells/HPF on urine microscopy, consistent with urinary tract infection. Renal function was preserved (urea 18 mg/dL, creatinine 0.26 mg/dL). Further evaluation revealed bulky Left kidney bulky with a 10 mm calculus at mid pole calyx of left kidney . Metabolic evaluation demonstrated profoundly reduced serum uric acid (&lt;0.5 mg/dL) with elevated xanthine levels, raising suspicion of hereditary xanthinuria. After medical management for UTI ,patient underwent mini PCNL with an uneventful postoperative course and satisfactory recovery. Stone analysis revealed pure xanthine stone ,whereas whole-exome sequencing identified a heterozygous pathogenic XDH frameshift variant, c.140_141insG (p.Cys48LeufsTer12), associated with xanthinuria type I. No second significant XDH variant was detected.<br/>\nDiscussion<br/>\nXDH deficiency results in reduced uric acid production with accumulation of xanthine and hypoxanthine. Poor solubility of xanthine predisposes to stone formation, obstruction, recurrent infection and progressive renal damage. The combination of early-onset urolithiasis, profound hypouricemia and elevated xanthine strongly supports an underlying disorder of xanthine metabolism. Early recognition permits preventive measures such as adequate hydration and dietary purine restrictions etc.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-49\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 49</p><h3>Laparoscopic Orchiopexy: A Series of 51 Cases</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Gaurav Jain, Dr. Taranpreet, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra, Dr. J.P. Swami</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Undescended testis (UDT) is among the most common congenital anomalies in male children, with approximately 20% being non-palpable. Intra-abdominal testes are challenging because of localization and surgical difficulties. Laparoscopy has emerged as the gold standard for diagnosis and definitive treatment of non-palpable testes.</p><p>Objective: To evaluate the role of laparoscopy in the diagnosis and management of intra-abdominal testes and assess outcomes of different laparoscopic treatment strategies.</p><p>Materials and Methods: A retrospective analysis was conducted in 51 patients with intra-abdominal testes managed between January 2018 and July 2025. All patients underwent examination under general anaesthesia followed by diagnostic laparoscopy. Assessment included testicular position, distance from the internal inguinal ring, mobility, spermatic vessel length, and vas deferens anatomy. Based on these findings, patients underwent single-stage laparoscopic orchiopexy, two-stage Fowler–Stephens orchiopexy, or orchidectomy. Postoperative follow-up at 1, 3, and 6 months assessed testicular position and viability.</p><p>Results: Fifty-one intra-abdominal testes were evaluated. Single-stage laparoscopic orchiopexy was performed in 45 cases, while 5 patients underwent two-stage Fowler–Stephens orchiopexy. Orchidectomy was performed in one patient with DSD, whose testis was located very high intra-abdominally, just below the kidney. Overall, orchiopexy had a high success rate, with most testes viable and well positioned during follow-up. At 6 months, the majority of orchiopexied testes were located in the lower scrotum with preserved size and consistency. No postoperative testicular atrophy was observed.</p><p>Conclusion: Laparoscopy is the diagnostic modality of choice for non-palpable testes under anaesthesia and provides a safe and versatile approach for intra-abdominal testes. Intra-operative assessment of testicular position, mobility, and vascular anatomy guides selection between single-stage and staged procedures. Orchidectomy is reserved for selected postpubertal or nonfunctional testes, suspected malignancy, or high-risk gonads in DSD. Routine laparoscopic exploration should be considered mandatory in all cases of non-palpable testes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-50\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 50</p><h3>Analysis of 259 Cases of Penile Fracture Over 12 Years</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Gaurav Jain, Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Topic: Analysis of 259 Cases of Penile Fracture Over 12 Years<br/>\nObjective:<br/>\nThe aim of this study was to report our experience with 259 cases of penile fracture managed at a tertiary care centre over a period of 12 years (2012 onwards).<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nThe primary diagnostic evaluation was based on clinical history and physical examination in 243 (93.9%) patients. Penile ultrasonography (USG) was performed in 29 (11.17%) cases, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in 3 (1.12%) cases, and retrograde urethrography (RGU) in 10 (3.91%) patients.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nOf the 259 patients, 178 (68.7%), presenting with a classical history along with penile swelling and deformity, underwent surgical exploration. Four (1.68%) patients had associated urethral injury, which was repaired after spatulation over a 14 Fr catheter. A few patients had involvement of all three corpora with associated urethral injury.<br/>\nThe remaining 81 (31.3%) patients, who had a classical history and recurrent urethral bleeding during erection but no significant penile swelling, were managed conservatively with 14 Fr Foley catheterization and compression dressing. In selected cases, USG, MRI, and RGU were performed for diagnostic confirmation and documentation.<br/>\nIn the surgical group, three patients had a longitudinal corporal tear, while a few patients had complete urethral transection associated with bilateral corporal tears. Three patients developed new-onset erectile dysfunction (one mild and two moderate), which subsequently resolved during follow-up.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nPenile fracture is primarily a clinical diagnosis and generally requires immediate surgical repair. However, patients with penile fracture and suspected urethral injury without penile swelling, presenting with unexplained recurrent nocturnal urethral bleeding, may be managed conservatively in appropriately selected cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-51\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 51</p><h3>Adult hypospadias repair – tertiary care center experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Taranpreet Singh, Gaurav Jain, Mukesh Arya, Ajay Gandhi, Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SPMC, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nHypospadias is a common congenital anomaly that needs repair at an early age (six months to one year). Ironically, many cases in India present late due to a lack of healthcare facilities, poverty, and illiteracy. Adult patients are different from children as they are aware of their genitalia.<br/>\nObjectives:<br/>\nIn this study, we present the perspectives and outcomes of 111 adult cases of hypospadias.<br/>\nMethodology:<br/>\nIn this retrospective study conducted between January 2010 and December 2020, 111 patients aged more than 14 years who were diagnosed with hypospadias of any level with or without mild-to-moderate chordee were included. Hypospadias repair using a tubularized incised plate (TIP) urethroplasty technique was performed.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nAge varied from 14 years to 32 years (mean = 19.88 years, SD = 5.93). The most frequent meatus positions after chordee adjustment were distal (n = 64, 57.65%), middle (n = 25, 22.52%), and proximal (n = 22,19.82%). Among these, four patients had penoscrotal transposition. Chordee was present in 65.7% (n = 73) of the cases. (&lt;30° in 38.7%, n = 43; 30°-60° in 23.4%, n = 26, and &gt;60° in 3.6%, n = 4). Chordee was corrected using many techniques, including ventral corporotomies. Urethroplasty was done using TIP and spongioplasty in 89% (n = 99), and one patient underwent inner preputial onlay flap urethroplasty. Snodgraft was used to augment the urethral plate in 10 cases. The success rate of one-stage surgery was 74.77% in our series, which significantly correlated with PROs.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nAdult patients undergoing primary hypospadias repair generally show good outcomes. Patients can have an acceptable mild degree of residual chordee and torsion, which correlate well with PROs. In our series, hypospadias fistula was the most common complication of hypospadias surgery, followed by glanular dehiscence.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-52\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 52</p><h3>Peri-anastomotic platelet-rich plasma and Doppler-defined maturation of radiocephalic arteriovenous fistulas: a randomized controlled trial</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Palash Sawhney, Dr Ashish Kumar, Dr. Swarnendu Mandal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, BHUBANESWAR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nFailure of arteriovenous fistula (AVF) maturation remains an important limitation of native vascular access for hemodialysis. Platelet-rich plasma (PRP), through growth factors involved in endothelial repair, angiogenesis and matrix remodeling, may potentially enhance AVF maturation. However, clinical evidence for PRP as an adjunct during AVF creation remains limited.</p><p>### Objective</p><p>To evaluate whether local peri-anastomotic autologous PRP improves Doppler-defined maturation of newly created radiocephalic AVFs.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nThis prospective, parallel-group randomized controlled trial was conducted during 2024–2026 and included 96 adults with end-stage kidney disease undergoing radiocephalic AVF creation. After successful fistula creation and confirmation of an immediate thrill, participants were randomized 1:1 to peri-anastomotic PRP infiltration or standard AVF creation alone. PRP was prepared using a standardized double-spin technique and 2–6 mL was infiltrated around the anastomosis. The primary outcome was AVF maturation at 3 months, defined as draining cephalic-vein diameter ≥4 mm and access flow ≥500 mL/min. Secondary outcomes included maturation at 1 and 2 months and early postoperative complications.</p><p>Results<br/>\nForty-eight participants were analyzed in each group with complete 3-month follow-up. At 3 months, AVF maturation occurred in 35/48 (72.9%) patients in the PRP group and 36/48 (75.0%) controls (RR 0.97, 95% CI 0.77–1.23; p=1.00). Maturation at 1 month was 20.8% versus 12.5% and at 2 months 62.5% versus 58.3%, respectively, without statistically significant differences. At 3 months, vein diameter ≥4 mm was achieved in 85.4% versus 87.5%, while access flow ≥500 mL/min was achieved in 79.2% versus 81.3%. Early thrombosis occurred in 10.4% versus 8.3%, and other postoperative complications were uncommon and comparable between groups.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nPeri-anastomotic autologous PRP was safe but did not improve 3-month Doppler-defined radiocephalic AVF maturation. Although numerically higher early maturation was observed with PRP, this was not sustained. These findings do not support routine PRP use for this indication.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-53\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 53</p><h3>Evaluating the Role of Enhanced Culture Techniques in Diagnosing and Treating Urinary Tract Infections in Patients with Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: A Prospective Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Palash Sawhney, Dr Ashish Kumar, Dr Swarnendu Mandal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, BHUBANESWAR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Routine urine culture may miss low-count or fastidious organisms in men with benign prostatic enlargement (BPE) and lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS), potentially limiting the recognition of clinically relevant bacteriuria.<br/>\nMethods: This prospective observational study included 203 men aged &gt;50 years with BPE and moderate-to-severe LUTS at a tertiary-care centre. Paired midstream urine samples underwent routine culture and enhanced quantitative urine culture (EQUC), which used a larger inoculum, multiple media, 5% CO₂ incubation for 48 hours, and a detection threshold of ≥10² CFU/mL. Clinical, symptom, uroflowmetric, ultrasonographic, microbiological, and antimicrobial-susceptibility data were analysed.<br/>\nResults: Routine culture detected pathogens in 27/203 patients (13.3%), compared with 57/203 (28.1%) using EQUC. EQUC identified 30 additional infections among 176 routine-culture-negative patients, corresponding to an incremental yield of 17.0% (95% CI, 12.2%–23.3%; exact McNemar p&lt;0.001). Of the EQUC-positive samples, 42/57 (73.7%) showed low-count bacteriuria of 10² to &lt;10⁵ CFU/mL. EQUC positivity occurred in 42.5% of patients with severe LUTS versus 3.9% with moderate LUTS and increased from 0% at a post-void residual urine volume &lt;50 mL to 69.7% at ≥150 mL. Escherichia coli was the most frequently isolated organism.<br/>\nConclusions: EQUC substantially increased pathogen detection in symptomatic men with BPE, particularly among those with severe LUTS or high residual urine volumes. Its clinical application should remain guided by symptoms, bacterial burden, organism identity, susceptibility findings, and antimicrobial-stewardship principles.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-54\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 54</p><h3>Navigating complex tumours- Tips and Tricks in Robotic Partial Nephrectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Kumar, Dr Harshit Rewari, Dr Supradeep N, Dr Gagandeep Talwar, Dr Puneet Ahluwalia</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta- The Medicity, Gurugram</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:Robotic partial nephrectomy (RAPN) is the standard treatment for localized renal tumours, providing excellent oncological and functional outcomes. However, hilar, completely endophytic, multifocal, and solitary kidney tumours remain technically demanding, requiring meticulous preoperative planning and individualized surgical strategies. We present a series demonstrating practical techniques that facilitate safe nephron-sparing surgery in these challenging scenarios.</p><p>Methods- Three representative cases of complex RAPN were analysed, highlighting the operative planning and intraoperative decision-making required for successful nephron preservation.<br/>\nScenario 1: 26x25 mm interpolar hilar cortical tumour (RENAL nephrometry score 9A).<br/>\nScenario 2: Bilateral multifocal renal tumors in a patient with Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome.<br/>\nScenario 3: 31x36 mm completely endophytic lower pole tumor<br/>\nPreoperative assessment included nephrometry scoring and three-dimensional reconstruction for surgical planning.</p><p>Results :3-dimensional reconstruction facilitated accurate understanding of tumor anatomy, vascular relationships, and resection planes in all cases.<br/>\nKey technical principles included:<br/>\n-Patient-specific ischemia strategies (selective segmental or off-clamp techniques).<br/>\n-Anatomical enucleo-resection preserving maximal renal parenchyma.<br/>\n-Precise hilar and segmental vascular dissection.<br/>\n-Intraoperative ultrasound for localisation of endophytic lesions.<br/>\n-Indocyanine green (ICG) fluorescence imaging to assess segmental perfusion and remnant renal vascularity.<br/>\n-Selective use of hemostatic patches to optimize renorrhaphy.</p><p>Scenario 1:Selective arterial clamping with anatomical enucleoresection achieved complete tumor excision with a warm ischemia time (WIT) of 31 mins.<br/>\nScenario 2:Multiple bilateral tumors were successfully excised with preservation of renal parenchyma and a WIT of 22 mins.<br/>\nScenario 3:Following selective lower pole arterial control, a lower pole heminephrectomy was performed for a completely endophytic lesion. ICG fluorescence confirmed adequate perfusion of the remaining kidney before reconstruction.<br/>\nConclusion:- Complex RAPN can be performed safely and reproducibly through meticulous planning, tailored integration of 3D reconstruction, selective ischemia strategies , intraoperative ultrasound , ICG guidance, and anatomical enucleoresection thus enhancing surgical precision while maximizing nephron preservation and achievement of trifecta outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-55\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 55</p><h3>Functional outcomes following minimally invasive vesicovaginal fistula repair: An Exploration beyond operative success</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Deepak, Dr Aravind, Dr Anup Kumar, Dr Pawan Vasudeva, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Ruchi Mittal, Dr Niraj Kumar, Dr Sandeep Kumar, Dr Rohan Nautiyal, Dr Abhishek Johari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC AND SJH ,DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>OBJECTIVE: Minimally invasive vesicovaginal fistula (VVF) repairs have become the standard of care when attempting transabdominal repairs. We analyzed the sexual and voiding outcomes of patients that underwent successful minimally invasive vesicovaginal fistula repair.</p><p>METHODS: A retrospective observational study of patients who underwent successful minimally invasive vesicovaginal fistula repair between August 2018 and December 2024 was undertaken. Post-operative Sexual and Bladder functioning was assessed by the Female Sexual Function Index (FSFI) and the International Consultation on Incontinence Questionnaire Female Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (ICIQ-FLUTS) score.</p><p>RESULTS: 35 patients who underwent either robotic or laparoscopic VVF repairs were assessed. Demographical parameters were comparable between both the groups, with the robotic arm faring better in terms of operative duration (192.2 ± 84.7 minutes vs. 263.6 ± 105.2 minutes, p =&lt;0.033) and estimated blood loss (56 ± 44.4 ml vs. 104 ± 55.8 ml, p&lt;0.007). 77% and 31.4% of the population respectively reported various forms of sexual and bladder dysfunction post procedure. Psychogenic causes (65%) predominated to account for the former, while stress urinary incontinence (17%) was the most encountered post-operative bladder dysfunction.</p><p>CONCLUSION: Despite a successful surgical repair, bladder and sexual dysfunction affects 1/3rd and 3/4th patients respectively that undergo minimally invasive VVF repair. There is a need for a comprehensive peri-operative care team involving sex therapists and psychologists in addition to the surgical team to improve the sexual outcomes after VVF repair. Larger prospective studies are required to better understand the impact of VVF repair on functional outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-56\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 56</p><h3>Laparoscopic Radical Prostatectomy in the Era of Robotics. A single centre Experience from Tertiary care set up .</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR YAWAR NAZIR , DR ABDUL RAUF KHAWAJA,DR ARIF HAMID</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS , J&amp;K</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP) has increasingly dominated the surgical management of localized prostate cancer supported by technological advancements and widespread adoption. However, the high capital and maintenance costs of robotic platforms pose significant barriers in resource-constrained healthcare systems. This study evaluates the feasibility, perioperative, functional, and oncological outcomes of Laparoscopic Radical Prostatectomy (LRP) in a resource-constrained healthcare setting.<br/>\nMethods: A retrospective, single-center longitudinal study was conducted on 40 patients who underwent LRP for clinically localized prostate cancer at SKIMS between January 2023 and January 2026. Perioperative data, cost-effectiveness, urinary continence, erectile function recovery, and early oncological outcomes were analyzed.<br/>\nResults: The median operative time was 145 minutes, and the median estimated blood loss was 210 mL. The overall complication rate was low (7.5%), with no cases requiring open conversion. At 12 months postoperatively, 87.5% of patients were completely continent, and 65% of preoperatively potent patients retained erectile function. Positive surgical margins (PSM) were reported in 12.5% of cases. The financial burden was significantly lower compared to the literature-reported costs of RARP.<br/>\nConclusion: LRP remains a highly viable, cost-effective, and oncologically safe surgical choice in the robotic era. In tertiary care centers within developing economies, LRP provides satisfactory clinical outcomes while mitigating the severe financial burden associated with robotic platforms.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-57\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 57</p><h3>Early Experience of Renal Transplantation at a Young Tertiary Care Centre</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Garg, Dr AS Sandhu, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr Gautam Ram Choudhary, Dr Mahendra Singh, Dr Deepak Bhirud</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the early clinical outcomes of the first 100 renal transplants performed at a newly established transplant centre, and to assess the feasibility and safety of initiating a renal transplant program in this setting.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis was a retrospective observational study of 100 consecutive patients who underwent renal transplantation at our institute over a 5-year period. Recipient and donor demographics, donor type, surgical outcomes, graft function, and post-transplant complications were recorded. Outcome measures included patient survival, graft survival, incidence of return to maintenance dialysis, and donor outcomes. 89 donors were living related donors, and their post-donation course was also assessed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOf the 100 renal transplant recipients, 98 (98%) survived, with 2 (2%) mortalities recorded during follow-up. Graft failure requiring return to maintenance dialysis occurred in 4 patients, while the remaining 94 patients had functioning grafts with satisfactory renal function. All 89 living donors remained in good health post-donation, with no major donor morbidity reported. These outcomes were achieved despite this being the institute's initial experience with renal transplantation.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nOur early experience demonstrates that a newly established renal transplant centre can achieve patient and graft survival outcomes comparable to those reported from established transplant programs. The low mortality and graft loss rates, along with excellent donor safety, support the feasibility of safely initiating and scaling renal transplant services in new centres, provided adequate infrastructure, multidisciplinary support, and surgical expertise are in place. These findings may serve as a benchmark for other emerging transplant programs and reinforce the importance of structured protocols in achieving favourable outcomes even during the learning-curve phase.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-58\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 58</p><h3>Intravesical Botulinum Toxin A in Refractory Overactive Bladder: Our Initial Clinical Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR YAWAR NAZIR,DR ABDUL ROUF KHAWAJA,DR ARIF HAMID</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS SOURA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Abstract<br/>\nTitle: Intravesical Botulinum Toxin A in Refractory Overactive Bladder: Our Initial Clinical Experience</p><p>Objective: To evaluate the early clinical efficacy and safety of intravesical OnabotulinumtoxinA (BoNT-A) injections in patients with refractory overactive bladder (OAB).<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: A prospective cohort study was conducted on 10 patients with refractory OAB who had failed behavioral modifications and oral pharmacotherapy. Patients received 100 U of intravesical BoNT-A via 20 intradetrusor injections using a flexible cystoscope. The primary outcome was the reduction in urgency urinary incontinence (UUI) episodes per day at 12 weeks. Secondary outcomes included changes in daily micturition frequency, post-void residual (PVR) volume, and the incidence of adverse events such as urinary tract infections (UTIs) and urinary retention requiring clean intermittent self-catheterization (CISC).<br/>\nResults: The cohort consisted of 8 females and 2 males with a mean age of 56 years. At the 12-week follow-up, the mean number of daily UUI episodes decreased significantly from 6.8 at baseline to 2.9 (p &lt; 0.05). Mean daily micturition frequency also decreased from 12.4 to 8.1. Treatment success (patient-reported subjective improvement) was noted in 80% (n = 8) of the cohort. Adverse events included uncomplicated UTIs in1 patient (10%) and transient urinary retention requiring CISC in 1 patient (10%).<br/>\nConclusion: Intravesical 100 U BoNT-A is a highly effective, minimally invasive third-line therapy for refractory OAB, demonstrating a robust reduction in UUI episodes with an acceptable safety and tolerability profile in a small initial cohort</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-59\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 59</p><h3>Recurrent Graft Pyelonephritis Secondary to Infected Native ADPKD cyst</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shivam Upadhayaya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS RISHIKESH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVBES</p><p>Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) accounts for 5–10% of end-stage renal disease requiring transplantation. Acute graft pyelonephritis (AGPN) occurs in 10–20% of transplant recipients, with infected native ADPKD cysts being an underrecognized and persistent nidus. Cumulative cyst infection incidence post-transplant reaches 12% at 10 years. We present a case of recurrent AGPN caused by an infected native ADPKD cyst, successfully managed by laparoscopic native nephrectomy.</p><p>MATERIALS AND METHODS</p><p>A 42-year-old male with bilateral ADPKD, prior right native nephrectomy, post-transplant diabetes mellitus, and HCV infection (RNA undetected) underwent ABO-compatible live-related renal transplantation. Post-transplant course was complicated by graft renal artery stenosis (managed with bare metal stenting) .Patient also had recurrent UTI post transplant requiring multiple admissions for intravenous antibiotics. NCCT abdomen confirmed an multiple infected cyst in left native kidney with graft pyelonephritis . Patient then underwent laparoscopic left simple native nephrectomy . The patient was positioned in right lateral decubitus; pneumoperitoneum was created via Veress needle; descending colon mobilized; left ureter, gonadal vein, and hilar vessels identified, doubly clipped and divided; specimen retrieved through a left subcostal incision.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>The intraoperative course was uneventful. Post-operative graft Doppler on POD1 confirmed normal vascularity (RI 0.56–0.61) with no stenosis or outflow obstruction. Catheter was removed on POD1 and drain on POD2. Serum creatinine improved from 2.31 mg/dL (admission) to 1.76 mg/dL (POD4). The patient was discharged on POD6 on standard immunosuppression (tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil, prednisolone), asymptomatic on follow-up with no recurrent UTI or AGPN.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>Infected native ADPKD cysts are a clinically significant, underrecognized cause of recurrent AGPN post-transplantation.Native nephrectomy is a safe, definitive intervention that eradicates the infection source without compromising graft function, and should be considered early in refractory cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-60\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 60</p><h3>Influence of Voiding Position on Uroflowmetry Parameters and Post-Void Residual Urine in Men with Obstructive Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms: A Prospective Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sanchit Bansal, Dr Vikas kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Dayanand medical college and hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction &amp; Objective:<br/>\nUroflowmetry (UFM) is a simple, non-invasive screening tool for urinary flow, but the effect of voiding position on its parameters remains controversial, with conflicting reports across sitting, standing, and squatting positions. This study evaluated the effect of voiding position (sitting vs. standing vs. preferred) on UFM parameters and post-void residual urine (PVRU) across age groups in men with obstructive lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS).<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nOne hundred male patients (age 18-80 years, mean ~55 years) with obstructive LUTS attending Urology OPD (March-September 2025) were prospectively enrolled. After adequate bladder filling (&gt;150 ml), each patient voided in sitting, standing, and preferred positions. Maximum flow rate (Qmax), average flow rate (Qavg), voided volume, voiding time, and PVRU were recorded using a urodynamic system and bladder scanner. Patients with urethral stricture, neurogenic bladder, spinal cord injury, or voided volume &lt;150 ml were excluded. Paired t-tests were used, with p&lt;0.05 considered significant.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nNo significant difference in UFM parameters was seen in men under 50 years across positions. In the 51-65 year group, Qmax and Qavg were significantly higher in standing versus sitting (p=0.015 and p=0.046). PVRU was lower and voiding time shorter in the preferred position, particularly after age 50, though not statistically significant. Qmax was higher and voiding time shorter in standing position across all age groups. Most patients preferred standing, and voiding in the preferred position gave better flow rates and lower PVRU, especially in the 50-65 year group.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nVoiding position influences UFM parameters, particularly beyond 50 years, with standing associated with higher flow rates and preferred position associated with lower PVRU and shorter voiding time. UFM performed in a patient's preferred voiding position, rather than a standardized position, may better reflect true voiding function and aid individualized management of obstructive luts</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-61\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 61</p><h3>Functional Restoration via Orthotopic Neobladder (ONB) Following Radical Cystectomy for MIBC: Six year experience at Tertiary care Centre in North India.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Omar Mukhtar , Prof Dr Arif Hamid Bhat , Dr Abdul Rauf Khwaja</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS , Srinagar , J&amp;K</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Radical cystectomy (RC) remains the gold standard treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC). Over the past two decades, the orthotopic neobladder (ONB) has emerged as the preferred urinary diversion for suitably selected patients due to its good functional restoration and quality of life. We present the long-term functional outcomes of ONB reconstruction at the Sher-i-Kashmir Institute of Medical Sciences (SKIMS).<br/>\nMethods: A retrospective review was conducted on 25 consecutive patients who underwent RC and ONB reconstruction for MIBC between January 2020 and July 2026. Functional outcomes, including daytime and nighttime continence, reliance on clean intermittent catheterization (CIC), renal function preservation, and early/late complications, were evaluated.<br/>\nResults: The cohort consisted of 22 males and 3 females with a median age of 57 years. The median follow-up period was 42 months. Satisfactory daytime continence (0-1 pads/day) was achieved in 88% of patients by 12 months, while nighttime continence was reported at 72%. Clean intermittent catheterization was required by 12% of patients due to chronic urinary retention or large post-void residuals. Metabolic complications (mild to moderate hyperchloremic acidosis) were observed in 20% of patients and managed conservatively.<br/>\nConclusion: Our experience demonstrates that ONB is a highly effective, safe, and viable urinary diversion that offers excellent functional restoration and satisfies patient expectations. Careful patient selection and meticulous surgical technique are paramount to mimicking native bladder function and preserving long-term quality of life.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-62\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 62</p><h3>Clinical Spectrum, Histopathological Characteristics, and Surgical Outcomes of Urachal Adenocarcinoma: A Single-Center Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vaibhav Aggarwal, Vanjinathan, Arun Kumar, Siddharth Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction &amp; Objectives: Urachal adenocarcinoma is a rare and aggressive non-urothelial malignancy arising from persistent urachal remnants. Due to its insidious growth and delayed presentation, optimal management strategies and prognostic determinants remain challenging. We aimed to analyze the clinical presentation, histopathological subtypes, immunohistochemical (IHC) profile, surgical strategies, and oncological outcomes in patients treated for urachal adenocarcinoma.</p><p>Methods: A retrospective review was conducted on 11 consecutive patients diagnosed and managed for urachal adenocarcinoma. Data regarding patient demographics, presenting symptoms, associated congenital anomalies, surgical techniques, Sheldon staging, histopathological features, IHC markers, and post-treatment oncological outcomes were systematically evaluated.</p><p>Results: The median patient age was 37 years (mean 45.4; range 29–72 years), with a strong male predominance of 90.9% (n=10/11). Gross hematuria was the primary symptom in 54.5% (n=6), an abdominal lump in 9.1% (n=1), and 18.2% (n=2) presented with underlying bladder exstrophy-epispadias complex. Surgical management involved partial cystectomy with pelvic lymph node dissection (PLND) in 45.5% (n=5) of cases, radical cystectomy with urinary diversion and abdominal wall reconstruction in 36.4% (n=4), cytoreductive surgery with hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC) in 9.1% (n=1), and exploratory laparotomy with abandoned extirpation due to unresectable disease in 18.2% (n=2). Histopathology confirmed enteric and mucinous adenocarcinoma subtypes, including signet-ring cell variants. Diagnostic IHC demonstrated consistent positivity for CDX2, CK20, SATB2, and Beta-catenin. The majority presented at advanced stages (Sheldon Stage III–IV). Advanced stage, lymph node involvement, and positive surgical margins were strongly associated with early recurrence, peritoneal dissemination, distant metastasis (lungs, vertebrae, bowel), and disease-related mortality.</p><p>Conclusions: Urachal adenocarcinoma predominantly affects younger males and frequently presents at advanced Sheldon stages. Complete partial cystectomy with bilateral PLND provides durable local control in localized disease, whereas locally advanced cases necessitate radical extirpation or aggressive multimodality therapy. Biomarker profiling with CDX2 and CK20 remains essential for accurate diagnosis.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-63\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 63</p><h3>Use of freshly prepared allogenic amniotic membrane as an interposition graft in recurrent transvaginal vesicovaginal fistula repairs: Our novel technique and initial clinical outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vaibhav Aggarwal, Cyril Joseph, Siddharth Jain, Mounika Balabolu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Human amniotic membrane (hAM) has emerged as a promising biomaterial for genitourinary reconstruction due to its regenerative, anti-inflammatory, and low-immunogenic properties. We present our technique and initial clinical outcomes of using hAM as an interposition graft in recurrent transvaginal vesicovaginal fistula (VVF) repairs.</p><p>Methods: Five patients aged between 18-50 years with post hysterectomy recurrent VVF underwent transvaginal VVF repair with hAM interposition between January 2025 to June 2025. The multilayered AM graft was harvested from screened female donors after elective cesarean sections and placed between the bladder and vaginal closure layers using interrupted vicryl sutures to prevent displacement. Fistula closure success was defined as complete closure without leak. Urinary and sexual outcomes were recorded using a 5-point likert scale.</p><p>Results: The mean age of the cohort was 36 years. All patients were multiparous. The median duration of symptoms before presentation was 10 months. At the 3 week and 6-month follow-up, successful fistula closure was achieved in 100% (5/5) patients. 1 patient developed postoperative fever. Sexual activity resumed in all cases within 6 months. 1 patient reported dyspareunia and dysuria each in this cohort.</p><p>Conclusions: Our novel technique of augmenting recurrent VVF with hAM transvaginally has provided good initial results. It is technically a simple and safe procedure that may decrease operative morbidity associated with other types of interposition grafts. Patients demonstrated favorable trends regarding postoperative sexual and urinary function. Case control studies are warranted to further define the utility of AM interposition in complex or recurrent fistulas.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-64\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 64</p><h3>Adrenal Masses, Demographics, Clinical Profile, and Outcomes : Our experience at Tertiary Care Centre.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Omar Mukhtar, Prof Dr Arif Hamid , Dr Abdul Rauf Khwaja</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Sgr, J&amp;K</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims : Adrenal masses comprise a heterogeneous group of benign and malignant lesions with variable hormonal activity and clinical presentation. Increasing use of cross-sectional imaging has led to a rise in incidental detection. This study evaluated the demographic profile, clinical characteristics, management strategies, and outcomes of patients with adrenal masses treated at a tertiary referral center.<br/>\nMethods: A retrospective review was conducted of 48 consecutive patients with adrenal masses managed between January 2021 and December 2024. Demographic data, clinical presentation, hormonal work-up, radiological findings, operative details, histopathology, and postoperative outcomes were analyzed. Statistical analysis was performed using IBM SPSS version 25, with p&lt;0.05 considered significant.<br/>\nResults: The median age was 45 years, with female predominance (62.5%). Incidentalomas constituted the commonest presentation (47.9%), followed by localized symptoms (27.1%) and hormonal manifestations (18.8%). Functional tumors accounted for 54.2%, with pheochromocytoma being the predominant pathology (39.6%). Forty-two patients (87.5%) underwent adrenalectomy, including 22 laparoscopic and 20 open procedures. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy was associated with significantly shorter hospital stay (2.5 vs. 3.8 days; p=0.014). Tumor size &gt;4 cm showed a significant association with malignancy (p=0.03). Overall perioperative morbidity was low, with no mortality. Rare presentations included adrenocortical carcinoma with inferior vena cava thrombus and aldosterone-producing adenoma presenting with hypokalemic quadriparesis, both managed successfully.<br/>\nConclusions: Adrenal incidentalomas and pheochromocytomas represented the majority of adrenal lesions in our cohort. Appropriate endocrine evaluation, risk-stratified surgical decision-making, and multidisciplinary management resulted in excellent perioperative outcomes. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy remains the preferred approach whenever feasible, while larger tumors were resected with open approach due to increased malignancy risk.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-65\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 65</p><h3>Comparative Assessment of Artificial Intelligence Chatbots in Addressing Patient Queries on Erectile Dysfunction: A Multi-Platform Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Prakhar Pratap, Dr Utkarsh Tyagi, Dr Sanchit Rustagi, Dr Arpan Yadav, Dr Uday Pratap Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS LUCKNOW</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objectives<br/>\nTo evaluate and compare the quality, clinical accuracy, and readability of responses generated by three widely used AI platforms — ChatGPT (GPT-4o), Google Gemini, and Perplexity AI — to a validated set of patient queries on erectile dysfunction.</p><p>Materials and methods<br/>\nA Cross-sectional observational study conducted in accordance with STROBE reporting guidelines.</p><p>A question bank of 20 patient queries was prospectively derived from outpatient urology consultations at a tertiary care center and validated using Content Validity Index (CVI) methodology. Each question was independently submitted to all three AI platforms, and responses were evaluated by 10 expert urologists and andrologists with a minimum of five years post-qualification experience using the validated Global Quality Scale (GQS; 1–5). Readability was assessed using the Flesch Reading Ease score and Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level for each AI response.</p><p>Results<br/>\nNo significant inter-platform GQS difference was found (ChatGPT 3.85 ± 0.59; Gemini 3.65 ± 0.67; Perplexity 3.75 ± 0.44; H = 1.435, p = 0.488). No platform received GQS = 1. Perplexity AI demonstrated significantly superior readability (FRE 48.20 vs 28.74 and 29.66; FKGL 11.97 vs 16.15 and 15.54; p &lt; 0.001 for both). Response length did not predict quality (r = 0.033, p = 0.804). Fleiss' κ = 0.315.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nAll platforms demonstrated comparable clinical accuracy. Perplexity AI generated significantly more readable responses without sacrificing accuracy. All platforms exceeded recommended readability thresholds, supporting a supplementary, clinician-overseen role for AI in ED patient education.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-66\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 66</p><h3>Renal Mucormycosis in Predominantly Immunocompetent Patients in Post COVID era: Emphasizing Early Radiological Diagnosis and Aggressive Surgical Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ujjwal Das, Dr. Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr. Gautam Ram Choudhary, Dr. Mahendra Singh, Dr. Deepak Prakash Bhirud, Dr. A S Sandhu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nRenal mucormycosis is a rare, fulminant angioinvasive fungal infection increasingly reported in immunocompetent individuals, particularly from India, where non-specific presentation mimicking bacterial pyelonephritis delays diagnosis and mortality reaches 50–100% in bilateral disease. This study aimed to evaluate the contrast-enhanced CT (CECT) features enabling early diagnosis and to assess outcomes of a protocol of empirical antifungal therapy initiated on radiological suspicion followed by early nephrectomy.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis single-centre retrospective observational study included all patients diagnosed and managed for renal mucormycosis at a tertiary care centre between March 2022 and July 2025. Diagnosis was established on CECT findings of nephromegaly, renal infarction, and vascular thrombosis, supported by histopathology where available. Demographic, laboratory, radiological, operative, and outcome data were analysed. All patients received intravenous liposomal amphotericin B (5–10 mg/kg) at admission based on radiological suspicion irrespective of microbiological confirmation, followed by nephrectomy with or without debridement of involved adjacent structures within 24 hours of antifungal initiation where feasible. Primary outcome was in-hospital mortality.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nTen patients were included, all male (mean age 46.8 ± 12.4 years); 70% were immunocompetent, with only 30% having diabetes mellitus. Fever (80%) and flank pain (70%) were the predominant symptoms. CECT demonstrated enlarged, bulky kidneys with patchy-to-complete non-enhancement and loss of corticomedullary differentiation in 90%, thickened Gerota fascia, wedge-shaped infarcts, renal vein thrombosis in 30%, and extra-renal extension in 60%. Nephrectomy was performed in 90%, with histopathological confirmation of fungal hyphae in 80%. Mean hospital stay was 16.6 ± 4.4 days, and overall mortality was 10%, considerably lower than historical Indian cohorts (40–75%).<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nA bulky, non-enhancing kidney with vascular thrombosis on CECT should prompt strong suspicion of renal mucormycosis, even without classical immunosuppression. Radiology-triggered antifungal therapy with early nephrectomy is associated with markedly improved survival and may be considered for adoption as a standard management approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-67\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 67</p><h3>Clinical efficacy of tamsulosin and solifenacin combination vs mirabegron and silodosin combination in double-J ureteral stent-related symptoms: a randomized controlled trial</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kawaljit Singh Kaura, Dr. Abhay S Gaur, Dr. Abhishek Chhabra, Dr. Tejas Chaudhari, Dr. Manish Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Bathinda</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo compare the clinical efficacy of tamsulosin plus solifenacin versus mirabegron plus silodosin in patients with DJ stent related symptoms following ureteroscopic lithotripsy (URSL) and percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL), using Ureteral Stent Symptom Questionnaire (USSQ) scores at 1 and 3 weeks as the primary outcome, and adverse drug reactions as the secondary outcome.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nIn this randomized controlled trial, 96 patients who underwent DJ stent placement after URSL or PCNL were allocated 1:1 to Group A (tamsulosin plus solifenacin, n=48) or Group B (mirabegron plus silodosin, n=48). USSQ scores across six domains and adverse reactions were compared between groups at 1 and 3weeks post-procedure, and within each group over time.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nBaseline variables were comparable between groups. At week 1, Group B had significantly higher pain (20.4±2.9 vs 18.8±2.5, p=0.005) and additional-problems scores (13.4±1.5 vs 12.6±1.8, p=0.025) than Group A. By week 3, this reversed: Group B showed significantly better urinary symptom scores (13.6±2.1 vs 17.1±4.6, p&lt;0.001), pain scores (9.9±1.4 vs 11.2±1.8, p&lt;0.001), and work performance (p=0.021), while general health and additional problems were comparable. Both groups improved significantly within-group across all domains (p&lt;0.001). Adverse reaction rates did not differ significantly (20.8% vs 14.6%, p=0.594).<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nBoth regimens effectively reduce DJ stent-related morbidity, with statistically significant within-group improvement across every USSQ domain. Mirabegron plus silodosin achieves superior urinary symptom, pain, and work-performance control by the third week, with a comparable adverse-effect profile, supporting its preferential use for stent-related symptom management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-68\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 68</p><h3>Simultaneous Surgical Management of Synchronous Bilateral Renal Masses: Oncological and Functional Outcomes from a Single-Center Case-Based Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Swatantra Nagendra Rao, Dr. Yajvender Pratap Singh Rana, Dr. Bhabtosh Das, Dr. Akshit Minochas</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">BLK-Max Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To evaluate the feasibility, oncological outcomes, and renal functional outcomes of simultaneous surgical management of SBRMs.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective single-center observational study included 10 consecutive patients with SBRMs who underwent simultaneous bilateral surgery between 2019 and 2025. Clinical characteristics, tumor complexity, surgical strategies, histopathological findings, perioperative outcomes, renal functional parameters, and oncological outcomes were analyzed. Surgical management was individualized based on tumor size, R.E.N.A.L. nephrometry score, baseline renal function, and oncological considerations.<br/>\nResults: The mean age was 59.1 ± 6.8 years, and 80% of patients were male. Bilateral partial nephrectomy was performed in 60% of patients, radical nephrectomy with contralateral partial nephrectomy in 30%, and bilateral radical nephrectomy in 10%. Simultaneous surgery was successfully completed in all patients without conversion to staged procedures. Histopathology demonstrated bilateral renal cell carcinoma in 70% of patients, while 30% had discordant histology. Negative surgical margins were achieved in all cases. Mean serum creatinine increased transiently from 2.1 ± 1.7 mg/dL preoperatively to 2.8 ± 1.5 mg/dL postoperatively, improving to 2.3 ± 0.8 mg/dL at 3 months. Functional renal preservation was achieved in 90% of patients. At a median follow-up of 36 months, no local recurrence or distant metastasis was observed, and cancer-specific survival was 100%.<br/>\nConclusion: Simultaneous surgical management of SBRMs is a feasible and effective approach in carefully selected patients. Individualized nephron-sparing strategies provide excellent oncological control while preserving renal function and avoiding the morbidity associated with staged procedures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-69\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 69</p><h3>Unrecognized Accessory Renal Artery Injury During Living Donor Nephrectomy: A Salvage Reconstruction and Its Consequences in the Renal Allograft</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Deepak Prakash Bhirud, Associate Professor, Department of Urology, AIIMS Jodhpur</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS JODHPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nDonor nephrectomy requires meticulous identification and preservation of all renal arterial branches, as injury to an accessory or early-branching artery may compromise segmental graft perfusion. We present a potentially avoidable accessory (vascular complication), unrecognized during donor nephrectomy, which was identified only during bench preparation and subsequently manifested as segmental hypoenhancement on imaging.<br/>\nCasePresentation:<br/>\nA young male recipient underwent LRRT using a donor kidney with two renal arterial channels. During donor nephrectomy, accessory lower pole renal artery was inadvertently injured with cautery, and the vascular injury was not recognized at the time of retrieval, with 1.5 hours of WIT for lower pole. During bench preparation, transected artery was identified. The margins were freshened, and vascular reconstruction was performed using a pantaloon anastomosis to restore perfusion . The graft reperfused satisfactorily following implantation, with good color and turgor, and urine output was observed intraoperatively.<br/>\nIn postoperative period, patient developed persistent high drainage output and fall in hemoglobin, prompting CT angiography which demonstrated normal uptake in aorta, main renal artery, and accessory renal artery with hypoenhancement of lower half of renal parenchyma. Subsequent dynamic EC renogram demonstrated (ERPF) of 66 mL/min, with reduced radiotracer uptake in lower pole; however, delayed static images showed adequate radiotracer accumulation in lower pole suggesting segmental acute tubular necrosis (ATN) in the ischemic segment rather than rejection.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nUnrecognized injury to an accessory or early-branching renal artery during donor nephrectomy can have significant consequences despite successful bench salvage and apparently satisfactory immediate graft reperfusion. This case highlights the importance of meticulous hilar dissection, active confirmation of all arterial branches before division, and careful inspection of the graft during bench preparation as vascular complications become clinically evident after transplantation. Early recognition of segmental hypoenhancement on imaging can facilitate timely management and avoid the need for a graft biopsy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-70\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 70</p><h3>Beyond White Light: Incremental Lymph Node Detection with Indocyanine Green–Guided Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection During Radical Cystectomy—A Comparative Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR ASHISH KUMAR, DR PALASH, DR DILPREET, DR SAMBIT TRIPATHY, DR SWARNENDU MANDAL</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, BHUBANESWAR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Pelvic lymph node dissection (PLND) is integral to radical cystectomy for bladder cancer. Small nodes within pelvic fibro-fatty tissue may be difficult to identify under white light. Indocyanine green (ICG)-guided near-infrared fluorescence may improve detection of residual nodal tissue.<br/>\nObjective: To assess whether ICG-guided PLND increases pelvic lymph-node yield compared with conventional PLND.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods: This ambispective, single-centre comparative study included 18 patients undergoing radical cystectomy with bilateral PLND: 9 ICG-guided cases and 9 historical controls. In the ICG group, conventional PLND was followed by near-infrared fluorescence assessment. Fluorescent nodal tissue identified separately from the conventional specimen was excised and submitted independently for histopathology. The primary outcome was total lymph-node yield. Secondary outcomes included additional fluorescence-detected nodes, incremental nodal yield, and pathological nodal involvement. Group yields were compared using the Mann–Whitney U test.<br/>\nResults: Both groups comprised 7 males and 2 females. A total of 153 nodes were retrieved in the ICG group versus 128 in controls. Mean nodal yield was 17.0 versus 14.2 nodes/patient, respectively. Median yield was 15 (IQR 14–18; range 12–27) with ICG and 14 (IQR 11–16; range 11–19) without ICG (p=0.31). Conventional specimens in the ICG cohort contained 139 nodes (15.4/patient), while fluorescence-guided assessment identified 14 additional histologically confirmed nodes, representing a 10.1% incremental yield. Additional fluorescent nodes were detected in 5/9 patients (55.6%), with 3, 6, 1, 2 and 2 additional nodes, respectively. All 14 were histologically benign, with no fluorescence-related pathological upstaging.<br/>\nConclusion: ICG-guided PLND produced a numerically higher nodal yield and identified additional nodal tissue in more than half of patients, contributing approximately 10% incremental nodal recovery. Although statistical significance was not reached, ICG may be a useful adjunct for identifying otherwise inconspicuous residual nodes. Larger prospective studies are required to determine its impact on staging and oncological outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-71\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 71</p><h3>Comparison between ultrasound guided transperineal and transrectal prostate biopsy for suspected carcinoma prostate: A randomized controlled trial</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Anand</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS JODHPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: To compare ultrasound-guided transperineal and transrectal prostate biopsy in biopsy-naive men with suspected prostate cancer, focusing on complications, tissue adequacy, cancer detection, and<br/>\nthe relationship between core length and tumour stage and grade.<br/>\nMethods: In this randomized controlled trial, 200 biopsy-naive men with suspected prostate cancer<br/>\nwere randomized 1:1 to transperineal (n=100) or transrectal (n=100) biopsy under local anaesthesia.<br/>\nBoth groups underwent an identical 12-core biopsy protocol with antibiotic prophylaxis. The primary<br/>\noutcome was overall complication rate. Secondary outcomes included tissue adequacy, cancer<br/>\ndetection, core fragmentation, operative time, pain, and correlation of core length with tumour stage<br/>\nand Gleason grade.<br/>\nResults: Cancer detection was similar between transperineal and transrectal biopsy (54.0% vs. 59.0%;<br/>\np=0.568). Tissue adequacy was comparable, with similar numbers of adequate cores (9.76 vs. 10.26;<br/>\np=0.266) and fragmented cores (6.17 vs. 5.42; p=0.314). Core length showed no significant<br/>\ncorrelation with T, N, or M stage, cancer detection, or Gleason grade in either group (all |r|≤0.21;<br/>\np&gt;0.05). Overall complications were significantly lower following transperineal biopsy (25.0% vs.<br/>\n40.0%; p=0.03), mainly due to less per-rectal bleeding (1.0% vs. 9.0%; p=0.018). No sepsis occurred<br/>\nafter transperineal biopsy compared with one case after transrectal biopsy. However, transperineal<br/>\nbiopsy required longer operative time (23.3 vs. 18.5 min; p&lt;0.001) and was associated with higher<br/>\npain scores (VAS 4.85 vs. 2.58; p&lt;0.0001).<br/>\nConclusions: Transperineal prostate biopsy provides oncological outcomes comparable to the<br/>\ntransrectal approach, with equivalent tissue adequacy and cancer detection. It is associated with fewer<br/>\noverall complications, less per-rectal bleeding, and no sepsis, although it requires longer operative<br/>\ntime and results in greater procedural pain. These findings support wider adoption of the transperineal approach in the Indian setting.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-72\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 72</p><h3>“Shear Wave Elastography of Testicular Stiffness in Unilateral and Bilateral Clinical Varicocele: Association With Clinical Grade”</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">dr keshav paliwal, dr nachiket vyas, dr nauratmal kumawat, dr neeraj agarwal, dr dharmendra jangid dr shivam priyadarshi, dr sarthak sharma, dr raghav khurana</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">sms hospital jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objective<br/>\nTo evaluate testicular stiffness using shear wave elastography (SWE) in unilateral and bilateral clinical varicocele and compare findings with contralateral non-varicocele testes. We also assessed the association between clinical varicocele grade and testicular stiffness.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nThis hospital-based observational study included men aged 18–45 years with clinically diagnosed varicocele at SMS Medical College and Attached Hospitals, Jaipur. After ethical approval and written informed consent, testes were categorized as unilateral varicocele (Group A, n=57), bilateral varicocele (Group B, 26 testes from 13 patients), and contralateral non-varicocele testes (Group C, n=57). Colour Doppler ultrasonography and SWE were performed with participants supine and the scrotum supported. Stiffness was measured at the upper, middle, and lower poles using a 5-mm region of interest, and mean SWE was calculated. Testicular volume and stiffness were compared among groups and clinical grades.</p><p>Results<br/>\nMean testicular volume was 15.37 ± 4.78, 14.97 ± 6.57, and 15.04 ± 4.26 mL in Groups A, B, and C, respectively (p=0.919). Mean testicular stiffness was 1.77 ± 0.69, 2.80 ± 2.73, and 1.87 ± 1.29 kPa, respectively (p=0.011). Bilateral varicocele showed significantly higher stiffness than unilateral varicocele and contralateral non-varicocele testes. No significant difference in stiffness was observed across clinical grades in unilateral (p=0.976) or bilateral varicocele (p=0.411).</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nBilateral clinical varicocele was associated with higher testicular stiffness despite comparable testicular volumes. SWE may provide a useful non-invasive quantitative assessment of testicular parenchymal alterations in clinical varicocele.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-73\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 73</p><h3>Is Routine Urine Analysis Sufficient Before Elective Endourological Procedures for Urolithiasis? A Prospective Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Y S Chundawat, Dr S K Mittal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Santokba Durlabji Memorial Hospital,Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate whether routine urine analysis (RUA) can predict postoperative urinary tract infection (UTI) following elective endourological procedures for urolithiasis and identify urine parameters associated with postoperative infection.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nThis prospective observational study was conducted at the Department of Urology, Santokba Durlabhji Memorial Hospital, Jaipur, from July 2024 to July 2026. A total of 216 patients with urolithiasis undergoing elective ureterorenoscopy (URS), retrograde intrarenal surgery (RIRS), or percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) were included. Patients with clinical suspicion of UTI, raised systemic WBC count, RUA suggestive of active infection, or pre-existing ureteric stents were excluded. Preoperative RUA findings were recorded, and patients were followed for postoperative UTI for 30 days. Associations between individual urine parameters and postoperative infection were analysed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOf 216 patients, 168 (77.8%) were male and 48 (22.2%) female. URS was the most common procedure (61.1%), followed by PCNL (24.1%) and RIRS (12.0%). Postoperative UTI occurred in 6 patients (2.8%), all requiring readmission within 30 days. Urinary WBC count &gt;5 cells/HPF was significantly associated with postoperative UTI (5/6 cases; p=0.011). Positive leukocyte esterase was also significantly associated with infection (4/6 cases; p=0.013). Increased urinary RBC count was another significant predictor (p=0.046). Oxalate crystals and urinary casts showed no significant association. Klebsiella pneumoniae and Escherichia coli were the predominant organisms isolated.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nIn this carefully selected cohort without clinical evidence of UTI, postoperative UTI incidence was low (2.8%). Preoperative urinary WBCs, leukocyte esterase, and microscopic haematuria were significantly associated with postoperative infection. RUA may serve as a practical initial screening tool before elective endourological procedures, helping identify patients who may benefit from further urine culture and closer perioperative assessment.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-74\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 74</p><h3>Upfront sternal metastasis as the initial presentation of renal cell carcinoma: A rare case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shabbah Alam, Dr Arif Hamid Bhat, Dr Saqib Mehdi, Dr Abdul Rouf Khawaja, Dr Sajad Ahmad Malik, Dr Sajad Ahmad Para</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Soura, Srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo highlight the rare presentation of renal cell carcinoma (RCC) with sternal metastasis as the initial clinical manifestation and emphasize the importance of evaluating an underlying renal primary in patients presenting with a destructive sternal lesion.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA 46-year-old man presented with a painful sternal swelling. Imaging demonstrated a destructive sternal lesion along with an incidental left renal mass. Fine-needle aspiration cytology of the sternal lesion was performed to establish the diagnosis.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nFine-needle aspiration cytology of the sternal lesion confirmed metastatic clear cell RCC. The patient subsequently underwent cytoreductive left radical nephrectomy. Histopathological examination confirmed clear cell RCC, staged as pT4. The sternal lesion represented the initial clinical manifestation of the renal malignancy.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nIsolated sternal metastasis as the initial presentation of RCC is exceptionally rare. A destructive sternal lesion, particularly when presenting as a solitary swelling, should prompt consideration of metastatic malignancy and evaluation for an underlying renal primary. Recognition of this unusual presentation can facilitate timely diagnosis and appropriate multidisciplinary management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-75\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 75</p><h3>Retained wooden foreign body following scrotal trauma: A case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shabbah Alam, Dr Abdul Rouf Khawaja, Dr Sajad Ahmad Para, Dr Arif Hamid Bhat, Dr Sajad Ahmad Malik, Dr Saqib Mehdi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Soura, srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo highlight the diagnostic challenges and management of a retained wooden foreign body following scrotal trauma, presenting with delayed infection and sinus formation.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA 45-year-old man presented with persistent purulent discharge from the left scrotum approximately two months after a fall from a tree. Clinical examination revealed a chronic discharging sinus. Ultrasonography followed by non-contrast computed tomography was performed to identify and localize the suspected retained foreign body.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nImaging demonstrated two linear retained foreign bodies extending from the left hemiscrotum along the spermatic cord towards the deep inguinal region, without intra-abdominal extension. The patient underwent inguinoscrotal exploration. Two wooden splinters were identified and removed. The associated abscess was drained and thorough debridement performed. The testis was viable and was preserved. The postoperative course was uneventful, with complete resolution of the discharging sinus and satisfactory wound healing at two-week follow-up.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nRetained wooden foreign bodies should be considered in patients presenting with persistent infection or sinus formation following penetrating trauma, even when the initial injury appears minor. As wood is frequently radiolucent, ultrasonography and cross-sectional imaging are valuable for diagnosis and anatomical localization. Early surgical exploration with complete foreign-body removal, drainage and adequate debridement can achieve definitive infection control while preserving adjacent structures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-76\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 76</p><h3>Role of Suprapubic Catheterization in the Management of Prostatic Abscess: A Retrospective Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shabbah Alam, Dr Abdul Rouf Khawaja, Dr Arif Hamid Bhat, Dr Sajad Ahmad Malik, Dr Sajad Ahmad Para, Dr Saqib Mehdi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Soura, Srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo evaluate the association of suprapubic catheterization (SPC) with symptom relief, hospital stay and recurrence in patients with prostatic abscess (PA).</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe retrospectively analysed 40 patients with radiologically confirmed PA managed at a tertiary referral centre between January 2020 and March 2025. All received antibiotics and conservative management or abscess drainage as indicated. SPC was used selectively, particularly in patients with acute urinary retention (AUR), sepsis or large/complicated abscesses. The primary outcome was time to symptom relief; secondary outcomes were hospital stay and recurrence within 3 months. Multivariable Cox regression adjusted for abscess size, AUR and diabetes.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nSPC was performed in 13 patients (32.5%). The SPC group had higher rates of diabetes (69.2% vs 29.6%, p=0.038), AUR (53.8% vs 11.1%, p=0.006) and complicated presentation (61.5% vs 11.1%, p=0.002). Despite greater disease severity, median symptom relief occurred earlier with SPC (7 vs 9 days, p=0.032), as did hospital discharge (10 vs 16 days, p=0.001). Kaplan-Meier analysis showed earlier symptom relief with SPC (log-rank p=0.007). After adjustment, SPC remained associated with faster symptom relief (adjusted HR 3.39, 95% CI 1.35–8.49; p=0.009) and approximately 53% shorter hospital stay. Recurrence was similar (15.4% vs 11.1%, p=1.00).</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nSPC was associated with faster symptom resolution and shorter hospital stay despite preferential use in patients with more severe disease. However, the retrospective design precludes causal inference. SPC may be beneficial for urinary drainage in selected patients with PA, particularly those with AUR or complicated infection.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-77\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 77</p><h3>Diagnosis to management of Congenital Anomalies of the Kidney and Urinary Tract (CAKUT) in Adults: SKIMS experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shabbah Alam, Dr Abdul Rouf Khawaja, Dr Arif Hamid Bhat, Dr Sajad Ahmad Malik, Dr Sajad Ahmad Para, Dr Saqib Mehdi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Soura , Srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo evaluate the demographic profile, clinical presentation, spectrum of congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract (CAKUT), and their management in adults presenting to a tertiary care centre.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective observational study was conducted on 53 adult patients diagnosed with CAKUT. Demographic characteristics, mode of presentation, type and laterality of anomaly, and management strategies were analysed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe mean age was 46 years, with 32 males and 21 females. Pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction (PUJO) was the commonest anomaly (20/52), followed by duplex collecting system (11/52), ureterocele (5/52), horseshoe kidney (5/52), retrocaval ureter (3/52), unilateral renal agenesis (3/52), Zinner syndrome (2/52), ectopic kidney (2/52), and crossed-fused renal ectopia (1/52) and one patient had penoscrotal hypospadias. Pain was the predominant presentation, followed by incidental detection. Among PUJO patients, 12 had right-sided and 8 left-sided disease; all underwent laparoscopic Anderson-Hynes pyeloplasty. In duplex systems, 6 were right-sided and 5 left-sided; three underwent heminephroureterectomy, two lower-moiety pyeloplasty, and the remainder conservative management. All ureteroceles underwent endoscopic incision. One patient with horseshoe kidney and calculus underwent PCNL, while the remaining patients were managed conservatively. All three patients with retrocaval ureter presented with pain and underwent laparoscopic ureteroureterostomy. Both patients with Zinner syndrome underwent laparoscopic seminal vesicle cyst excision. One patient had penoscrotal hypospadias who underwent chordae correction with staged urethroplasty. Ectopic kidneys, renal agenesis and crossed-fused ectopia were managed conservatively.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nCAKUT represents a heterogeneous spectrum of anomalies that may remain undiagnosed until adulthood. Symptomatic obstruction was a common indication for intervention, while incidentally detected and asymptomatic anomalies were frequently managed conservatively. Minimally invasive and endourological approaches provided effective management for appropriately selected symptomatic patients. Early recognition can convert complex congenital anomalies into manageable clinical condition and merits individualized management to preserve bladder and Renal function.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-78\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 78</p><h3>Surgical Management of Various Adrenal Disorders: Our Institutional Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ankit Jain, Dr. Manish Gupta, Dr. Giriraj Prasad Sharma, Dr. Hoti Lal Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Mahatma Gandhi Medical College and Hospital, Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To evaluate the efficacy and outcomes of minimally invasive adrenalectomy, including laparoscopic and robotic approaches, particularly in the management of large adrenal tumors.<br/>\nMethods: A prospective analysis of 85 adrenalectomies performed in 77 patients with adrenal masses of varying sizes was conducted. Patients underwent comprehensive preoperative evaluation, including hormonal studies, radiological imaging, and assessment of surgical and oncological risk. Intraoperative and postoperative outcomes, including blood loss, operative time, conversion to open surgery, postoperative hospital stay, morbidity, and mortality, were recorded. Histopathological findings and follow-up data were also analyzed.<br/>\nResults: The mean tumor size was 7.26 cm. The average blood loss was 115 mL, while the mean operative time was 146 minutes. The mean postoperative hospital stay was 4 days. Among the 77 patients, 40 had functional adrenal tumors. Histopathological examination revealed malignancy in 4 patients (5.19%). Conversion to open surgery occurred in two cases (2.35% of adrenalectomies), primarily due to intraoperative bleeding and technical difficulties. No major postoperative complications were observed. Follow-up demonstrated favorable outcomes, with no recurrence observed in patients with benign pathology during the available follow-up period.<br/>\nConclusion: Minimally invasive adrenalectomy is a safe and effective approach for a wide range of adrenal disorders, including large adrenal tumors. It is associated with acceptable blood loss, operative time, and postoperative hospital stay, with a low conversion rate. Tumor size alone should not be considered an absolute contraindication to a minimally invasive approach, provided that appropriate preoperative evaluation, careful patient selection, adequate surgical expertise, and adherence to oncological principles are ensured. Robotic adrenalectomy may offer additional advantages, including a magnified three-dimensional view, improved dexterity and tumor handling, reduced postoperative pain, and improved cosmesis. Minimally invasive adrenalectomy therefore remains an effective approach for appropriately selected patients with adrenal pathology, including large adrenal masses.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-79\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 79</p><h3>Outcomes of Pyeloplasty in “Non-Functional Kidneys” due to UPJO in children</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Anuj yadav</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nPyeloplasty is increasingly accepted for poorly functioning kidneys in children, but published series define \"poor function\" as 10-20% differential renal function (DRF), and units under 5% usually undergo nephrectomy. Children have considerable renal reserve, and nephrectomy commits a child to a solitary kidney and the long-term risk of hyperfiltration injury. We evaluated the short-term outcome of pyeloplasty in kidneys with pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction (UPJO) showing no function on renography.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nRetrospective analysis of a prospectively maintained database, January 2018 to December 2022. Children under 18 years with unilateral UPJO and a non-visualised kidney (0% DRF on EC renography) undergoing dismembered pyeloplasty with DJ stenting were included. Bilateral obstruction, associated anomalies, previous surgery, chronic kidney disease and follow-up under six months were excluded. Parents consented preoperatively to possible subsequent nephrectomy; stents were removed at six weeks. Symptoms, blood pressure, urinalysis, creatinine, ultrasound (pelvic anteroposterior diameter, parenchymal thickness) and EC renography were assessed at three and six months.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nTen children underwent pyeloplasty; nine with adequate follow-up were analysed. Median age was 5.8 years (1-17), M:F 9:1, and median follow-up 15.1 months (6-27). Presentation was flank pain in seven, antenatal hydronephrosis in two and incidental in one; median creatinine 0.38 mg/dL. Median DRF improved from 0% to 14.83% at three months (p&lt;0.005) and parenchymal thickness from 4.1 to 6.0 mm (p=0.027); the reduction in anteroposterior diameter (51 to 27.8 mm) did not reach significance (p=0.078). Six of nine (66.7%) showed significant functional recovery. Hydronephrosis reduced in six, was stable in two and increased in one, in whom renography confirmed good function and drainage. All became asymptomatic, none developed hypertension, and no kidney required nephrectomy.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nPyeloplasty restored measurable function in two-thirds of kidneys reported as non-visualised, and avoided nephrectomy in all, in the short term. Numbers are small and follow-up limited.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-80\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 80</p><h3>Percutaneous Nephrostomy in Urological Practice: Should Urologists Reclaim the Procedure? A Single-Centre Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Shabbah Alam, Sajad Ahmad Para, Abdul Rouf Khawaja, Arif Hamid Bhat, Sajad Ahmad Malik, Saqib Mehdi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SKIMS, Soura, Srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims</p><p>To evaluate the safety and effectiveness of urologist-performed emergency percutaneous nephrostomy (PCN) and to compare the technical success and complication outcomes of our series with published urologist- and interventional radiologist-performed PCN series.</p><p>Methods</p><p>We retrospectively reviewed 596 emergency PCNs performed between January 2022 and December 2025 at a tertiary-care urology centre. All procedures were performed by urologists using an ultrasound-guided Seldinger technique. Indications, technical and clinical success, Clavien-Dindo complications, and repeat PCN were analysed. Outcomes were compared descriptively with published urologist- and radiologist-performed PCN series.</p><p>Results</p><p>The commonest indications were stone disease (260; 43.6%), carcinoma cervix (120; 20.1%), prostate malignancy (90; 15.1%), bladder malignancy (80; 13.4%) and ureteric stricture (46; 7.7%). Technical success was achieved in 585/596 procedures (98.2%), with 11 failures (1.8%), while clinical success was 91.9%. No complication occurred in 530 patients (88.9%); Clavien-Dindo grade I, II, IIIa and IIIb complications occurred in 6.0%, 3.0%, 1.7% and 0.3%, respectively, with no grade IV/V events. Repeat PCN was required in 28 patients (4.7%). Mahaffey et al. reported 100 urologist-directed ultrasound-guided PCNs with 3% sepsis, 2% pelvic perforation and 1% haemorrhage. Radiologist-performed series reported technical success of 98–99%; Farrell and Hicks reported 99% success with 6.5% overall complications in 454 radiologically guided PCNs, while Lee et al. reported 98% initial success in 169 emergency PCNs. Our outcomes therefore compare favourably with established radiological benchmarks.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>Urologist-performed emergency PCN achieved high technical and clinical success with low major morbidity. Although direct head-to-head emergency PCN comparisons between urologists and interventional radiologists are limited, our outcomes are comparable with published radiology series and urologist experience. These findings support retaining PCN as a core urological skill, with interventional radiology collaboration for complex cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-81\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 81</p><h3>Kidney donation by Individuals with Pre-diabetes: Long-term Renal and Metabolic Outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Girotra, Dr Abhiyutthan Singh Jadaon</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ILBS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: To compare the long-term post-operative kidney function, development of diabetes and hypertension, and proteinuria, of living donors having pre-diabetes at the time of donation with those donors without any evidence of pre-diabetes or diabetes<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: Retrospective data was collected for living donor kidney transplants between January 2014 and December 2024 at our center, with a mean follow up of 6.2 years . Study population: Donors with co-existing conditions such as hypertension, hypothyroidism, or other comorbidities were excluded. Pre-diabetes was not considered a comorbidity, and therefore donors with pre-diabetes at the time of donation were included. A total of 254 donors were studied. They were divided into two groups based on their glycemic status at the time of donation: 36 donors with pre-diabetes (Group 1) and 218 donors without pre-diabetes or any other comorbidity (Group 2). Serum creatinine, 24-hour urinary creatinine clearance, urinary protein and albumin, blood pressure, and development of diabetes mellitus were assessed during follow-up.<br/>\nResults: There was no significant difference observed for the change in serum creatinine or 24-h urinary creatinine clearance between Group 1, having pre-diabetes at the time of donation, and Group 2, which did not have prediabetes or any other comorbidity at the time of donation. Nor was there any significant difference in blood pressure in Group 1 and Group 2. There was a significant difference in the development of significant proteinuria and albuminuria between the two groups. Furthermore, the development of drug or insulin-dependent diabetes was significantly higher in Group 1.<br/>\nConclusion: Pre-diabetic donors may be acceptable but require closer monitoring. As our study did not show evidence of deterioration in kidney function in them post donor nephrectomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-82\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 82</p><h3>Effect of interactive virtual reality on surgical outcomes in living donor nephrectomy - a randomized controlled trial</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kedareswar Maramreddy, Prof Uttam Kumar Mete, Prof Santosh Kumar, Dr Shanky singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PGIMER, Chandigarh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Preoperative planning for live donor nephrectomy (LDN) relies on conventional computed tomography angiography (CTA), which imposes a significant cognitive burden on the operating surgeon and may contribute to surgical surprises. Interactive virtual reality (iVR) externalises this burden by converting patient-specific DICOM datasets into immersive stereoscopic 3D models. We conducted a prospective randomised controlled trial to evaluate whether iVR-assisted preoperative planning reduces operative time and donor morbidity in LDN compared with CTA.</p><p>Methods: 48 live kidney donors were randomized 1:1 at PGIMER, Chandigarh, India (December 2024 to November 2025) to Conventional planning (CTA; n = 24) or iVR planning (patient-specific 3D models via head-mounted VR display, n = 24). The primary endpoint was total operative time. Secondary endpoints included warm ischaemia time (WIT), estimated blood loss (EBL), perioperative morbidity graded by the Clavien-Dindo classification, and length of hospital stay (LOS). Surgeon satisfaction was assessed using a validated 16-item, Likert questionnaire.</p><p>Results: Both arms were well-matched at baseline. The iVR arm demonstrated significantly shorter median operative time (107.5 vs 140.0 min; p = 0.003), lower median EBL (115 vs 275 ml; p &lt; 0.001), and reduced mean WIT (3.01 vs 3.58 min; p = 0.004). Median LOS was 3.5 versus 5.5 days (p &lt; 0.001). The overall complication rate was markedly lower in the iVR cohort (4.2% vs 41.7%; p = 0.004). Surgeon questionnaire internal consistency was excellent (Cronbach's alpha = 0.91), and all 16 items significantly exceeded the neutral midpoint (all p &lt; 0.001).</p><p>Conclusions: The trial confirms the feasibility, safety, and preliminary efficacy of iVR-assisted preoperative planning in LDN. iVR reduces operative time by 23.2%, minimizes postoperative morbidity, and shortens hospital stay by two days. Replacing 2D mental mapping with interactive stereoscopic spatial memory provides clear clinical benefits, supporting its routine adoption in complex donor nephrectomy planning.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-83\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 83</p><h3>ASSOCIATION OF PENILE LENGTH AND PENILE GIRTH WITH DEVELOPMENT OF URETHRAL STRICTURE FOLLOWING TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF THE PROSTATE: A PROSPECTIVE OBSERVATIONAL STUDY</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Abhay Garg , Dr Ketan Vartak , Dr Sachin Patil , Dr Sanjay P Dhangar, Dr Madhur Anand , Dr Shashank Patil</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Bharati Vidyapeeth Medical College Hospital And Research Centre</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS<br/>\nTo prospectively evaluate the association between stretched penile length (SPL) and mid-shaft penile girth with the development of urethral stricture following TURP and to predict stricture risk in patients undergoing TURP with instrumentation designed for Western anthropometric dimensions.</p><p>METHODS<br/>\nA prospective observational study is being conducted in the Department of Urology, Bharati Hospital, Pune (July 2025 to June 2026 ). Men undergoing TURP for benign prostatic enlargement who provided informed consent were included; those with pre-existing urethral stricture, prior urethral or prostate instrumentation, penile anatomical abnormality (hypospadias, Peyronie's disease, prosthesis), or carcinoma prostate were excluded. Under anaesthesia and prior to resection, stretched penile length (dorsally from pubic bone to glans tip after compressing prepubic fat) and mid-shaft penile girth (non-elastic tape) were measured. All patients received standard postoperative care and were followed up for stricture development. No conflict of interest to disclose.</p><p>RESULTS<br/>\nFifty patients have been enrolled and analysed. Mean stretched penile length was 14.6 ± 2.2 cm (range 11–18 cm) and mean mid-shaft girth was 7.0 ± 0.8 cm. Four of 50 patients (8%) developed urethral stricture postoperatively and all four of these patients had a stretched penile length greater than 17.5 cm, with a comparatively reduced penile girth. Increasing penile length together with reduced girth thus appeared to track with a higher incidence of post-TURP urethral stricture in this cohort.</p><p>CONCLUSION<br/>\nGreater penile length with reduced penile girth is associated with a higher risk of urethral stricture after TURP. Preoperative measurement of penile length and girth is a simple, reproducible, non-invasive assessment that may help identify patients at higher risk of stricture, guide instrument selection, and prompt closer postoperative surveillance.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-84\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 84</p><h3>“When the Brain Led to the Kidney and then to the Real Culprit: An Unusual Presentation of an Unusual Small Renal Mass—A Diagnostic Odyssey\"</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Farhat Saleem, Dr Anshuman Agarwal, Dr. Saifullah Nizam, Dr Rajan Prasad Gupta, Dr Rizul Prashar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">indraprastha apollo hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims :<br/>\nTo describe a rare presentation of metastatic gestational choriocarcinoma<br/>\nmasquerading as metastatic renal cell carcinoma and highlight the importance of timely pathological and biochemical evaluation in guiding appropriate management.<br/>\nMaterials and methods:<br/>\nA 33-year-old woman, three months postpartum, presented with headache, vertigo, nausea and right hemiparesis. MRI brain demonstrated a 2.8 × 3 cm left temporoparietal hemorrhagic lesion with mass effect. FDG PET-CT revealed a solitary 1.6 × 1.4 cm FDG-avid exophytic lesion in the mid-pole of right kidney, suspicious for metastatic renal cell carcinoma. Owing to progressive neurological deficits, she underwent urgent left parietal craniotomy with excision of the brain lesion. 48 hours later, she developed spontaneous rupture of the renal mass (Wunderlich syndrome) with gross hematuria and active arterial extravasation on CECT, necessitating emergency laparoscopic radical nephrectomy. Initial brain histopathology suggested poorly differentiated metastatic carcinoma with inconclusive immunohistochemistry. Renal tumour histopathology demonstrated diffuse positivity for β-hCG, SALL4, GATA3, CK7, and MUC4, with negativity for PAX8, CK20, EMA, and p63, establishing metastatic gestational choriocarcinoma. Serum β-hCG was 12,771 mIU/mL, confirming the diagnosis.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe diagnosis changed from presumed metastatic renal cell carcinoma to metastatic gestational choriocarcinoma, fundamentally altering management from treatment of primary renal malignancy to systemic chemotherapy. The patient achieved complete neurological recovery after craniotomy, recovered uneventfully following laparoscopic radical nephrectomy, and was referred for multi-agent chemotherapy.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nThis case illustrates how sequential life-threatening events concealed a highly curable malignancy. Postpartum gestational choriocarcinoma following a live birth is rare and often presents with advanced metastatic disease . Gestational choriocarcinoma should be considered in postpartum women presenting with hemorrhagic brain lesions and atypical renal masses. Early multidisciplinary evaluation, including serum β-hCG estimation and appropriate immunohistochemistry, is crucial to avoid diagnostic delay and facilitate timely initiation of potentially curative chemotherapy, even in metastatic disease</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-85\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 85</p><h3>Beyond Hypertension: Ten Faces of Pheochromocytoma—Lessons learnt from consecutive adrenalectomies on Diagnostic Diversity, Perioperative Challenges and surgical Outcomes.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Farhat Saleem, Dr. Anshuman Agarwal, Dr Rajan Prasad Gupta, Dr. Saif Nizam, Dr. Rizul Prashar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">indraprastha apollo hospital, new delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo highlight that pheochromocytoma extends beyond its classical hypertensive phenotype by analyzing the diverse clinical presentations, diagnostic challenges, multidisciplinary management, and<br/>\nsurgical outcomes.<br/>\nMaterials and methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was performed of 10 consecutive patients with histopathologically confirmed pheochromocytoma who underwent adrenalectomy between 2018 and 2025. Demographic characteristics,<br/>\nclinical presentation, blood pressure profile, biochemical evaluation, imaging findings, operative approach, intraoperative haemodynamic events, histopathology, postoperative complications, and surgical outcomes were analysed.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThese ten patients demonstrated remarkable clinical heterogeneity.<br/>\nApart from classical symptoms , two patients were normotensive despite markedly elevated catecholamine metabolites, while one patient had negative biochemical evaluation and was diagnosed only on histopathology, representing a biochemically silent pheochromocytoma. Other distinctive presentations included catecholamine-induced dilated cardiomyopathy, pyrexia of unknown origin, cystic pheochromocytoma, adrenal incidentaloma, and synchronous functional pheochromocytoma in a patient with metastatic prostate adenocarcinoma, emphasizing that adrenal masses in patients with known malignancy are not always metastatic. Tumour size ranged from 3.5 to 12.7 cm. Following standardized endocrine optimization with α-blockade and multidisciplinary perioperative planning, seven patients underwent robotic adrenalectomy, two laparoscopic adrenalectomy, and one open adrenalectomy for a giant tumour. One patient developed an intraoperative hypertensive surge, while another required postoperative vasopressor support. Histopathology<br/>\nconfirmed pheochromocytoma in all patients. There were no conversions, perioperative mortality, and all patients achieved favourable postoperative outcomes.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nWhile pheochromocytoma is traditionally associated with the classic triad of headache, palpitations, diaphoresis, and hypertension (1), our experience demonstrates that it is a true clinical masquerader with diverse and often atypical presentations.Normotensive, biochemically silent, cystic, incidental, cardiomyopathy-associated, malignancy-associated, and pyrexia of unknown origin presentations underscore the need for a high index of suspicion and comprehensive biochemical evaluation of all adrenal masses. Recognition of these varied phenotypes, coupled with comprehensive endocrine evaluation and multidisciplinary perioperative management, enables safe adrenalectomy and excellent surgical outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-86\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 86</p><h3>Pre-treatment Neutrophil-Lymphocyte Ratio(NLR) in Carcinoma Penis: Promising Prognostic Marker in Indian Cohort</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Toshib G A, Dr Saurabh Meshram, Dr Rohit Gupta, Dr Prashant Singh, Prof Amlesh Seth</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: The neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio (NLR) is an inexpensive biomarker of systemic inflammation that has been proposed as a surrogate prognostic marker in penile cancer. Although several studies and meta-analyses report associations between elevated NLR and adverse outcomes, the evidence is largely from the west and lacks standardisation. We examined the correlation between pre-treatment NLR and tumour characteristics, lymph-node status, cancer-specific survival (CSS), and overall survival (OS) among surgically treated patients at a tertiary-care hospital in India.<br/>\nMethods: This was a retrospective analysis of a prospectively maintained database. A total of 140 patients with histologically confirmed carcinoma of the penis, treated surgically between 2015 and 2025 with an available pre-treatment complete blood count, were included. NLR was calculated from the differential count. Associations were analysed using Fisher’s exact and Mann–Whitney tests; discrimination was assessed by receiver-operating-characteristic (ROC) analysis. CSS and OS were analysed using Kaplan–Meier, log-rank, and Cox regression, using both a data-derived cut-off and the literature-standard threshold of 3.0.<br/>\nResults: Follow-up was available for 131 of 140 patients for final analysis. Median age was 56 years; median NLR was 2.58 (IQR 1.88–3.70), with 53 patients (37.9%) having NLR ≥3.0. NLR discriminated poorly for pathological (AUC 0.47) and clinical (AUC 0.54) nodal positivity, and was not associated with pathological (OR 0.88, p=1.00) or clinical (OR 1.39, p=0.38) node positivity. Elevated NLR was associated with perineural invasion (OR 2.47, p=0.047). Lymphovascular invasion was the only independent predictor of CSS (HR 2.68, p=0.037) and OS (HR 2.62, p=0.030).<br/>\nConclusions: Pre-treatment NLR was associated with nodal metastasis, CSS in carcinoma of the penis. Its prognostic value noted on multivariable analysis, suggesting utility as a prognostic marker. NLR and newer prognostic markers need to be considered in the Indian population.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-87\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 87</p><h3>Changing Paradigm in Emphysematous Pyelonephritis: Zero Mortality with an Early Minimally Invasive Approach – A Single-Centre Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sadiq Husain, Dr Pragnesh Desai, Dr S Venkatesh, Dr Devi Chauhan, Dr Trushar Baman</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Yatharth Superspeciality Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the clinical profile, management strategies, and outcomes of patients with emphysematous pyelonephritis (EPN) treated at a tertiary care centre with particular emphasis on renal-preserving interventions and their effectiveness.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA retrospective observational study was conducted between Jan 2024 to Jan 2026 which included patients with CT-confirmed EPN. Demographic characteristics, comorbidities, Huang–Tseng classification, microbiological profile, treatment modalities and clinical outcomes were reviewed. The primary outcomes were renal salvage and in-hospital mortality. Secondary outcomes included intensive care unit (ICU) admission, need for nephrectomy, renal replacement therapy and hospital stay.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThirteen patients were included in the study with a mean age of 60.4 ± 9.2 years; 92.3% were female. Diabetes mellitus was present in all patients, while hypertension, chronic kidney disease, and coronary artery disease were observed in 38.5%, 15.4%, and 7.7%, respectively. Advanced Huang–Tseng Class III disease was present in 69.2% of patients, and 30.8% presented with septic shock. Escherichia coli was the predominant pathogen isolated followed by Klebsiella pneumoniae. All patients received empirical broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics followed by culture-directed therapy. Patients were managed with percutaneous nephrostomy in 7 (53.8%) patients, primary retrograde DJ stenting in 5 (38.5%), while 2 (15.4%) required combined PCN and DJ stenting. One patient (7.7%) underwent emergency nephrectomy. ICU admission was required in 76.9% of patients. Renal preservation was achieved in 92.3%. No patient required renal replacement therapy and there was no in-hospital mortality. Patients presenting with septic shock had significantly longer hospital stays (P = 0.039).<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nEarly multidisciplinary management with prompt resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and timely minimally invasive urinary drainage achieved excellent renal salvage and zero mortality despite a high proportion of advanced EPN. These findings support an organ-preserving approach as the preferred first-line management strategy, reserving nephrectomy for carefully selected patients with uncontrolled sepsis or non-salvageable kidneys.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-88\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 88</p><h3>Native nephrectomy in ADPKD before renal transplantation: an individualized approach to timing and laterality</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aditya Pradhan , Dr Pankaj gupta, Dr Ashutosh Kumar, Dr Rakshit pancholi, Dr Suman</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Venkateshwar superspeciality hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo demonstrate a symptom- and anatomy-driven strategy for nephrectomy in patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) awaiting renal transplantation, with emphasis on the choice of nephrectomy timing, laterality and simultaneous transplantation.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nTwo contrasting patients with massive bilateral nephromegaly were evaluated using ultrasonography and computed tomography as part of pre-transplant planning. Decisions regarding pre-transplant versus simultaneous nephrectomy, unilateral versus bilateral nephrectomy, operative approach and avoidance of transfusion were made following multidisciplinary discussion.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nCase 1 was a 41-year-old potential recipient with massive bilateral nephromegaly extending to the inguinal ligaments. He had early satiety, weight loss, breathing difficulty, bilateral flank pain and pyuria (10–12 pus cells/high-power field). Pre-transplant bilateral nephrectomy was selected to relieve symptoms and provide adequate space for right iliac fossa graft placement. Right laparoscopic nephrectomy was completed uneventfully, with specimen extraction through a midline upper-abdominal incision. The same incision provided access to the left renal hilum in the setting of a lax abdominal wall, allowing completion of open left nephrectomy. No blood transfusion was required. Renal transplantation was performed six weeks later with an uncomplicated recovery. Case 2 was a 51-year-old patient with comparable nephromegaly but without abdominal compartment symptoms or clinical evidence of infection. Simultaneous right nephrectomy and renal transplantation was therefore chosen. Open right nephrectomy through a flank incision was completed in 70 minutes, followed by right iliac fossa renal transplantation through a separate incision. Recovery was uneventful.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nMassive native-kidney enlargement in ADPKD should not mandate routine pre-transplant bilateral nephrectomy. Individualized assessment of symptoms, infection, abdominal space and graft placement can guide timing and laterality. Selected patients may undergo unilateral nephrectomy with simultaneous transplantation, whereas symptomatic patients requiring bilateral nephrectomy may benefit from a staged approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-89\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 89</p><h3>Neobladder- A single high volume center experience from Eastern India</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Bikram Haldar, Dr. Abhay Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Manipal Hospital EM Bypass</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nIn patients with urinary bladder cancer, Radical cystectomy with reconstruction of a Neobladder restores the voiding pattern to a near normal level essentially avoiding the need of a permanent abdominal stoma. To achieve the best outcome, careful patient selection and appropriate surgical technique is of upmost importance. Data on such a complicated surgical principle is very low from the Eastern India.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nWe retrospectively reviewed all the patients who underwent Radical cystectomy with orthotopic Neobladder at a single tertiary-care Oncology center by a single primary surgeon over the last 11 years. Various parameters like the demographics, indications, technique of reconstruction of neobladder, post-operative complications, outcomes and follow up were analysed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nAmong the 310 patients who underwent Radical cystectomy, 90 patients received an Orthotopic Neobladder (89 men and 1 women). The mean age range was 61 years. Common indications were muscle invasive urothelial carcinoma (69 cases) and BCG-refractory non-muscle invasive bladder carcinoma (21 cases). 84.4% of the patients achieved complete day and night time continence, 11.1% required pad and clamp support and 4.4% cases required CISC for bladder outlet stricture. Early urine leak was noted in 6.7% cases, readmission due to complicated UTI in 8.9%, 3.3% cases required re-exploration of the abdomen. 4.4% cases developed recurrence. Overall patient data was suggestive of stable renal function in this series. On two-proportion z-testing, our continence and surgical complications rate did not differ significantly from that of major Indian (Tata Medica Centre, North Indian tertiary care hospital) and International (Bern, Mayo clinic) series. The day and night time continence pattern was also comparable to the published evidences.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nWith the help of careful patient selection, meticulous surgical technique, a structured rehabilitation programme and proper patient training, orthotopic Neobladder reconstruction can be offered with acceptable morbidity and good functional outcome.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-90\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 90</p><h3>Three Dimensional Versus Two Dimensional Laparoscopic Partial Nephrectomy for Intermediate to High Complexity Renal Tumours: A Prospective Single Surgeon Comparative Study Using Pentafecta as the Primary Endpoint</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vineet Bhardwaj, Dr Muzzain Iqbal, Dr Elias Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Super Speciality Hospital Govt Medical College Jammu</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nLaparoscopic partial nephrectomy (LPN) is a nephron‑sparing procedure with lower morbidity than open surgery. Conventional two‑dimensional (2D) laparoscopy lacks stereoscopic depth perception, prolonging sutured renorrhaphy under warm ischaemia. Three‑dimensional (3D) laparoscopy restores stereoscopic vision, potentially reducing ischaemia time and improving renal function at lower cost than robotic platforms. Prospective evidence using composite pentafecta outcomes for intermediate‑to‑high complexity tumors remains limited. This study evaluated whether 3D‑LPN improves pentafecta achievement compared with 2D‑LPN in a single‑surgeon setting.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nBetween January 2022 and December 2023, 70 patients with RENAL nephrometry scores of 7–9 were prospectively randomized to 3D‑LPN (n=35) or 2D‑LPN (n=35). All procedures were performed by one urologist at a tertiary teaching hospital in North India. Minimum follow‑up was 18 months. The primary endpoint was pentafecta achievement: warm ischaemia time (WIT) ≤25 minutes, negative margins, no Clavien‑Dindo ≥III complication, ≥90% eGFR preservation, and no CKD stage upstaging.</p><p>Results<br/>\nPentafecta was achieved in 68.6% of 3D‑LPN versus 42.9% of 2D‑LPN patients (p=0.030). Median WIT was shorter with 3D (21.8 vs. 27.4 minutes; p=0.002). eGFR preservation ≥90% occurred in 85.7% versus 62.9% (p=0.029). CKD upstaging at 18 months was lower in the 3D group (5.7% vs. 25.7%; p=0.022). Serum creatinine normalized by 3 months in the 3D arm but remained elevated in the 2D arm. Positive margins (2.9% vs. 5.7%; p=1.000) and major complications (2.9% vs. 5.7%; p=1.000) were equivalent.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nThree‑dimensional LPN significantly improved pentafecta achievement for intermediate‑to‑high complexity renal tumors, primarily through reduced warm ischaemia time and sustained functional benefit. In centres lacking robotic platforms, 3D laparoscopy represents a cost‑effective upgrade for complex nephron‑sparing surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-91\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 91</p><h3>Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy in Crossed Fused Renal Ectopia with Obstructing Calculi: A Challenging Endourological Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr S middha , Dr S mittal , Dr y chundawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sdmh jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nTo describe the feasibility and technical considerations of percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) in a<br/>\npatient with crossed fused renal ectopia presenting with obstructing calculi.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 42-year-old male with obstructive uropathy underwent NCCT KUB, which demonstrated crossed fused<br/>\nrenal ectopia on the right. The left kidney was absent from the left renal fossa and fused with the lower<br/>\npole of the right kidney; both renal units were horizontally oriented and conglomerated. An 11-mm left<br/>\nmid-ureteric calculus at L3–L4 with moderate hydronephrosis and a 15-mm right PUJ calculus with<br/>\nmild-to-moderate hydronephrosis were identified, along with an upper-pole calyceal calculus. Perinephric<br/>\nstranding was present. Following bilateral DJ stenting for decompression, right PCNL was performed in<br/>\nthe prone position. Retrograde pyelography was followed by fluoroscopy-guided percutaneous puncture<br/>\nand tract dilatation to 21 Fr. Stone fragmentation was performed using lithotripsy, followed by DJ stent<br/>\nplacement.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nNephroscopy identified the right pelvic/PUJ calculus and near-complete clearance of the targeted stone<br/>\nburden was achieved. The procedure was completed without reported intraoperative complication. The<br/>\npostoperative period was uneventful and the patient was discharged in stable condition.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nPCNL is feasible in crossed fused renal ectopia despite altered renal orientation and collecting-system<br/>\nanatomy. Detailed preoperative imaging, careful access planning and precise fluoroscopy-guided<br/>\npuncture are essential for safe and effective stone management in renal fusion anomalies.<br/>\nConflict of interest: No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-92\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 92</p><h3>Pushing Surgical Boundaries: Outcomes of Radical Nephrectomy with IVC Thrombectomy for Level III–IV RCC</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Rahil Kumar, Bhanu Pradeep Yadav, Anurag Kushwaha, Prashant Singh, Amlesh Seth</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nRenal cell carcinoma (RCC) with inferior vena cava (IVC) tumor thrombus is technically challenging, particularly with Level III and IV thrombus. Successful management requires advanced surgical expertise and multidisciplinary coordination, including cardiothoracic surgery support. We evaluated outcomes following radical nephrectomy with Level III/IV IVC thrombectomy.</p><p>Methodology:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was performed of patients with RCC and Level III/IV IVC thrombus undergoing radical nephrectomy with thrombectomy at our tertiary care center with planned cardiothoracic surgery backup. Demographic, operative, and histopathological data, including tumor subtype, ISUP grade, and adverse pathological features, were evaluated.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nEighteen patients were included; 4 (22.2%) had Level III and 14 (77.8%) had Level IV thrombus. Mean age was 52.85±10.22 years, with male predominance. Comorbidities were present in 6 (33%) patients, with most being ASA grade I–II. Mean tumor size was 10.7±5.18 cm, with a predominance of left-sided tumors. Preoperative mean hemoglobin, creatinine, and albumin were 10.47±2.30 g/dL, 0.88±0.21 mg/dL, and 3.7±0.46 g/dL, respectively. Mean operative time was 349±58.9 minutes, estimated blood loss 1158±464 mL, transfusion requirement 2.9±1.18 units, and hospital stay 10.42±3.09 days. Level IV thrombus was associated with longer operative time and greater blood loss, although not statistically significant. Postoperative complications occurred in 16 (88%) patients, predominantly low-grade (66%); high-grade complications occurred in 22%, with one perioperative mortality (5.5%).</p><p>Clear cell RCC predominated (72%), followed by papillary RCC (17%). Grade 3–4 tumors comprised 66%. Tumor necrosis and lymphovascular invasion were each present in 44%, while sarcomatoid differentiation and perineural invasion occurred in 11% and 17%, respectively, and were more common in Level IV thrombus.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRadical nephrectomy with IVC thrombectomy for Level III/IV RCC is feasible with acceptable perioperative outcomes in a multidisciplinary setting. Structured multidisciplinary management facilitates successful surgery despite advanced thrombus and aggressive pathological features.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-93\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 93</p><h3>From Embryologic Remnant to Malignancy: A Tertiary Centre Experience with Urachal Adenocarcinoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Rahil Kumar, Arun Kumar, Mehul Gupta, Toshib G A, Nitish Aggarwal, Seema Kaushal, Amlesh Seth, Siddharth Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nUrachal adenocarcinoma is a rare malignancy accounting for &lt;0.7% of bladder cancers and arises from remnants of the urachus. Owing to its predominantly extraperitoneal location at the bladder dome, early-stage disease is often clinically silent, leading to delayed presentation and advanced stage at diagnosis. Surgical en-bloc resection remains the cornerstone of treatment. Data from the Indian population remain limited. This study evaluates the clinical presentation, management strategies, and oncological outcomes of patients with urachal adenocarcinoma treated at a tertiary care centre.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nA retrospective observational study was conducted at our institution, reviewing patients diagnosed with urachal adenocarcinoma over an eight-year period. Adult patients with primary or recurrent tumours arising from the urachus or bladder dome were included based on established diagnostic criteria. Demographic characteristics, clinical presentation, imaging findings, histopathology, immunohistochemistry, staging (TNM and Sheldon systems), surgical management, and follow-up outcomes were analysed.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nFifteen patients with histopathologically confirmed urachal adenocarcinoma were identified, with a male predominance (80%) and mean age of 39.5 years. Painless gross hematuria was the most common presenting symptom (73%). Fourteen patients presented with primary disease, while one had recurrent disease. Surgical resection was feasible in 14 patients (93%), with open partial cystectomy performed in 10 (67%) and radical cystectomy in three (20%). One underwent partial cystectomy with cytoreductive surgery and HIPEC. Most tumours were enteric-type adenocarcinoma (87%). According to the Sheldon system, pT3a stage was most common (53%). Lymphnode metastasis was present in two patients (14%). At a mean follow-up of 30 months, 9 of 11 evaluable patients remained disease-free, while two developed recurrence. Overall mortality was 20%.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nUrachal adenocarcinoma commonly presents at an advanced stage, most frequently with hematuria. Despite advanced disease, aggressive en-bloc surgical resection can achieve favourable oncological outcomes, emphasising the importance of early recognition and definitive surgical management.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-94\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 94</p><h3>Overcoming Diagnostic Dilemmas: Proceeding with Organ Donation and Urethroplasty in Suspected Prostate Malignancy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Udit Jain, Vikas Agarwal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Hospital, Dwarka</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To evaluate clinical decision-making and surgical management strategies when suspected or confirmed prostate malignancy intersects with living organ donation and complex reconstructive urology.<br/>\nMethod: Two clinical scenarios involving elevated prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and suspicious multiparametric magnetic resonance imaging (MPMRI) findings were managed:<br/>\nCase 1: A 68-year-old prospective living kidney donor with a PSA of 9.2\" ng/ml\" and a PI-RADS 5 lesion underwent a TRUS-guided 12-core biopsy and PSMA PET scan, confirming intermediate-risk localized acinar adenocarcinoma (Gleason 3+4=7, cT2a). Following evidence-based risk assessment for tumour transmission, a left laparoscopic donor nephrectomy was executed.<br/>\nCase 2: A 70-year-old male scheduled for progressive perineal urethroplasty for a pelvic fracture urethral injury (PFUI) was found to have a PSA of 8.09\" ng/ml\" and a PI-RADS 4 lesion. To avoid surgical delays, the patient underwent concurrent urethral reconstruction and intraoperative TRUS-guided prostate biopsy after multidisciplinary counselling.<br/>\nResult:<br/>\nCase 1: Successful donor nephrectomy was completed without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, the donor received Leuprolide therapy, and the recipient demonstrated stable graft function with no evidence of tumour transmission during follow-up.<br/>\nCase 2: The intraoperative biopsy revealed benign prostatic tissue. The patient achieved successful urethral reconstruction, uneventful catheter removal, and excellent voiding outcomes.<br/>\nConclusion: Suspected or confirmed low-to-intermediate-risk prostate cancer should not serve as an absolute contraindication to living kidney donation or necessary urological reconstruction. Tailored interventions and multidisciplinary collaboration enable safe, effective clinical outcomes for both organ donors and complex reconstructive candidates.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-95\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 95</p><h3>Clinical Outcomes of Retained Double J Ureteral Stents and Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Nitin Kumar Jhalani</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JLN Medical college &amp; attached hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nDouble-J (DJ) ureteral stents are widely used to maintain urinary tract patency. Prolonged retention may cause encrustation, infection, migration, fragmentation, and stone formation. This study evaluates complications of retained DJ stents and their management.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nTwenty patients (12 males, 8 females), aged 11–60 years, with DJ stents retained for ≥12 months were studied. Evaluation included X-ray KUB, IVU, NCCT, and urine culture. Cystoscopic removal was performed for minimal encrustation, URSL/CLT for ureteric or bladder involvement, PCNL for renal coil encrustation, and open ureterotomy for severe knotting and migration.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nDysuria occurred in 45%, recurrent UTI in 60%, loin pain in 25%, and hematuria in 20%. Encrustation was seen in 90%, stone formation in 25%, migration in 25%, and fragmentation in 15%. Endoscopic management was successful in 95%, while 5% required open surgery. One patient developed postoperative sepsis; there were no deaths.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRetained DJ stents require individualized management. Regular follow-up, timely stent exchange, preoperative imaging, sterile urine culture, and endourological management are recommended.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-96\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 96</p><h3>A Prospective Observational Study on Outcome and Complications of Radical Cystectomy with Ileal Conduit for muscle invasive bladder cancer and the Role of Perioperative Factors</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Marbaitki Nongpluh, Prof (Dr) Syed Sajjad Nazir</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Shreenbagh, SSH, GMC Srinagar</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\n- to describe the incidence and severity of post operative complications using clavien dindo classification<br/>\nMethods<br/>\n- descriptive observational study from jan 2023 to jan 2026 in the department of urology, SSH, GMC, Srinagar<br/>\nResults<br/>\n- mean length of stay is 16.9 days<br/>\n- 39.5% had one or more complications<br/>\n- 18.6% had Grade 3 or more<br/>\n- NACT was associated with higher complications rate<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\n- Radical cystectomy remains a morbid procedure with significant complications rate</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-97\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 97</p><h3>Xanthogranulomatous Pyelonephritis Secondary to a Giant Staghorn Calculus in a Non-functioning Horseshoe Kidney: A Rare Surgical Challenge Managed by a Staged Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Prashant Garg, Dr Devesh Bhatia, Dr. M. Shazib Faridi, Dr Kartik Sridhar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Dr baba saheb ambedkar mdical college and hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo describe the staged management of an uncommon coexistence of giant staghorn calculus, pyonephrosis, and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis in a non-functioning horseshoe kidney, and to highlight the surgical principles that facilitated safe extirpative surgery despite complex renovascular anatomy and extensive inflammatory disease.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA 30-year-old woman presented with intermittent fever and right flank pain. Contrast-enhanced CT demonstrated a horseshoe kidney with a 10-cm right staghorn calculus, gross hydronephrosis, pyonephrosis, and severe cortical thinning. Diuretic renography confirmed a non-functioning right renal moiety (GFR 2.15 mL/min; split renal function 4.63%). Ultrasound-guided percutaneous nephrostomy was performed, draining approximately 250 mL of purulent material, followed by culture-directed intravenous antibiotics. After resolution of sepsis and optimization, CT renal angiography was obtained to define the anomalous arterial anatomy and guide operative planning before definitive open heminephrectomy.</p><p>Results<br/>\nOpen right heminephrectomy revealed a markedly enlarged renal moiety densely adherent to surrounding structures, with complete distortion of hilar anatomy and a thick vascularized isthmus. Preoperative CT angiography enabled systematic vascular control despite multiple aberrant vessels. Dense inflammatory fibrosis consistent with chronic infection rendered hilar dissection particularly challenging. Intraoperative inferior vena cava injury and pleural rent were identified promptly and repaired primarily without sequelae. Histopathological examination demonstrated chronic xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, while stone analysis revealed calcium oxalate monohydrate. The postoperative course was uneventful, with preservation of contralateral renal function and complete symptomatic resolution.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nThe concurrence of giant staghorn calculus, pyonephrosis, xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis, and a non-functioning horseshoe kidney is exceptionally uncommon. A staged strategy comprising early source control, detailed vascular mapping, and carefully planned heminephrectomy can facilitate safe management of this formidable surgical condition and should be considered when extensive inflammatory pathology coexists with complex congenital renal anatomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-98\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 98</p><h3>Urodynamic Findings in Diabetic Patients Presenting with Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Harneet Kaur Khurana, Dr Sandeep Sharma, Dr Harmandeep Singh Chahal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Dayanand medical college, Ludhiana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) represent a little known, but common complication to diabetes mellitus, caused by secondary progressive bladder dysfunction related to diabetic neuropathy. Urodynamic examination is an objective test of bladder functionality and helps with the identification of underlying pathologies.<br/>\nObjective: To assess the urodynamic results of diabetic patients who presented with lower urinary tract symptoms and to assess how these are related to length of diabetes.<br/>\nMethods: This prospective observational study involved the Department of Urology, Dayanand Medical College and Hospital in Ludhiana between April 2024 and July 2025. A total of 40 patients of over 35 years with LUTS who were diabetic were subjected to comprehensive clinical examination, laboratory tests, ultrasound and multichannel urodynamic studies. Data were done in SPSS version 26.0.<br/>\nResults: The average age of the participants was 63.55 + 11.27 years, with a higher number of males (80%). The most prevalent urodynamic abnormality was low bladder compliance followed by detrusor underactivity and increased post-void residual urine. The occurrence of classical diabetic cystopathy was less among the patients. The longer a person has diabetes the worse urodynamic parameters it has, which makes it progressive bladder dysfunction.<br/>\nConclusion: Diabetic patients who have LUTS have varying urodynamic abnormalities. Urodynamic assessment can be administered effectively to perform accurate diagnoses, provide customized treatment planning and manage diabetic bladder dysfunction better.<br/>\nKeywords: Diabetes mellitus; Lower urinary tract symptoms; Urodynamic study; Diabetic cystopathy; Detrusor underactivity; Bladder compliance.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-99\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 99</p><h3>Comprehensive Evaluation and Algorithmic Management of the Forgotten Ureteral Double-J Stent: A Retrospective Cohort Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr N Swaminarayan, Dr K Sridhar, Dr D Bhatia, Dr M S Faridi, Dr A Boringwala, Dr P Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Dr Babasaheb Ambedkar Hospital, Rohini</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Objective: The double-J (DJ) ureteral stent is an indispensable tool in modern endourology but<br/>\nbecomes a highly morbid nidus for complications when patients are lost to follow-up. Managing<br/>\n\"forgotten,\" heavily encrusted stents remains a daunting surgical challenge lacking standardized<br/>\nguidelines. This study evaluates a tailored, multi-stage management algorithm driven by indwelling<br/>\ntime, encrustation severity, and renal functional reserve.<br/>\nMethods: We conducted a retrospective cohort study analyzing 13 patients with forgotten DJ stents<br/>\nretained for 6 to 24 months. Following comprehensive radiological and functional evaluations,<br/>\npatients were stratified using the Forgotten, Encrusted, Calcified (FECal) grading system and Split<br/>\nRenal Function (SRF) via renography. Systematic treatment pathways matched pathology to targeted<br/>\nendourological or extirpative surgery.<br/>\nResults: Simple cystoscopic traction succeeded only for minimal encrustations (FECal Grade I; n=2).<br/>\nModerate-to-severe encrustations (FECal Grades II–IV; n=9) required advanced, often staged<br/>\ninterventions. Single-setting Endoscopic Combined IntraRenal Surgery (ECIRS) rapidly cleared<br/>\ncomplex cases, while others necessitated staged ureteroscopy and Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy<br/>\n(PCNL). Terminal stenting (FECal Grade V; n=2) caused irreversible parenchymal destruction (SRF &lt;<br/>\n15%). One terminal case suffered stent fracture during attempted extraction; both ultimately<br/>\nrequired extirpative surgery (nephrectomy or nephro-ureterectomy). A stone-free state was<br/>\nachieved in 100% of the salvaged renal units.<br/>\nConclusion: The forgotten DJ stent is a preventable urological tragedy. While advanced combined<br/>\nendourology can salvage most calcified units, prolonged retention may cause complete organ loss.<br/>\nAdopting a structured, FECal-driven algorithmic approach is critical to safely navigating this<br/>\nendourological challenge.<br/>\nKeywords: Ureteral Stents; Device Removal; Lithotripsy; Postoperative Complications; Algorithms;<br/>\nEndourology</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-100\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 100</p><h3>Optimal Trendelenburg Tilt Angle in Robot-Assisted Pelvic Surgery: A Prospective Study of Hemodynamic and Surgical Outcomes</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh C Sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Girish Sharma, Dr Shivam Chaudhary</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim and Objectives<br/>\nRobot-assisted pelvic surgeries offer significant advantages, particularly in terms of improved intraoperative maneuverability and better postoperative outcomes. However, the steep Trendelenburg position required during these procedures is associated with several physiological complications. This study aimed to determine the optimal tilt angle for robot-assisted pelvic surgeries.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nA total of 60 patients were enrolled in this study and divided into case and control groups. All patients underwent robot-assisted pelvic surgery in the Trendelenburg position, with the tilt angle maintained at the minimum required to achieve adequate surgical exposure without compromising the procedure. The two primary procedures evaluated were robotic radical prostatectomy and robotic hysterectomy. Intraocular pressure (IOP) and intracranial pressure (ICP) were measured during Trendelenburg positioning. Demographic variables, including body mass index (BMI) and waist-to-hip ratio, were also analyzed. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS version 24.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nA significant increase in IOP and ICP was observed at Trendelenburg angles of 25°–30°, whereas no significant changes were noted at angles below 25°. Hemodynamic parameters also remained more stable at tilt angles below 25°. Surgical exposure was satisfactory with minimal technical difficulty at 20°–25° of Trendelenburg tilt. Patients with a higher BMI and increased waist-to-hip ratio demonstrated greater adverse physiological changes at tilt angles exceeding 25°.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nOur findings suggest that a Trendelenburg angle of 20°–25° provides an optimal balance between adequate surgical exposure and minimal hemodynamic and physiological alterations during robot-assisted pelvic surgeries.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-101\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 101</p><h3>Retzius-Sparing Robot-Assisted Radical Prostatectomy Following Previous TURP: TIPS AND TRICKS</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sanyam Nandwani, Dr Swapan Sood</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PATEL HOSPITAL, CIVIL LINES, JALANDHAR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Prior transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) significantly alters periprostatic anatomy, resulting in obliterated tissue planes, bladder neck distortion, periurethral fibrosis, and loss of conventional surgical landmarks. These changes are further accentuated during the Retzius-sparing approach, where posterior orientation and precise identification of the prostatovesical junction are critical. This video demonstrates our operative strategy and technical modifications for performing Retzius-sparing robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RS-RARP) in a post-TURP patient while maintaining oncological principles and reconstructive integrity.</p><p>Methods: A stepwise surgical video highlights preoperative planning with multiparametric MRI and cystoscopic correlation, posterior dissection, identification of the distorted bladder neck, management of the TURP cavity, dissection through dense fibrosis, neurovascular bundle preservation where oncologically appropriate, apical urethral dissection, tailored bladder neck reconstruction, and tension-free vesicourethral anastomosis. Emphasis is placed on anatomical cues, maintaining orientation within the posterior approach, avoiding inadvertent entry into the TURP cavity, and strategies to minimize urinary leak and anastomotic complications.</p><p>Results: The procedure was completed robotically without conversion or major intraoperative complications. The video illustrates reproducible technical solutions to overcome the challenges posed by altered post-TURP anatomy, facilitating safe dissection, secure reconstruction, and adherence to sound oncological principles.</p><p>Conclusion: RS-RARP following TURP remains one of the more technically demanding scenarios in robotic prostatectomy. Success depends on meticulous preoperative assessment, careful intraoperative decision-making, and thoughtful modification of standard technique. This video focuses on practical operative nuances and troubleshooting strategies that may help experienced robotic surgeons safely manage this challenging subset of patients.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-102\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 102</p><h3>Multidisciplinary Management of Adrenal Masses: A Five-Case Series Highlighting Diverse Presentations of Pheochromocytoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sangram , Dr Rajeev Sarpal , Dr Shikhar Agarwal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS Dehradun</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo evaluate the clinical presentation, diagnostic workup, perioperative management, and surgical outcomes of patients with adrenal masses, with special emphasis on pheochromocytoma.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was conducted of five patients with adrenal masses managed at our tertiary care center. Demographic details, presenting symptoms, biochemical investigations, imaging findings, perioperative preparation, surgical approach, histopathological diagnosis, and postoperative outcomes were analyzed. Patients with suspected pheochromocytoma underwent biochemical evaluation using plasma or urinary catecholamine/metanephrine levels. Cross-sectional imaging with contrast-enhanced computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging was performed to characterize tumor size, location, and vascularity. Patients with functional tumors were optimized preoperatively using alpha-adrenergic blockade followed by beta-blockade when indicated. Depending on tumor characteristics, patients underwent either laparoscopic or open adrenalectomy. Intraoperative hemodynamic changes, perioperative complications, histopathological findings, and follow-up outcomes were assessed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe patients presented with a spectrum of symptoms including flank pain, headache, palpitations, excessive sweating, and uncontrolled hypertension, while some adrenal masses were detected incidentally during imaging. Tumor size and location varied, with highly vascular lesions identified in several patients. Elevated catecholamine or metanephrine levels supported the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma in the majority of cases. Three patients underwent laparoscopic adrenalectomy, whereas two required open adrenalectomy because of larger tumor size or suspected local involvement. Intraoperative blood pressure fluctuations were successfully managed with close anesthetic monitoring and appropriate pharmacological intervention. Histopathological examination confirmed pheochromocytoma in most patients. No major perioperative complications or mortality occurred. During follow-up, all patients demonstrated symptomatic improvement, with normalization or significantly improved control of blood pressure and satisfactory recovery.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nAdrenal masses, particularly pheochromocytomas, require comprehensive biochemical and radiological evaluation, meticulous preoperative optimization, and a multidisciplinary surgical approach. Appropriate perioperative planning facilitates safe adrenalectomy, minimizes complications, and results in excellent functional and clinical outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-103\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 103</p><h3>Title: Evaluation of Stone-Free Rates in Renal Stones &gt;2 cm: Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery (RIRS) versus Mini-Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy (Mini-PCNL)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sangram , Dr Rishin , Dr Shikhar Agarwal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS Dehradun</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo compare stone-free rates (SFR), operative time, postoperative recovery, and complications between Mini-Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy (Mini-PCNL) and Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery (RIRS) for the management of renal calculi larger than 2 cm.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nThis prospective observational study was conducted over 12 months and included 40 patients with renal stones &gt;2 cm. Patients were allocated into two groups: Mini-PCNL (n=20) and RIRS (n=20). Baseline demographic and clinical characteristics were comparable between the groups. Outcomes assessed included operative time, postoperative pain, duration of catheterization, length of hospital stay, postoperative complications, and stone-free rates at 1 and 3 months. Residual fragments were managed with auxiliary procedures when indicated. Statistical analysis was performed using GraphPad Prism version 10.6.1, with a p-value &lt;0.05 considered statistically significant.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe mean operative time was significantly shorter in the Mini-PCNL group than in the RIRS group (67.45 vs. 89.75 minutes; p=0.0214). Patients undergoing RIRS experienced significantly lower postoperative pain, shorter catheterization duration (1.4 vs. 2.05 days), and reduced hospital stay (1.4 vs. 2.1 days; p=0.0007). At 1 month, the stone-free rate was 95% in the Mini-PCNL group compared with 85% in the RIRS group; however, this difference was not statistically significant (p=0.605). Following auxiliary procedures for residual fragments, both groups achieved a 100% stone-free rate at 3 months. Complications were infrequent and limited to Clavien-Dindo Grade I events, with no major perioperative morbidity observed.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nBoth Mini-PCNL and RIRS are safe and effective treatment modalities for renal calculi &gt;2 cm. Mini-PCNL offers shorter operative time and superior early stone clearance, whereas RIRS is associated with lower postoperative morbidity, less pain, and faster recovery. Comparable final stone-free rates can be achieved with both procedures following appropriate auxiliary interventions. Careful patient selection, consideration of stone characteristics, and meticulous surgical technique remain the key determinants of successful outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-104\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 104</p><h3>Differential expression of TRIMs modulates immune cell infiltration and determine survival outcome in renal cell carcinoma (RCC)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Rupesh Zarekar , Yashasvi Singh *, Shyam Kishore , Sameer Trivedi , Lalit Agarwal , Ujwal Kumar , Aviral Srivastava , Rajesh Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS BHU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC), the most prevalent subtype of renal cell carcinoma (RCC), exhibits poor prognosis and disproportionately higher mortality. Radical Nephrectomy remains the primary treatment however 30-40% of patients relapse, underscoring the need discovering robust molecular biomarkers. Cross talk between tumor cells and recruited immune cells in inflammatory tumor microenvironment determines the progression of renal cell carcinoma. Tripartite motif (TRIM) proteins regulate key cellular processes including inflammation, proliferation and metabolism, yet their roles in ccRCC progression remain poorly defined.<br/>\nMethods: We conducted a comprehensive transcriptomic analysis of the TRIM gene family across subtypes of renal cell carcinoma using the UALCAN database. Further correlation analysis were performed using TIMER database and qRT-PCR validation of selected TRIMs in ccRCC patients were done.<br/>\nResults: We identified 13 TRIMs in ccRCC and 5 in papillary RCC (pRCC) which are differentially expressed. Analysis of immune cell infiltration showed ccRCC as an immune- active (“hot”) subtype of RCC. TRIMs expression in RCC tumors show correlation with monocyte infiltration and immune marker genes. TRIM36 was significantly upregulated in<br/>\nccRCC and validated by real-time PCR in cohort of ccRCC patients. TRIM36 expression showed positive correlation with TNF-α and IFN-γ mediated inflammatory pathways, higher monocyte infiltration and significantly associated with overall poor survival.<br/>\nConclusion: Our findings identify TRIM36 as a potential diagnostic and prognostic biomarker in ccRCC, linking inflammatory signaling and immune cell infiltration to aggressive tumor behaviour.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-105\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 105</p><h3>Efficacy and Safety of Sequential Intravesical Gemcitabine plus Docetaxel in Non-Muscle Invasive Bladder Cancer: A Single-Arm Observational Study</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Pratik Gond, Dr Rajeev Sarpal, Dr Shikhar Aggarwal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Abstract<br/>\nBackground: Intravesical Bacille Calmette-Guérin (BCG) is the standard treatment for non-<br/>\nmuscle invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC), but its toxicity and limited availability have<br/>\nprompted the use of alternative regimens such as sequential Gemcitabine and Docetaxel.<br/>\nObjective: To evaluate the efficacy and safety of sequential intravesical Gemcitabine plus<br/>\nDocetaxel in NMIBC patients.<br/>\nMethods: This single-arm observational study included patients with NMIBC treated with<br/>\nsequential intravesical Gemcitabine plus Docetaxel following transurethral resection of<br/>\nbladder tumor (TURBT). Clinical and pathological data were analyzed. Primary outcome was<br/>\nassesing safety and recurrence-free survival (RFS), and secondary outcomes included<br/>\nprogression-free survival (PFS) and adverse events.<br/>\nResults: A total of N patients were included. The cohort demonstrated favorable recurrence-<br/>\nfree survival with significantly fewer urinary adverse events such as dysuria and hematuria.<br/>\nThe low incidence of severe adverse effects improves patient compliance and treatment<br/>\nadherence, which is critical in long-term NMIBC management. Adverse events were<br/>\npredominantly mild, with urinary symptoms being the most common. No significant<br/>\ntreatment-limiting toxicity was observed.<br/>\nConclusion: Sequential Gemcitabine plus Docetaxel therapy is an effective and well-<br/>\ntolerated alternative for NMIBC, particularly in settings of BCG intolerance or shortage.<br/>\nKeywords: NMIBC, Gemcitabine, Docetaxel, Intravesical therapy, Observational study</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"podium-106\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Podium Abstract 106</p><h3>Title: Influence of Infundibular Architecture on Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery (RIRS) Success for 1–2 cm Lower Pole Renal Calculi our experience in a tertiary care</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Saurabh Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">HIMS Dehradun</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Retrograde Intrarenal Surgery (RIRS) is an established minimally invasive modality for lower pole renal calculi; however, clinical outcomes are often limited by complex pelvicalyceal anatomy. This study aimed to identify specific infundibular parameters that predict primary stone-free status in patients undergoing RIRS for stones sized 1–2 cm.<br/>\nMethods: A prospective analysis was conducted on patients undergoing RIRS for lower pole calculi (1–2 cm) during the designated study period .Preoperative anatomical parameters—Infundibulopelvic Angle (IPA), Infundibular Length (IL), and Infundibular Width (IW)—were measured via CT Urography. Primary efficacy was defined by Stone-Free Rate (SFR) at Postoperative Day 21 (X-ray KUB/USG). Patients were stratified into \"Stone-Free\" and \"Residual Fragment\" cohorts, with independent t-tests used to compare anatomical variables.<br/>\nResults: Twenty-eight patients underwent RIRS (mean age 50.79 ± 12.85 years). The overall primary SFR was 82.1%. A targeted subgroup analysis revealed a highly significant correlation between infundibular anatomy and procedural failure. Patients who failed to achieve primary stone clearance presented with a significantly more acute mean IPA of 42.34° ± 12.11°, compared to 59.04° ± 10.47° in the successful clearance group (p = 0.004). No statistically significant differences were identified regarding Infundibular Length or Width between the two cohorts.<br/>\nConclusion: An acute Infundibulopelvic Angle (IPA &lt; 45°) is a potent, independent predictor of RIRS failure for 1–2 cm lower pole calculi. While RIRS remains a safe and well-tolerated option for obtuse-angled anatomy, surgeons should exercise caution with steep IPA. In such cases, preoperative planning should prioritize alternative modalities, such as Mini-Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy (Mini-PCNL), to guarantee optimal stone clearance and patient satisfaction.</p></div></details></article>"

